# BOOK	010	2SA	2 Samuel	2 Samuel
010	2SA	001	001	After the death of Saul, David returned from attacking the Amalekites. He stayed in Ziklag for two days.
010	2SA	001	002	Then on the third day a man arrived from Saul's camp. His clothes were torn and he had dust on his head. When he approached David, he bowed before him, and fell to the ground in respect.
010	2SA	001	003	“Where have you come from?” David asked him. “I got away from the Israelite camp,” he replied.
010	2SA	001	004	“Tell me what happened,” David asked. “The army ran away from the battle,” the man replied. “Many of them died, and Saul and his son Jonathan also died.”
010	2SA	001	005	“How do you know Saul and Jonathan died?” David asked the man giving the report.
010	2SA	001	006	“I just happened to be there on Mount Gilboa,” he replied. “I saw Saul, leaning on his spear, with the enemy chariots and the charioteers advancing on him.
010	2SA	001	007	He turned around and saw me. He called out and I replied, ‘I'm here to help!’
010	2SA	001	008	He asked me, ‘Who are you?’ I told him, ‘I'm an Amalekite.’
010	2SA	001	009	Then he told me, ‘Please come over here and kill me! I'm in terrible agony but life is still hanging on.’
010	2SA	001	010	So I went over him and killed him, because I knew that wounded as he was he couldn't last long. I took the crown from his head and his bracelet from his arm, and I've brought them here to you, my lord.”
010	2SA	001	011	David grabbed hold of his clothes and ripped them, as did his men.
010	2SA	001	012	They mourned and cried and fasted until the evening for Saul and his son Jonathan, and for the army of the Lord, the Israelites, that had been killed by the sword.
010	2SA	001	013	David asked the man who brought him the report, “Where are you from?” “I'm the son of a foreigner,” he replied. “I'm an Amalekite.”
010	2SA	001	014	“Why weren't you worried about killing the Lord's anointed one?” David asked.
010	2SA	001	015	David called over one of his men and said, “Go ahead, kill him!” So the man cut the Amalekite down and killed him.
010	2SA	001	016	David had told the Amalekite, “Your death is your own fault because you testified against yourself when you said, ‘I killed the Lord's anointed one.’”
010	2SA	001	017	Then David sang this lament for Saul and his son Jonathan.
010	2SA	001	018	He ordered it to be taught to the people of Judah. It is called “The Bow” and is recorded in the Book of the Just:
010	2SA	001	019	“Israel, the glorious one lies dead on your mountains. How the mighty have fallen!
010	2SA	001	020	Don't announce it in the town of Gath, don't proclaim it in the streets of Ashkelon, so that the Philistine women won't rejoice, so that the heathen women won't celebrate.
010	2SA	001	021	Mountains of Gilboa, may no dew or rain fall on you! May you have no fields that produce offerings of grain. For it was there that the shield of the mighty was defiled; Saul's shield, no longer cared for with olive oil.
010	2SA	001	022	Jonathan with his bow did not retreat from attacking the enemy; Saul with his sword did not return empty-handed from shedding blood.
010	2SA	001	023	During their lives, Saul and Jonathan were much loved and very pleasant, and death did not divide them. They were faster than eagles, stronger than lions.
010	2SA	001	024	Women of Israel, mourn for Saul, who gave you fine scarlet clothes decorated with gold ornaments.
010	2SA	001	025	How the mighty have fallen in battle! Jonathan lies dead on your mountains.
010	2SA	001	026	I weep so much for you, my brother Jonathan! You were so very dear to me! Your love for me was so wonderful, greater than the love women have!
010	2SA	001	027	How the mighty have fallen! The weapons of war are gone!”
010	2SA	002	001	Sometime after this, David asked the Lord, “Should I go to one of the towns of Judah?” “Yes, do it,” the Lord replied. “Which one should I go to?” David asked. “Go to Hebron,” said the Lord.
010	2SA	002	002	So David moved there with his two wives, Ahinoam from Jezreel and Abigail, Nabal's widow from Carmel.
010	2SA	002	003	He also brought the men who were with him, along with their families, and they settled in the villages near Hebron.
010	2SA	002	004	Then the men of Judah came to Hebron, and there they anointed David king of the people of Judah. When David found out that it was the men from Jabesh-gilead who had buried Saul,
010	2SA	002	005	he sent messengers to them, saying, “May the Lord bless you, because you demonstrated your loyal love to Saul your master, and you buried him properly.
010	2SA	002	006	Now may the Lord show you loyal love and trustworthiness, and I will also be good to you because of what you did for Saul.
010	2SA	002	007	So be strong and be brave, for even though Saul your master is dead, the people of Judah have anointed me as their king.”
010	2SA	002	008	However, Abner, son of Ner, commander of Saul's army, had taken Ishbosheth, son of Saul, to Mahanaim.
010	2SA	002	009	There he set up Ishbosheth as king over Gilead, Asher, Jezreel, Ephraim, and Benjamin, in fact over all Israel.
010	2SA	002	010	Ishbosheth, son of Saul, was forty when he became king over Israel, and he reigned for two years. However, the people of Judah were on David's side.
010	2SA	002	011	David ruled in Hebron as king over the people of Judah for seven years and six months.
010	2SA	002	012	One day Abner and Ishbosheth's men left Mahanaim and went to the town of Gibeon.
010	2SA	002	013	Joab, son of Zeruiah, and David's men set off and met them at the pool of Gibeon, where they all sat down, facing each other across the pool.
010	2SA	002	014	Abner said to Joab, “Let's have some of the men fight in hand-to-hand combat before us.” “Fine,” Joab agreed.
010	2SA	002	015	So twelve men came forward from each side—twelve for Benjamin and Ishbosheth, and twelve for David.
010	2SA	002	016	Each man grabbed his opponent's head and drove his sword into his opponent's side so that they all fell down dead together. That's why this place in Gibeon is called the Field of Sword-edges.
010	2SA	002	017	The battle that followed was hard-fought, but eventually Abner and his men were defeated by David's men.
010	2SA	002	018	The three sons of Zeruiah were there: Joab, Abishai, and Asahel. Asahel was a fast runner, like a gazelle racing across the open countryside.
010	2SA	002	019	He chased after Abner with single-minded determination.
010	2SA	002	020	Abner looked back and asked, “Is that you, Asahel?” “Yes, it's me,” Asahel replied.
010	2SA	002	021	Abner told him, “Leave me alone! Go and fight somebody else and take his weapons for yourself!” But Asahel refused to stop chasing him.
010	2SA	002	022	Abner warned Asahel again. “Stop chasing me!” he shouted. “Why do you want me to kill you? How could I ever face your brother Joab?”
010	2SA	002	023	But Asahel wouldn't stop chasing him, so Abner drove the handle of his spear into his belly. It came out the back, and he fell down dead right there. Everyone who passed by stopped at the place where Asahel had fallen and died.
010	2SA	002	024	But Joab and Abishai set off to chase after Abner. By the time the sun went down they had got as far as the hill of Ammah near Giah, on the way to the wilderness of Gibeon.
010	2SA	002	025	Abner's men from the tribe of Benjamin rallied to him there, forming a tight group around him standing at the top of the hill.
010	2SA	002	026	Abner shouted to Joab: “Do we have to keep killing each other forever? Don't you realize that if we go on it'll only get worse? How long are you going to wait before you order your men to stop chasing their brothers?”
010	2SA	002	027	“As God lives,” Joab replied, “if you had not said anything, my men would have continued chasing their brothers until the morning.”
010	2SA	002	028	Joab blew the horn so all the men stopped—they didn't continue chasing or fighting the Israelites.
010	2SA	002	029	All through the night Abner and his men marched through the Jordan Valley. They crossed the Jordan River, and continued all morning until they arrived back at Mahanaim.
010	2SA	002	030	When Joab got back from chasing Abner, he gathered all the men together. Nineteen of David's men were missing in addition to Asahel.
010	2SA	002	031	However, they had killed three hundred and sixty of Abner's men from the tribe of Benjamin.
010	2SA	002	032	They took Asahel's body and buried him in his father's tomb in Bethlehem. Then they marched all through the night and reached Hebron at dawn.
010	2SA	003	001	There was a long war between those on the side of Saul and those on the side of David. David's side grew stronger, while Saul's side grew weaker.
010	2SA	003	002	David's sons born at Hebron were: Amnon (firstborn), by Ahinoam from Jezreel;
010	2SA	003	003	Chileab (second), by Abigail, Nabal's widow from Carmel; Absalom (third), by Maacah, daughter of King Talmai of Geshur;
010	2SA	003	004	Adonijah (fourth), by Haggith; Shephatiah (fifth), by Abital;
010	2SA	003	005	Ithream (sixth), by David's wife Eglah. These were the sons born to David at Hebron.
010	2SA	003	006	Abner had been strengthening his position among the supporters of Saul's dynasty during the war between those on the side of Saul and those on the side of David.
010	2SA	003	007	Saul had a concubine named Rizpah, daughter of Aiah. One day Ishbosheth accused Abner, saying “Why have you been sleeping with my father's concubine?”
010	2SA	003	008	Abner got extremely angry at Ishbosheth's accusation. “Am I a dog's head siding with Judah?” he replied. “Right up to the present day I have been loyal to your dynasty—to your father Saul, and to his brothers and friends. I haven't betrayed you to David. But now you dare to accuse me of sinning with this woman!
010	2SA	003	009	So now may God punish me severely if I don't help David achieve what the Lord has promised him.
010	2SA	003	010	I will hand over the kingdom from Saul's dynasty, and help set up David's rule over Israel and Judah, from Dan to Beersheba.”
010	2SA	003	011	Ishbosheth didn't dare to say anything else to Abner because he was frightened of him.
010	2SA	003	012	Then Abner sent messengers to speak for him to David, saying, “Who does the country belong to anyway? Make an agreement with me, and you can be sure I'll be on your side to have all of Israel follow you.”
010	2SA	003	013	“Fine,” David replied, “I'll make an agreement with you. But I have one condition: I won't see you unless you bring Saul's daughter Michal when you come.”
010	2SA	003	014	Then David sent messengers to tell Ishbosheth, son of Saul, “Give me back my wife Michal—I paid a dowry for her of one hundred Philistine foreskins.”
010	2SA	003	015	Ishbosheth sent for her and took her away from her husband Paltiel, son of Laish.
010	2SA	003	016	Her husband followed her to the town of Bahurim, crying as he went. Then Abner ordered him, “Go back home!” So he went home.
010	2SA	003	017	Abner spoke with the elders of Israel and said, “For a while now you have wanted to have David as your king.
010	2SA	003	018	Now is the time to do it, because the Lord promised David, ‘Through my servant David I am going to save my people Israel from the Philistines and all their enemies.’”
010	2SA	003	019	Abner also talked to the people of Benjamin, and went to Hebron to let David know everything that the Israelites and the whole tribe of Benjamin had decided to do.
010	2SA	003	020	Abner came with twenty of his men to see David at Hebron, and David prepared a feast for them.
010	2SA	003	021	Abner told David, “Let me go immediately and summon all of Israel to come together for my lord the king, so they may make an agreement with you, and that you may rule over all you would wish.” Then David sent Abner safely on his way.
010	2SA	003	022	Soon after Joab and David's men came back from a raid, bringing with them a large amount of plunder. However, Abner wasn't there with David in Hebron because David had already sent him safely on his way in peace.
010	2SA	003	023	When Joab and all the army that was with him arrived, he was told, “Abner, son of Ner, came to see the king, who sent him safely on his way.”
010	2SA	003	024	Joab went to the king and asked, “What do you think you're doing? Here's Abner, who came to see you. Why on earth did you send him on his way? Now he's got clean away!
010	2SA	003	025	You do see that Abner, son of Ner, came here to trick you, to spy on the movements of your army, and to find out everything you're doing!”
010	2SA	003	026	When Joab left David, he sent messengers after Abner. They met up with him at the well of Sirah and brought him back, but David didn't know anything about it.
010	2SA	003	027	When Abner arrived back in Hebron, Joab took him aside into the town gatehouse as if he was going to talk with him in private. But Joab stabbed him in the belly, killing him in revenge for killing Joab's brother Asahel.
010	2SA	003	028	When David heard about this later he said, “I and my kingdom are totally innocent before the Lord regarding the death of Abner, son of Ner!
010	2SA	003	029	May the guilt for his death fall on Joab and his family! May Joab's descendants always have someone who has running sores or leprosy or is crippled or who is killed by the sword or who is starving.”
010	2SA	003	030	(This is why Joab and Abishai his brother killed Abner, because he had killed their brother Asahel during the battle at Gibeon.)
010	2SA	003	031	Then David ordered Joab and everyone who was there, “Tear your clothes, put on sackcloth, and mourn for Abner.” King David himself followed the body as it was carried to the grave.
010	2SA	003	032	They buried Abner in Hebron, and the king cried loudly at the grave, along with all the people.
