011	1KI	003	012	I am giving you what you asked for. I am giving you a mind that is wise, with an understanding of what is right, more than anyone else before you or after you.
011	1KI	003	013	I am also giving you what you did not ask for, wealth and status—so much so that no king will compare to you for the whole of your life.
011	1KI	003	014	And if you follow my ways by keeping my laws and my commands, as your father David did, I will give you a long life.”
011	1KI	003	015	Then Solomon woke up and realized he'd had a dream. He went back to Jerusalem, and stood in front of the Ark of the Lord's Agreement and he presented burnt offerings and friendship offerings, and he held a feast for all his officials.
011	1KI	003	016	Later two prostitutes came to the king and stood before him for judgment.
011	1KI	003	017	One of the women spoke up, saying, “If it please Your Majesty: I share a house with this woman. I had a baby while she was living in the house.
011	1KI	003	018	Three days after the birth of my baby, this woman also had a baby. We were together; there was no one else in the house, just the two of us.
011	1KI	003	019	During the night this woman's son died because she rolled over on him.
011	1KI	003	020	She got up in the middle of the night and took my son from beside me while I was sleeping. She lay him close beside her to cuddle him, and she lay her dead son close beside me.
011	1KI	003	021	When I got up in the morning to nurse my son I saw that he was dead. When I looked closely at him in the light I realized it wasn't my son.”
011	1KI	003	022	The other woman argued, “No! My son is the one that's alive. Your son is the one that's dead.” The first woman objected, “No! Your son is the one that's dead. My son is the one that's alive.” They went on quarrelling in front of the king.
011	1KI	003	023	The king intervened, saying, “So this woman says, ‘My son is the one that's alive. Your son is the one that's dead,’ while the other woman says, ‘No! Your son is the one that's dead. My son is the one that's alive.’”
011	1KI	003	024	“Bring me a sword,” the king ordered. So they brought him a sword.
011	1KI	003	025	“Cut the child that's alive in two, and give half to one woman and half to another,” he commanded.
011	1KI	003	026	But the woman whose son was alive had so much love for him as a mother that she cried out to the king, “Please, Your Majesty, give her the boy! Don't kill him!” But the other woman said, “He won't be mine or yours—cut him in two!”
011	1KI	003	027	The king gave his verdict. “Give the child that's alive to the first woman,” he ordered. “On no account kill him, for she is his real mother.”
011	1KI	003	028	When everyone in Israel heard about the verdict the king had delivered, they had great respect for the king, because they recognized the wisdom God had given him to judge rightly.
011	1KI	004	001	King Solomon ruled over all of Israel.
011	1KI	004	002	These were his officials: Azariah, son of Zadok, was the priest;
011	1KI	004	003	Elihoreph and Ahijah, sons of Shisha, were the king's secretaries. Jehoshaphat, son of Ahilud, kept the royal records.
011	1KI	004	004	Benaiah son of Jehoiada was the army commander. Zadok and Abiathar were priests.
011	1KI	004	005	Azariah, son of Nathan, was in charge of the governors. Zabud, son of Nathan, was a priest and the king's counselor.
011	1KI	004	006	Ahishar was the palace manager. Adoniram, son of Abda, was in charge of those forced to work for the king.
011	1KI	004	007	Solomon had twelve area governors whose responsibilities covered the whole of Israel, providing food for the king and his household. Each one in turn arranged supplies for one month of the year.
011	1KI	004	008	Their names were: Ben-hur, in the hill country of Ephraim;
011	1KI	004	009	Ben-deker in Makaz, Shaalbim, Beth-shemesh and Elon-beth-hanan;
011	1KI	004	010	Ben-hesed in Arubboth (Socoh and all the land of Hepher was his);
011	1KI	004	011	Ben-abinadab, in the whole of Naphath-dor (Taphath, the daughter of Solomon, was his wife);
011	1KI	004	012	Baana, son of Ahilud, in Taanach and Megiddo, the whole of Beth-shan near Zarethan below Jezreel, and from Beth-shan to Abel-meholah and across to Jokmeam;
011	1KI	004	013	Ben-geber, in Ramoth-gilead (the towns of Jair, son of Manasseh, in Gilead belonged to him, as well as the region of Argob in Bashan, with sixty great cities having walls and bronze bars);
011	1KI	004	014	Ahinadab, son of Iddo, in Mahanaim;
011	1KI	004	015	Ahimaaz, in Naphtali (he had married Basemath the daughter of Solomon);
011	1KI	004	016	Baana, son of Hushai, in Asher and in Aloth;
011	1KI	004	017	Jehoshaphat, son of Paruah, in Issachar;
011	1KI	004	018	Shimei, son of Ela, in Benjamin;
011	1KI	004	019	Geber son of Uri in the land of Gilead (the former country of Sihon, king of the Amorites, and of Og, king of Bashan. There was also a governor who was over the land of Judah).
011	1KI	004	020	Judah and Israel had become as numerous as the sand on the seashore. They ate, they drank, they were happy.
011	1KI	004	021	Solomon ruled over all the kingdoms from the Euphrates River to the land of the Philistines, right up to the border of Egypt. They presented tribute to Solomon and served him during his lifetime.
011	1KI	004	022	The food required every day for Solomon's court was thirty cors of the best flour and sixty cors of meal;
011	1KI	004	023	ten fattened cattle, twenty range cattle, one hundred sheep, as well as deer, gazelles, roe deer, and fattened poultry.
011	1KI	004	024	For Solomon ruled over the whole region west of the Euphrates, from Tiphsah to Gaza—over all the kingdoms west of the Euphrates. And he had peace on all sides around him. He had peace on every border.
011	1KI	004	025	During Solomon's lifetime, everyone in Judah and Israel lived in safety, from Dan to Beersheba. Each one had their own vine and fig tree.
011	1KI	004	026	Solomon had 40,000 stalls for his chariot horses, and 12,000 charioteers.
011	1KI	004	027	Every month the area governors provided in turn food for King Solomon and all who ate at his table. They made sure that nothing was missing.
011	1KI	004	028	They also delivered barley and straw where they were needed for the chariot horses and cart horses.
011	1KI	004	029	God gave to Solomon wisdom, very great discernment, and understanding as extensive as the sand on the seashore.
011	1KI	004	030	Solomon's wisdom was greater than that of all the Eastern wise men, greater than all of Egypt's wisdom.
011	1KI	004	031	He was wiser than anyone, wiser than Ethan the Ezrahite, wiser than Heman, Calcol, and Darda, sons of Mahol. His reputation spread through the nations around.
011	1KI	004	032	Solomon composed three thousand proverbs and one thousand and five songs.
011	1KI	004	033	He was able to discuss knowledge of trees, from the cedar in Lebanon to the hyssop that grows in the wall. He taught about animals, birds, reptiles, and fish.
011	1KI	004	034	People from all nations came to hear Solomon's wisdom. They were sent by all the kings of the earth, who had heard about his wisdom.
011	1KI	005	001	When Hiram king of Tyre heard that Solomon had been anointed king to succeed his father, he sent ambassadors to Solomon because Hiram had always been David's friend.
011	1KI	005	002	So Solomon sent this message back to Hiram,
011	1KI	005	003	“As you know, my father David was not able to build a Temple to honor the Lord his God because of the wars fought against him from every direction, until the Lord had conquered his enemies.
011	1KI	005	004	But now the Lord my God has given me peace all around—no enemies, no bad things happening.
011	1KI	005	005	So I plan to build a Temple to honor the Lord my God, as the Lord told my father David. He said to him, ‘Your son whom I will place on your throne to succeed you will build the Temple to honor me.’
011	1KI	005	006	So please order some cedars of Lebanon to be cut down for me. My workers will assist your workers, and I will pay your workers at the rate that you decide, for you know that we don't have anyone who knows how to cut timber like the Sidonians.”
011	1KI	005	007	When Hiram heard Solomon's message, he was very happy and said, “Praise the Lord today, for he has given David a wise son to lead this great nation!”
011	1KI	005	008	Hiram sent this reply to Solomon: “Thank you for your message. As for the cedar and cypress timber, I will do everything you want.
011	1KI	005	009	My workers will bring the logs down from Lebanon to the sea, and I will have them floated in rafts by sea to wherever you decide. I will have the rafts broken apart there, and you can take the logs away. In return I would like you to provide food for my household.”
011	1KI	005	010	So Hiram provided Solomon with as much cedar and cypress timber as he wanted,
011	1KI	005	011	Solomon gave Hiram 20,000 cors of wheat for food and 20,000 cors of olive oil for his household. Solomon provided this to Hiram every year.
011	1KI	005	012	The Lord gave Solomon wisdom just as he had promised him. Hiram and Solomon had a good relationship and they made a peace treaty with each other.
011	1KI	005	013	King Solomon drafted a labor force of 30,000 from all of Israel.
011	1KI	005	014	He sent them in shifts of 10,000 each month to Lebanon, so that they were one month in Lebanon and two months at home, Adoniram was in charge of the labor force.
011	1KI	005	015	Solomon had 70,000 porters and 80,000 stonecutters in the hill country,
011	1KI	005	016	as well as 3,300 foremen he placed in charge of the workers.
011	1KI	005	017	Following the king's orders, they quarried large blocks of stone that were expensive to produce, and laid these dressed stones as the foundation for the Temple.
011	1KI	005	018	So Solomon's and Hiram's builders, together with the Gebalites, cut the stone. They prepared the timber and stone to build the Temple.
011	1KI	006	001	Four hundred and eighty years after the Israelites left Egypt, in the fourth year of King Solomon's reign, in the month of Ziv, Solomon began building the Temple of the Lord.
011	1KI	006	002	The Temple King Solomon built for the Lord measured sixty cubits long by twenty cubits wide by thirty cubits high.
011	1KI	006	003	The entrance room at the front of the Temple was twenty cubits wide. It ran the whole width of the Temple and projected out ten cubits in front of the Temple.
011	1KI	006	004	He had latticed windows made to be placed high up in the Temple.
011	1KI	006	005	He also had a structure built against the outer wall of the Temple, both the outer and the inner sanctuary, to provide a series of side rooms.
011	1KI	006	006	The ground floor measured five cubits wide, the first floor six cubits, and the second floor seven cubits. In addition he had offset ledges placed all the way around the outside, so that beams would not have to be inserted into the Temple walls.
011	1KI	006	007	When the Temple was built the stones were finished at the quarry so that no noise from any hammer, ax, or any iron tool was heard in the Temple during construction.
011	1KI	006	008	The entrance to the ground floor was on the south side of the Temple. Stairs led to the first floor, and then on to the second floor.
011	1KI	006	009	So Solomon finished building the Temple, covering it with a roof made of cedar rafters and planks.
011	1KI	006	010	He built the external structure along the whole of the Temple. It was five cubits high, joined to the Temple with cedar joists.
011	1KI	006	011	The Lord sent this message to Solomon, telling him,
011	1KI	006	012	“About this Temple you are building: if you follow what I have said, fulfilling my requirements and keeping my commandments in what you do, I will keep the promise I made to your father David through you.
011	1KI	006	013	I will live among the Israelites and will not abandon Israel, my people.”
011	1KI	006	014	Solomon finished building the Temple.
011	1KI	006	015	He lined the walls with cedar panels from floor to ceiling and he covered the floor of the Temple with cypress boards.
011	1KI	006	016	He sectioned off twenty cubits at the rear of the Temple with boards of cedar from floor to ceiling, making an inner sanctuary as the Most Holy Place.
011	1KI	006	017	The main Temple in front of this room measured forty cubits long.
011	1KI	006	018	The cedar panels inside the Temple were decorated with carvings of gourds and open flowers. Everything was lined with cedar—none of the stone was visible.
011	1KI	006	019	He also had the inner sanctuary made within the Temple where the Ark of the Lord's Agreement would be placed.
011	1KI	006	020	The inner sanctuary measured twenty cubits long, twenty cubits wide, and twenty cubits high. He had the interior covered with a coating with pure gold, as well as the altar of cedar.
011	1KI	006	021	Solomon had the whole of the inside of the Temple covered with pure gold, He had gold chains stretched across the front of the inner sanctuary, which was also covered with gold.
011	1KI	006	022	He covered the whole inside of the Temple with a coating of gold until it was all finished. He also covered with gold the entire altar for the inner sanctuary.
011	1KI	006	023	He had two cherubim made out of olive wood for the inner sanctuary, each one ten cubits tall.
011	1KI	006	024	Both wings of the cherub measured five cubits, making a total wingspan of ten cubits.
011	1KI	006	025	The other cherub was also ten cubits tall, since they were identical both in size and shape.
011	1KI	006	026	Both cherubs were ten cubits tall.
011	1KI	006	027	He had the cherubim placed inside the inner sanctuary of the Temple. Since their wings were fully spread, a wing of the first cherub touched one wall, a wing of the second cherub touched the other wall, and in the middle of the Temple their wings touched.
011	1KI	006	028	The cherubim were also covered with a gold coating.
011	1KI	006	029	He had all the Temple walls, both the inner and outer sanctuaries, carved with designs of cherubim, palm trees, and open flowers.
011	1KI	006	030	He also had the floor of the Temple covered with gold, both the inner and outer sanctuaries.
011	1KI	006	031	He had doors of olive wood made for the entrance to the inner sanctuary, together with a lintel and doorposts that had five sides.
011	1KI	006	032	These olive wood double doors had carved into them designs of cherubim, palm trees, and open flowers. The cherubim and palm trees carvings were covered with beaten gold.
011	1KI	006	033	Similarly he had four-sided doorposts of olive wood made for the entrance to the sanctuary.
011	1KI	006	034	Its doors were made of cypress wood, each with two hinged panels.
011	1KI	006	035	He had them carved with designs of cherubim, palm trees, and open flowers, and covered them with gold beaten evenly over the carvings.
011	1KI	006	036	Solomon had the inner courtyard built with three courses of dressed stone and one of cedar beams.
011	1KI	006	037	The foundation of the Lord's Temple was laid in the fourth year of Solomon's reign, in the month of Ziv.
011	1KI	006	038	The Temple was finished exactly as specified and planned in Solomon's eleventh year, in the eighth month, the month of Bul. So it took him seven years to build the Temple.
011	1KI	007	001	However, it took Solomon thirteen years to finish building the whole of his palace.
011	1KI	007	002	He built the House of the Forest of Lebanon— a hundred cubits long, fifty cubits wide, and thirty cubits high. There were four rows of cedar pillars that supported cedar beams.
011	1KI	007	003	The cedar roof of the house was on top of the beams that rested on the pillars. There were forty-five beams, fifteen in each row.
011	1KI	007	004	The windows were placed high up, in three rows facing each other.
011	1KI	007	005	All the doorways and door casings had rectangular frames, the openings facing each other in sets of three.
011	1KI	007	006	He also had the Hall of Columns made—forty cubits long and thirty cubits wide. It had a porch in front, its canopy also supported by columns.
011	1KI	007	007	The throne room where he sat as judge was called the Hall of Justice, lined with cedar panels from floor to ceiling.
011	1KI	007	008	Solomon's own palace where he lived was in a courtyard behind the porch, made in a similar way to the Temple. He also had a palace made for Pharaoh's daughter, whom he'd married.
011	1KI	007	009	All these buildings were built using stone blocks that were expensive to produce. They were cut to size and trimmed with saws on the inside and outside. These stones were used from the foundation to the eaves, from the outside of the building all the way to the great courtyard.
011	1KI	007	010	The foundations were laid with very large top-quality stones, between eight and ten cubits long.
011	1KI	007	011	On these were placed top-quality stones, cut to size, along with cedar timber.
011	1KI	007	012	Around the great courtyard, the inner courtyard, and the porch of the Lord's Temple were three courses of dressed stone and a course of cedar beams.
011	1KI	007	013	King Solomon sent for Hiram from Tyre.
011	1KI	007	014	He was the son of a widow from the tribe of Naphtali, and his father was from Tyre, a craftsman who worked in bronze. Hiram had great expertise, understanding and being familiar with all kinds of bronze work. He came to King Solomon and carried out all that the king required.
011	1KI	007	015	He cast two columns in bronze. They were both eighteen cubits high and twelve cubits in circumference.
011	1KI	007	016	He also cast two capitals in bronze to place on top of the columns. Each capital was five cubits high.
011	1KI	007	017	He made a network of lattice using interlinked chains for both capitals, seven for each one.
011	1KI	007	018	Around the lattice network he made two rows of ornamental pomegranates to cover the capitals on the top of both the columns.
011	1KI	007	019	The capitals placed on top of columns in the porch were in the shape of lilies, four cubits high.
011	1KI	007	020	On the capitals of both columns were the two hundred pomegranates in rows that encircled them, just above the rounded part that was next to the chain network.
011	1KI	007	021	He erected the columns at the entrance porch of the Temple. The southern column he named Jachin, and the northern column he named Boaz.
011	1KI	007	022	The capitals on the columns were in the shape of lilies. And so the work on the columns was finished.
011	1KI	007	023	Then he made the Sea of cast metal. Its shape was circular, and measured ten cubits from edge to edge, five cubits in height, and thirty cubits in circumference.
011	1KI	007	024	Below the edge it was decorated with ornamental gourds that encircled it, ten per cubit all the way around. They were in two rows cast as one piece with the Sea.
011	1KI	007	025	The Sea stood on twelve metal bulls. Three faced to the north, three to the west, three to the south, and three to the east. The Sea was placed on them, with their rears toward the center.
011	1KI	007	026	It was as thick as the width of a hand, and its edge was like the flared edge of a cup or a lily flower. It held two thousand baths.
011	1KI	007	027	He also made ten carts to carry basins. The carts measured four cubits long, four cubits wide, and three cubits high.
011	1KI	007	028	This is how they were put together: side panels were attached to uprights.
011	1KI	007	029	Both the side panels and the uprights were decorated with lions, bulls, and cherubim. Above and below the lions and the bulls were decorative wreaths.
011	1KI	007	030	Each cart had four bronze wheels with bronze axles. A basin rested on four supports that had decorative wreaths on each side.
011	1KI	007	031	At the top of each cart was a round opening like a pedestal to hold the basin. The opening was one cubit deep, and one and a half cubits wide. The opening had carvings around it. The panels of the cart were square, not round.
011	1KI	007	032	The four wheels were under the panels, and the axles of the wheels were attached to the cart. Each wheel measured one and a half cubits in diameter.
011	1KI	007	033	The wheels were made in the same way as chariot wheels; their axles, rims, spokes, and hubs were all made by casting.
011	1KI	007	034	Each cart had four handles, one on each corner, made as part of the stand.
011	1KI	007	035	There was a ring on the top of the cart a half cubit wide. The supports and panels were cast as one piece with the top of the cart.
011	1KI	007	036	He had designs of cherubim, lions, and palm trees engraved on the panels, supports, and frame, wherever there was space, with decorative wreaths all around.
011	1KI	007	037	This is how he made the ten carts, with the same casts, size, and shape.
011	1KI	007	038	Then he made ten bronze basins. Each one held forty baths and measured four cubits across, one basin for each of the ten carts.
011	1KI	007	039	He placed five carts on the south side of the Temple and five on the north side. He placed the Sea on the south side, by the southeast corner of the Temple.
011	1KI	007	040	He also made the pots, shovels, and bowls. So Hiram finished making everything required by King Solomon for the Temple of the Lord:
011	1KI	007	041	the two columns; the two capitals shaped like bowls on top the columns; the two chain networks that covered the capitals shaped like bowls on top of the columns;
011	1KI	007	042	the four hundred ornamental pomegranates for the chain networks (in two rows for the chain networks that covered the capitals on top of the columns);
011	1KI	007	043	the ten carts; the ten basins on the carts;
011	1KI	007	044	the Sea; the twelve bulls under the Sea;
011	1KI	007	045	and the pots, shovels, and bowls. Everything that Hiram made for King Solomon in the Temple of the Lord was made of polished bronze.
011	1KI	007	046	The king had them cast in molds made of clay in the Jordan valley between Succoth and Zarethan.
011	1KI	007	047	Solomon did not weigh anything that had been made because there was just so much—the weight of bronze used could not be measured.
011	1KI	007	048	Solomon also had made all the items for the Temple of the Lord: the golden altar; the golden table where the Bread of the Presence was placed;
011	1KI	007	049	the lampstands made of pure gold that stood in front of the inner sanctuary, five on the right and five on the left; the flowers, lamps, and tongs that were all made of pure gold;
011	1KI	007	050	the basins, wick trimmers, bowls, ladles, and censers that again were all made of pure gold; and the gold hinges for the doors of the inner sanctuary, the Most Holy Place, in addition to the doors of the main hall of the Temple.
011	1KI	007	051	In this way all King Solomon's work for the Temple of the Lord was completed. Then Solomon brought in the items his father David had dedicated, the special objects made of silver, the gold, and the Temple furnishings, and he placed them in the treasuries of the Temple of the Lord.
011	1KI	008	001	Then Solomon summoned before him in Jerusalem the elders of Israel, including all the heads of the tribes and the family leaders of the Israelites. He instructed them to bring up with them the Ark of the Lord's Agreement from Zion, the City of David.
011	1KI	008	002	All the men of Israel assembled before King Solomon at the festival which is held in the seventh month, the month of Ethanim.
011	1KI	008	003	When all the elders of Israel had gathered, the priests picked up the Ark and brought the Ark of the Lord and the Tent of Meeting with all its holy items.
011	1KI	008	004	The priests and Levites carried them up.
011	1KI	008	005	In front of the Ark, King Solomon and all the congregation of Israel who had gathered there with him sacrificed many, many sheep and bulls—so numerous they couldn't be counted!
011	1KI	008	006	Then the priests carried the Ark of the Lord's Agreement to its place in the inner sanctuary of the Temple, the Most Holy Place, underneath the wings of the cherubim.
011	1KI	008	007	The cherubim spread their wings over the place where the Ark was, covering the Ark and its carrying poles.
011	1KI	008	008	The poles were so long that the ends could be seen from the Holy Place in front of the inner sanctuary, but not from outside. They are there to this day.
011	1KI	008	009	There was nothing in the Ark apart from the two tablets of stone that Moses had placed in it at Horeb, where the Lord had made an agreement with the Israelites after they came out of the land of Egypt.
011	1KI	008	010	When the priests left the Holy Place, the cloud filled the Temple of the Lord.
011	1KI	008	011	Due to the cloud, the priests could not stay there to carry out their service, for the glory of the Lord had filled the Lord's Temple.
011	1KI	008	012	Then Solomon said: “Lord, you said that you would live in the thick cloud.
011	1KI	008	013	Now I have built for you a majestic Temple, a place where you may live for ever.”
011	1KI	008	014	The king turned around to the whole assembly of Israel who were standing there, and blessed them,
011	1KI	008	015	saying, “Praise the Lord, the God of Israel, who by his own power has fulfilled the promise he made to my father David when he said,
011	1KI	008	016	‘Since the day I brought my people Israel out of Egypt I have not chosen any town of the tribes of Israel as the place to build a Temple to honor me. But I have chosen David as king over my people Israel.’
011	1KI	008	017	My father David really wanted to build a Temple to honor the Lord, the God of Israel.
011	1KI	008	018	But the Lord told my father David, ‘You had the desire to build a Temple to honor me, and it was good that you really wanted to do this.
011	1KI	008	019	But it won't be you who will build this Temple, but your son who is to be born to you—he will build the Temple to honor me.'
011	1KI	008	020	Now the Lord has kept his promise that he made. I have succeeded my father David, and I sit on the throne of Israel, just as the Lord promised. I have built the Temple to honor the Lord, the God of Israel.
011	1KI	008	021	I have provided a place there for the Ark, which contains the agreement of the Lord that he made with our forefathers when he led them out of the land of Egypt.”
011	1KI	008	022	Then Solomon stood in front of the altar of the Lord before the whole assembly of Israel and held out his hands toward heaven.
011	1KI	008	023	He said, “Lord, God of Israel, there is no God like you in heaven above or on earth below. You keep your agreement of trustworthy love with your servants, those who are totally committed to following you.
011	1KI	008	024	You have kept the promise you made to your servant David, my father. You yourself promised, and by your own power you have fulfilled it today.
011	1KI	008	025	So now, Lord, God of Israel, please also keep the promise you made to your servant, my father David, when you said: ‘You will never fail to have a descendant sit in my presence on the throne of Israel, as long as they make sure to follow me as you have done.’
011	1KI	008	026	Now, God of Israel, please keep the promise you made to our servant, my father David.
011	1KI	008	027	But will God really live here on earth? The heavens, even highest heaven, cannot contain you, much less this Temple I have built!
011	1KI	008	028	Please hear the prayer of your servant and his request, Lord my God. Please hear the appeals and the prayers that your servant is presenting before you today.
011	1KI	008	029	May you watch over this Temple day and night, caring for the place where you said you would be honored. May you hear the prayer your servant prays toward this place,
011	1KI	008	030	and may you hear the request of your servant and your people Israel when they pray toward this place. Please hear from heaven where you live. May you hear and forgive.
011	1KI	008	031	When someone sins against another and is required to take an oath before your altar in this Temple,
011	1KI	008	032	listen from heaven—act and judge your servants. Pay back the guilty; vindicate and reward those who do right.
011	1KI	008	033	When your people Israel are defeated by an enemy because they have sinned against you, and if they come back in repentance to you, praying for forgiveness in this Temple,
011	1KI	008	034	then hear from heaven and forgive the sin of your people Israel, and bring them back to the land you gave to them and their forefathers.
011	1KI	008	035	If the skies are closed shut and there is no rain because your people have sinned against you, if they pray toward this place and if they come back in repentance to you, turning away from their sin because you have punished them,
011	1KI	008	036	then hear from heaven and forgive the sin of your servants, your people Israel. Teach them the good way so that they can walk in it, and send rain on the earth that you have given to your people as their possession.
011	1KI	008	037	If there is famine in the land, or disease, or blight or mildew on the crops, or if there are locusts or caterpillars, or if an enemy comes to lay siege to the towns in the land—it can be whatever kind of plague or whatever kind of disease—
011	1KI	008	038	then whatever kind of prayer or whatever kind of appeal is made by anyone or all your people Israel, (in fact anyone who, knowing their problems and pains, prays toward this Temple),
