011	1KI	020	037	The prophet found another man and said, “Please hit me!” So the man hit him, wounding him.
011	1KI	020	038	Then the prophet went and stood beside the road, waiting for the king. He had disguised himself with a bandage over his eyes.
011	1KI	020	039	As the king was passing by, he shouted out to the king: “Your servant had gone out fighting right in the middle of the battle, when all of a sudden a man came over with a prisoner and told me, ‘Guard this man! If for any reason he escapes, you will pay for his life with your life, or you will be fined a talent of silver.’
011	1KI	020	040	But while your servant was busy with other things, the man got away.” “So that will be your punishment then,” the king of Israel told him. “You have sentenced yourself.”
011	1KI	020	041	Then the prophet quickly took off the bandage from his eyes, and the king of Israel recognized he was one of the prophets.
011	1KI	020	042	He told the king, “This is what the Lord says: You have let go a man I had decided should die. Therefore you will pay for his life with your life, your people for his people.”
011	1KI	020	043	The king of Israel went home to Samaria, sulking and infuriated.
011	1KI	021	001	Sometime later this happened: There was a man named Naboth from Jezreel, who owned a vineyard in Jezreel close to King Ahab's palace in Samaria.
011	1KI	021	002	Ahab went to Naboth and said, “Give me your vineyard so I can turn it into a vegetable garden, because it's close to my palace. In return I'll give you a better vineyard, or if you want I'll pay you for it in cash.”
011	1KI	021	003	But Naboth replied, “The Lord curse me if I should give you my forefathers' inheritance.”
011	1KI	021	004	Ahab went home sulking and infuriated because Naboth from Jezreel had said, “I will not give you my forefathers' inheritance.” He went to bed, wouldn't look at anyone, and refused to eat.
011	1KI	021	005	His wife Jezebel came in and asked him, “What are you so upset about that you don't want to eat?”
011	1KI	021	006	Ahab replied, “It's because I talked with Naboth from Jezreel and I asked him, ‘Give me your vineyard for cash, or if you want, I'll give you another vineyard instead.’ But he said, ‘I will not give you my vineyard.’”
011	1KI	021	007	“Aren't you the king of Israel?” his wife Jezebel replied. “Get up, have something to eat, and cheer up. I'll get you the vineyard of Naboth from Jezreel.”
011	1KI	021	008	So she wrote some letters in Ahab's name and sealed them with his seal. She sent the letters to the elders and leaders in the town where Naboth lived.
011	1KI	021	009	In the letters she wrote to tell them, “Announce a religious fast, and give Naboth a seat of honor.
011	1KI	021	010	But seat two bad men opposite him and have them accuse him, saying, ‘You have cursed God and the king!’ Then take him out and stone him to death.”
011	1KI	021	011	So the elders and leaders who lived in Naboth's town did as Jezebel had said in the letters she had written and sent to them.
011	1KI	021	012	They announced a religious fast, and gave Naboth a seat of honor.
011	1KI	021	013	Two bad men came and sat opposite him, and accused him in front of the people, saying, “Naboth has cursed God and the king.” So they took him outside the town and stoned him to death.
011	1KI	021	014	Then they sent a message to Jezebel, saying, “Naboth has been stoned. He's dead.”
011	1KI	021	015	As soon as Jezebel heard that Naboth had been stoned and he was dead, Jezebel told Ahab, “Get up, go and claim ownership of the vineyard of Naboth from Jezreel, which he refused to sell you, for Naboth is no longer alive, but dead.”
011	1KI	021	016	When Ahab heard that Naboth was dead, he got up and went to claim ownership of Naboth's vineyard.
011	1KI	021	017	Then the Lord sent a message to Elijah the Tishbite:
011	1KI	021	018	“Go and meet Ahab, king of Israel, in Samaria. He is right now in Naboth's vineyard, where he has gone to claim ownership of it.
011	1KI	021	019	Tell him: This is what the Lord says: Have you murdered a man and robbed him? Then tell him: This is what the Lord says: In the very spot where dogs licked up Naboth's blood, dogs will lick up your own blood.”
011	1KI	021	020	“So have you come to find me, my enemy?” Ahab asked Elijah. “I have found you, because you have sold yourself to do what is evil in the Lord's sight,” Elijah replied.
011	1KI	021	021	He says, “Watch out! I'm going to bring disaster on you and destroy your descendants. I will kill every male of Ahab's line, both slave and free, throughout the whole of Israel.
011	1KI	021	022	I will make your house like that of Jeroboam, son of Nebat, and like that of Baasha, son of Ahijah, because you have angered me and made Israel sin.
011	1KI	021	023	And as far as Jezebel is concerned, the Lord says, ‘Dogs will eat Jezebel by the wall of Jezreel.’
011	1KI	021	024	Those of Ahab's family who die in the town will be eaten by dogs, and those who die in the countryside will be eaten by birds.”
011	1KI	021	025	(No one else was so bad as Ahab, who sold himself to do what is evil in the Lord's sight, because his wife Jezebel encouraged him.
011	1KI	021	026	He did the most despicable things, worshiping idols like the Amorites the Lord had driven out before Israel.)
011	1KI	021	027	As soon as Ahab heard this message, he tore his clothes, put on sackcloth, and fasted. He even went to bed in sackcloth, and walked around repentantly.
011	1KI	021	028	Then the Lord sent a message to Elijah the Tishbite:
011	1KI	021	029	“Have you seen how Ahab has humbled himself before me? Because he has humbled himself before me, I will not bring the disaster during his lifetime, but I will bring down disaster on his family in the lifetime of his son.”
011	1KI	022	001	For three years Aram and Israel were not at war.
011	1KI	022	002	But in the third year Jehoshaphat, king of Judah, went to visit the king of Israel.
011	1KI	022	003	The king of Israel had said to his officers, “Aren't you aware that Ramoth-gilead really belongs to us and yet we haven't done anything to take it back from the king of Aram?”
011	1KI	022	004	So he asked Jehoshaphat, “Will you join me in an attack to recapture Ramoth-gilead?” Jehoshaphat replied to the king of Israel, “You and I are as one, my men and your men are as one, and my horses and your horses are as one.”
011	1KI	022	005	Then Jehoshaphat said to the king of Israel, “But first though, please find out what the Lord says.”
011	1KI	022	006	So the king of Israel brought out the prophets—four hundred of them—and he asked them, “Should I go up and attack Ramoth-gilead, or should I not?” “Yes, go ahead,” they replied, “for the Lord will hand it over to the king.”
011	1KI	022	007	But Jehoshaphat asked, “Isn't there another prophet of the Lord here that we can ask?”
011	1KI	022	008	“Yes, there's another man who could consult the Lord,” the king of Israel replied, “but I don't like him because he never prophesies anything good for me—it's always bad! His name is Micaiah, son of Imlah.” “You shouldn't talk like that,” said Jehoshaphat.
011	1KI	022	009	The king of Israel called over one of his officials and told him, “Bring me Micaiah, son of Imlah, right away.”
011	1KI	022	010	Dressed in their royal robes, the king of Israel and King Jehoshaphat of Judah, were sitting on their thrones at the threshing floor beside the gate of Samaria, with all of the prophets prophesying in front of them.
011	1KI	022	011	One of them, Zedekiah, son of Chenaanah, had made himself iron horns. He announced, “This is what the Lord says: ‘With these horns you will gore the Arameans until they're dead!’”
011	1KI	022	012	All the prophets were prophesying the same thing, saying, “Go ahead, attack Ramoth-gilead; you will be successful, for the Lord will hand it over to the king.”
011	1KI	022	013	The messenger who went to call Micaiah told him, “Look, all the prophets are unanimous in prophesying positively to the king. So please make sure to speak positively like them.”
011	1KI	022	014	But Micaiah replied, “As the Lord lives, I can only say what my God tells me.”
011	1KI	022	015	When he came before the king, the king asked him, “Should we go up and attack Ramoth-gilead, or should we not?” “Yes, go ahead and be victorious,” Micaiah replied, “for the Lord will give it into the king's hand.”
011	1KI	022	016	But the king said to him, “How many times must I make you swear to tell me only the truth in the name of the Lord?”
011	1KI	022	017	So Micaiah answered, “I saw all Israel scattered on the mountains like sheep without a shepherd. The Lord said, ‘These people have no master; let each of them go home in peace.’”
011	1KI	022	018	The king of Israel said to Jehoshaphat, “Didn't I tell you he never prophesies anything good for me, only bad?”
011	1KI	022	019	Micaiah went on to say, “So listen to what the Lord says. I saw the Lord sitting on his throne, surrounded by the whole army of heaven standing to his right and to his left.
011	1KI	022	020	The Lord asked, ‘Who will trick Ahab, king of Israel, into attacking Ramoth-gilead so he will be killed there?’ One said this, another said that, and another said something else.
011	1KI	022	021	Finally a spirit came and approached the Lord and said, ‘I will trick him.’
011	1KI	022	022	‘How are you going to do that?’ the Lord asked. ‘I will go and be a lying spirit and make all his prophets tell lies,’ the spirit replied. ‘That will work,’ the Lord responded. ‘Go and do it.’
011	1KI	022	023	As you see, the Lord has put a lying spirit into these prophets of yours, and the Lord has pronounced your death sentence.”
011	1KI	022	024	Then Zedekiah, son of Chenaanah, went and slapped Micaiah in the face, and demanded, “Which way did the Spirit of the Lord go when he left me to speak to you?”
011	1KI	022	025	“You'll soon find out when you try and find some secret place to hide!” Micaiah replied.
011	1KI	022	026	The king of Israel ordered, “Place Micaiah under arrest and take him back to Amon the governor of the city and to my son Joash.
011	1KI	022	027	Tell them these are the king's instructions: ‘Put this man in jail. Give him only bread and water until my safe return.’”
011	1KI	022	028	“If you do in fact return safely then the Lord has not spoken through me,” Micaiah declared. “Pay attention everyone to all I've said!”
011	1KI	022	029	The king of Israel and Jehoshaphat, king of Judah, went to attack Ramoth-gilead.
011	1KI	022	030	The king of Israel told Jehoshaphat, “When I go into battle I will be in disguise, but you should wear your royal robes.” So the king of Israel disguised himself and went into battle.
011	1KI	022	031	The king of Aram had already given these orders to his chariot commanders: “Head straight for the king of Israel alone. Don't fight with anyone else, whoever they are.”
011	1KI	022	032	So when the chariot commanders saw Jehoshaphat, they shouted, “This must be the king of Israel!” So they turned to attack him, but when Jehoshaphat called out for help,
011	1KI	022	033	the chariot commanders saw it wasn't the king of Israel and stopped chasing him.
011	1KI	022	034	However, an enemy archer shot an arrow at random, hitting the king of Israel between the joints of his armor by his breastplate. The king told his charioteer, “Turn around and get me out of the fight, because I've been wounded!”
011	1KI	022	035	The battle lasted all day. The king of Israel was propped up in his chariot to face the Arameans, but in the evening he died. The blood had poured out of his wound onto the floor of the chariot.
011	1KI	022	036	At sunset, a shout went out from the lines: “Retreat! Every man back to his town, every man back to his own country!”
011	1KI	022	037	So the king died. He was taken back to Samaria where they buried him.
011	1KI	022	038	They washed his chariot at a pool in Samaria where the prostitutes came to bathe, and dogs licked up his blood, just as the Lord had said.
011	1KI	022	039	The rest of what happened in Ahab's reign, all that he did, the ivory palace he constructed and all the cities he built are recorded in the Book of Chronicles of the Kings of Israel.
011	1KI	022	040	Ahab died and his son Ahaziah succeeded him as king.
011	1KI	022	041	Jehoshaphat, son of Asa, became king of Judah in the fourth year of the reign of Ahab, king of Israel.
011	1KI	022	042	Jehoshaphat was thirty-five when he became king, and he reigned in Jerusalem for twenty-five years. His mother's name was Azubah, daughter of Shilhi.
011	1KI	022	043	He followed all the ways of his father; he did not depart from them, and he did what was right in the Lord's sight. However, the high places were not removed and the people still sacrificed and presented offerings there.
011	1KI	022	044	Jehoshaphat also made peace with the king of Israel.
011	1KI	022	045	The rest of what happened in Jehoshaphat's reign, his great achievements and the wars he fought are recorded in the Book of Chronicles of the Kings of Judah.
011	1KI	022	046	He expelled from the land any cult prostitutes who were left from the time of his father Asa.
011	1KI	022	047	(At that time there was no king in Edom; only a deputy who served as king.)
011	1KI	022	048	Jehoshaphat built seagoing ships to sail to Ophir for gold, but they didn't go because they were wrecked at Ezion-geber.
011	1KI	022	049	During that time Ahaziah, son of Ahab, asked Jehoshaphat, “Let my men sail with your men,” but Jehoshaphat refused.
011	1KI	022	050	Jehoshaphat died and was buried with his forefathers in the City of David. His son Jehoram succeeded him as king.
011	1KI	022	051	Ahaziah, son of Ahab, became king of Israel in Samaria in the seventeenth year of Jehoshaphat, king of Judah, and he reigned over Israel for two years.
011	1KI	022	052	He did what was evil in the Lord's sight and followed the ways of his father and mother, and of Jeroboam, son of Nebat, who had made Israel sin.
011	1KI	022	053	He served Baal and worshiped him, and angered the Lord, the God of Israel, just as his father had.
