012	2KI	012	001	Joash became king in the seventh year of the reign of Jehu, and he reigned in Jerusalem for forty years. His mother's name was Zibiah of Beersheba.
012	2KI	012	002	Joash did what was right in the Lord's sight during the years that Jehoiada the priest advised him.
012	2KI	012	003	Even so, the high places were not removed—the people went on sacrificing and presenting burnt offerings at these places.
012	2KI	012	004	Joash told the priests, “Collect together all the money that is brought as holy offerings to the Lord's Temple, whether the census money, the money from individual vows, or the money brought as a voluntary donation to the Lord's Temple.
012	2KI	012	005	Let each priest receive the money from those who give, and use it to repair whatever damage is discovered in the Temple.”
012	2KI	012	006	But by the twenty-third year of the reign of Joash, the priests still had not repaired the damage to the Temple.
012	2KI	012	007	So King Joash called together Jehoiada and the other priests and asked, “Why haven't you repaired the damage in the Temple? Don't use any more money you're given for yourselves, instead hand it over to others to repair the Temple.”
012	2KI	012	008	The priests agreed not to receive any more money from the people, and that they wouldn't carry out the repairs to the Temple themselves.
012	2KI	012	009	Jehoiada the priest took a large wooden box, cut a hole in its lid, and placed it on the right side of the altar next to the entrance to the Lord's Temple. There the priests who guarded the doorway put all the money brought into the Lord's Temple into the collection box.
012	2KI	012	010	Whenever they saw there was a lot of money in the box, the king's secretary and the high priest would come, count the money brought into the Lord's Temple, and put it into bags.
012	2KI	012	011	Then they weighed out the money and gave it to the supervisors of the work on the Lord's Temple. They paid the ones doing the work—the carpenters, builders,
012	2KI	012	012	masons, and stonecutters. They also bought the timber and blocks of cut stone needed for the repair of the Lord's Temple, and paid all the other costs of restoring the Temple.
012	2KI	012	013	However, the money collected for the Lord's Temple was not used for making silver basins, lamp trimmers, bowls, trumpets, or any items of gold or silver for the Lord's Temple.
012	2KI	012	014	It was used to pay the workers doing the repairs to the Lord's Temple.
012	2KI	012	015	No accounts were demanded from the men who received the money to pay the workers because they did everything honestly.
012	2KI	012	016	The money from the guilt offerings and sin offerings was not collected for the Lord's Temple because it belonged to the priests.
