012	2KI	015	032	Jotham, son of Uzziah, became king of Judah in the second year of the reign of Pekah son of Remaliah, king of Israel.
012	2KI	015	033	He was twenty-five when he became king, and he reigned in Jerusalem for sixteen years. His mother's name was Jerusha, daughter of Zadok.
012	2KI	015	034	He did what was right in the Lord's sight, just as his father Uzziah had done.
012	2KI	015	035	But the high places were not removed. The people still were sacrificing and presenting burnt offerings in those places. He rebuilt the upper gate of the Lord's Temple.
012	2KI	015	036	The rest of the events of Jotham's reign are recorded in the Book of Chronicles of the Kings of Judah.
012	2KI	015	037	During that time the Lord started sending Rezin, king of Aram, and Pekah, son of Remaliah, to attack Judah.
012	2KI	015	038	Jotham died and was buried with his forefathers in the City of David, his ancestor. His son Ahaz succeeded him as king.
012	2KI	016	001	Ahaz, son of Jotham, became king of Judah in the seventeenth year of the reign of Pekah, son of Remaliah.
012	2KI	016	002	Ahaz was twenty when he became king, and he reigned in Jerusalem for sixteen years. But unlike David his forefather, he did not do what was right in the sight of the Lord his God.
012	2KI	016	003	He followed the ways of the kings of Israel, and he even sacrificed his son in the fire, participating in the disgusting practices of the nations the Lord had driven out before the Israelites.
012	2KI	016	004	He sacrificed and presented burnt offerings on the high places and on the hills and under every green tree.
012	2KI	016	005	Rezin, king of Aram, and Pekah, son of Remaliah, king of Israel, came and attacked Jerusalem. They besieged Ahaz but couldn't defeat him.
012	2KI	016	006	This was when Rezin, king of Aram, recovered Elath for Edom. He expelled the people of Judah, and sent Edomites to Elath, where they still live to this day.
012	2KI	016	007	Ahaz sent messengers to Tiglath-pileser, king of Assyria, saying, “I'm your servant, and your son. Please come and rescue me from the kings of Aram and Israel who are attacking me.”
012	2KI	016	008	Ahaz took the silver and gold from the Lord's Temple and from the treasuries of the king's palace, and he sent it to the king of Assyria as a gift.
012	2KI	016	009	The king of Assyria responded positively to him. He went and attacked Damascus, and captured it. He deported its inhabitants to Kir and executed Rezin.
012	2KI	016	010	King Ahaz went to Damascus to meet Tiglath-Pileser, king of Assyria. During his visit he saw an altar in Damascus, and he sent Uriah the priest a drawing of the altar, along with instructions how to build it.
012	2KI	016	011	So Uriah the priest built an altar following all the instructions King Ahaz had sent from Damascus, completing it before King Ahaz returned.
012	2KI	016	012	When the king came back from Damascus he saw the altar. He went over to it and made offerings on it.
012	2KI	016	013	He presented his burnt offering and his grain offering, he poured out his drink offering, and sprinkled the blood of his friendship offerings on it.
012	2KI	016	014	He also moved the bronze altar that stood before the Lord from the front of the Temple, between the new altar and the Lord's Temple, and he placed it to the north of the new altar.
012	2KI	016	015	Then King Ahaz ordered Uriah the priest: “Use this new important altar to offer the morning burnt offering, the evening grain offering, the king's burnt offering and grain offering, and the burnt offering of all the people, and their grain offerings and their drink offerings. Sprinkle on this altar the blood of all the burnt offerings and sacrifices. The old bronze altar I'll use for divination.”
012	2KI	016	016	Uriah the priest followed King Ahaz's orders.
012	2KI	016	017	King Ahaz also removed the frames of the movable carts, and also took out the bronze basin from each of them. He removed the Sea from the bronze bulls it rested on and placed it on a stone pedestal.
012	2KI	016	018	He took down the Sabbath canopy they had built in the Temple, as well as the king's outer entrance to the Lord's Temple. He did this to please the king of Assyria.
012	2KI	016	019	The rest of what happened in Ahaz's reign and all he did are recorded in the Book of Chronicles of the Kings of Judah.
012	2KI	016	020	Ahaz died and was buried with his forefathers in the City of David. His son Hezekiah succeeded him as king.
012	2KI	017	001	Hoshea, son of Elah, became king of Israel, in the twelfth year of the reign of King Ahaz of Judah. He reigned in Samaria for nine years.
012	2KI	017	002	He did what was evil in the Lord's sight, but not in the same way as the kings of Israel who were before him.
012	2KI	017	003	Shalmaneser, king of Assyria, came and attacked him, and Hoshea submitted to him and paid him tribute.
012	2KI	017	004	But then the king of Assyria discovered that Hoshea was being disloyal. Hoshea had sent messengers to So, king of Egypt, asking for help, and he also had stopped sending annual tribute to the king of Assyria as he had done previously. So the king of Assyria arrested Hoshea and put him in prison.
012	2KI	017	005	Then the king of Assyria invaded the whole country, and attacked Samaria, besieging it for three years.
012	2KI	017	006	In the ninth year of the reign of Hoshea, the king of Assyria captured Samaria and deported the Israelites to Assyria. He settled them in Halah, in Gozan on the Habor River, and in the towns of the Medes.
012	2KI	017	007	All this happened because the people of Israel had sinned against the Lord their God, the one who had led them out of Egypt, saving them from the power of Pharaoh, king of Egypt. They had worshiped other gods,
012	2KI	017	008	adopting the ways of the nations the Lord had driven out before the Israelites, and the pagan practices introduced by the kings of Israel.
012	2KI	017	009	Secretly the Israelites did things that were not right against the Lord their God. They built high places in all their towns, from watchtowers to fortified cities.
012	2KI	017	010	They set up pagan stone pillars and Asherah poles on every high hill and under every green tree.
012	2KI	017	011	They offered sacrifices on all the high places, just like the nations the Lord drove out before them. They did evil things, angering the Lord.
012	2KI	017	012	They worshiped idols, even though the Lord had told them, “You are not to do this.”
012	2KI	017	013	Yet the Lord had warned Israel and Judah repeatedly through all his prophets and seers, saying, “Leave your wicked ways and keep my commandments and instructions. Follow the whole law that I ordered your forefathers to obey, and gave to you through my servants the prophets.”
012	2KI	017	014	But they refused to listen, and they were as stubborn as their forefathers who did not trust the Lord their God.
012	2KI	017	015	They gave up on his regulations and the covenant he had made with their forefathers, as well as the decrees he had given them. They followed pointless idols and they became pointless themselves, copying the nations around them that the Lord ordered them not to copy.
012	2KI	017	016	They ignored all the commandments of the Lord their God, and they made themselves two metal idols of calves and an Asherah pole. They bowed down in worship to the sun, moon, and stars and served Baal.
012	2KI	017	017	They sacrificed their sons and daughters as pagan burnt offerings, and practiced fortune-telling and witchcraft. They gave themselves over to doing what was evil in the Lord's sight, making him angry.
012	2KI	017	018	So the Lord was extremely angry with Israel, and he banished them from his presence. Only the tribe of Judah was left,
012	2KI	017	019	but even Judah did not keep the commandments of the Lord their God, but followed the idolatry that Israel had introduced.
012	2KI	017	020	The Lord gave up on all the descendants of Israel. He punished them and handed them over to their enemies, until he had banished them from his presence.
012	2KI	017	021	When the Lord ripped Israel away from the house of David, they made Jeroboam, son of Nebat, their king. Jeroboam led Israel away from the Lord and made them commit terrible sins.
012	2KI	017	022	The Israelites continued to practice all the sins that Jeroboam committed. They did not stop doing them,
012	2KI	017	023	so the Lord eventually expelled them from his presence, just as he had said he would through all his servants, the prophets. So the Israelites were deported from their land and taken to Assyria, where they are to this day.
012	2KI	017	024	The king of Assyria brought people from Babylon, Kuthah, Avva, Hamath, and Sepharvaim and settled them in the towns of Samaria instead of the Israelites. They took over ownership of Samaria and lived in its towns.
012	2KI	017	025	When they first started living there they did not worship the Lord, so he sent lions there among them, killing some of them.
012	2KI	017	026	The king of Assyria was told, “The people that you brought and settled in the towns of Samaria they don't know the rules of the God of the land. As a result, he has sent lions among them, which are killing them off because they don't know what the God of the land requires.”
012	2KI	017	027	The king of Assyria gave the order: “Send back one of the priests you deported from Samaria, and let him go back and live there and teach the rules of the God of the land.”
012	2KI	017	028	So one of the priests who had been deported from Samaria returned to live in Bethel, and taught them how to worship the Lord.
012	2KI	017	029	But the people of the different nations went on making their own gods in the towns where they had settled, and they placed them in the shrines of the high places that the people of Samaria had made.
012	2KI	017	030	Those from Babylon made Sukkoth Benoth, those from Kuthah made Nergal, and those from Hamath made Ashima.
012	2KI	017	031	The Avvites made Nibhaz and Tartak, and the Sepharvites sacrificed their children as burnt offerings to their gods Adrammelech and Anammelech.
012	2KI	017	032	While they worshiped the Lord, they also appointed priests from all kinds of their own people to offer sacrifices for them in the shrines of the high places.
012	2KI	017	033	So even though they worshiped the Lord, they also worshiped their own gods following the practices of the nations they had come from.
012	2KI	017	034	Even to this day they are still following their former practices. None of them truly worships the Lord or observes the regulations, requirements, laws, and commandments that the Lord gave to the descendants of Jacob, the one he named Israel.
012	2KI	017	035	For the Lord had made an agreement with the Israelites, ordering them, “Don't worship other gods or bow down to them; don't serve them or offer sacrifices to them.
012	2KI	017	036	You must worship only the Lord, who led you out of Egypt, helping you with his great power and strong arm. Only bow down to him; only offer sacrifices to him.
012	2KI	017	037	Always pay attention to observe the regulations, requirements, laws, and commandments he gave you in writing, and don't worship other gods.
012	2KI	017	038	Don't forget the agreement I have made with you, and don't worship other gods.
012	2KI	017	039	You must worship only the Lord your God, and he will save you from all your enemies.”
