012	2KI	023	015	He also demolished the altar at Bethel, the high place set up by Jeroboam, son of Nebat, who had made Israel sin. Then he burned the high place, ground it to dust, and burned the Asherah pole.
012	2KI	023	016	As Josiah looked around he saw some tombs there on the hill. He had the bones taken from the tombs, and he burned them on the altar to defile it, just as the Lord had said through the man of God who had prophesied these things.
012	2KI	023	017	Then he asked, “Whose gravestone is this that I see?” “It's the tomb of the man of God who came from Judah and proclaimed exactly what you have done to the altar of Bethel,” the townspeople replied.
012	2KI	023	018	“Let him rest in peace,” said Josiah. “Don't anyone touch his bones.” So they left his bones undisturbed, together with the bones of the prophet who came from Samaria.
012	2KI	023	019	Josiah destroyed, just as he did at Bethel, all the shrines of the high places in the towns of Samaria that the kings of Israel had built that had angered the Lord.
012	2KI	023	020	Josiah slaughtered all the priests who were there at the high places on the altars, and burned human bones on them. Then he returned to Jerusalem.
012	2KI	023	021	The king sent out an order to all the people, “Observe the Passover of the Lord your God, as it is written in this Book of the Agreement.”
012	2KI	023	022	Such a Passover as this had not been observed from the days of the judges who ruled Israel on through all the days of the kings of Israel and Judah.
012	2KI	023	023	But in the eighteenth year of King Josiah, this Passover was observed to honor the Lord in Jerusalem.
012	2KI	023	024	In addition, Josiah got rid of the mediums and spiritists, the household gods and idols, and all the disgusting practices that were present in the land of Judah and in Jerusalem. He did this to fulfill the words of the law written in the book that Hilkiah the priest had found in the Lord's Temple.
012	2KI	023	025	Never before was there a king like him who committed himself to the Lord in all his thoughts and attitudes, and with all his strength, keeping all the Law of Moses. There was no king like him afterwards either.
012	2KI	023	026	However, the Lord had not given up his furious hostility, which burned against Judah because of all that Manasseh had done to anger him.
012	2KI	023	027	So the Lord announced, “I'm also going to banish Judah from my presence, just as I banished Israel. I will abandon this city that I have chosen, Jerusalem, and the Temple regarding which I said, My name will be there.”
012	2KI	023	028	The rest of what happened in Josiah's reign, and all he did, are recorded in the Book of Chronicles of the Kings of Judah.
012	2KI	023	029	While Josiah was still king, Pharaoh Neco, king of Egypt, led his army to help the king of Assyria at the Euphrates River. King Josiah took his army to fight him at Megiddo, but when Neco saw Josiah he killed him.
012	2KI	023	030	His servants put his body in a chariot, brought him back from Megiddo to Jerusalem, and buried him in his own tomb. Then the people of the land chose Jehoahaz, son of Josiah, anointed him, and made him king in succession to his father.
012	2KI	023	031	Jehoahaz was twenty-three when he became king, and he reigned in Jerusalem for three months. His mother's name was Hamutal, daughter of Jeremiah. She came from Libnah.
012	2KI	023	032	He did what was evil in the Lord's sight, just as all his forefathers had done.
012	2KI	023	033	Pharaoh Neco imprisoned Jehoahaz at Riblah in the land of Hamath to stop him ruling in Jerusalem. He also imposed on Judah a tribute of one hundred talents of silver and a talent of gold.
012	2KI	023	034	Pharaoh Neco made Eliakim, son of Josiah, king in succession to his father Josiah, and he changed Eliakim's name to Jehoiakim. Neco took Jehoahaz to Egypt, where he died.
012	2KI	023	035	Jehoiakim paid the silver and gold to Pharaoh Neco, but in order to meet Pharaoh's demand he taxed the land and required payment of the silver and the gold from the people, each in proportion to their wealth.
012	2KI	023	036	Jehoiakim was twenty-five when he became king, and he reigned in Jerusalem for eleven years. His mother's name was Zebidah, daughter of Pedaiah She came from Rumah.
012	2KI	023	037	He did evil in the Lord's sight, just as his forefathers had done.
012	2KI	024	001	During the reign of Jehoiakim, Nebuchadnezzar, king of Babylon, invaded the country and Jehoiakim submitted to him. But after three years Jehoiakim rebelled against Nebuchadnezzar.
012	2KI	024	002	Then the Lord sent bands of raiders against Judah to destroy them. They came from Babylonia, Aram, Moab, and Ammon, just as the Lord had said through his servants the prophets.
012	2KI	024	003	The Lord spoke against Judah to banish them from his presence because of all the sins that Manasseh had committed, and the innocent people he had killed,
012	2KI	024	004	filling Jerusalem with their blood. The Lord was not willing to forgive this.
012	2KI	024	005	The rest of what happened in Jehoiakim's reign, and all he did, are recorded in the Book of Chronicles of the Kings of Judah.
012	2KI	024	006	Jehoiakim died, and his son Jehoiachin succeeded him as king.
012	2KI	024	007	The king of Egypt didn't leave his country again for the king of Babylon had taken all the territory that used to belong to him, from the Wadi of Egypt all the way to the Euphrates River.
012	2KI	024	008	Jehoiachin was eighteen when he became king, and he reigned in Jerusalem for three months. His mother was Nehushta, daughter of Elnathan. She came from Jerusalem.
012	2KI	024	009	Jehoiachin did what was evil in the Lord's sight, just as his father had done.
012	2KI	024	010	At that time the officers of Nebuchadnezzar, king of Babylon attacked Jerusalem and besieged it.
012	2KI	024	011	Then Nebuchadnezzar, king of Babylon came himself while his officers were besieging the city.
012	2KI	024	012	Jehoiachin, king of Israel, surrendered to the king of Babylon, along with his mother, his officers, his commanders, and his officials. It was in the eighth year of his reign that Nebuchadnezzar captured Jehoiachin.
012	2KI	024	013	Nebuchadnezzar took all the treasures from the Lord's Temple and the royal palace, and he cut up all the gold items that Solomon, king of Israel, had made for the Lord's Temple, as the Lord had said would happen.
012	2KI	024	014	He deported all of Jerusalem, all the commanders and experienced soldiers, all the craftsmen and metal-workers, a total of ten thousand prisoners. Only the very poor people of the land were left.
012	2KI	024	015	He took Jehoiachin away into exile to Babylon, as well as the king's mother and the king's wives and his officials and the leading men of the land, he deported them all from Jerusalem to Babylon.
012	2KI	024	016	The king of Babylon also deported to Babylon all seven thousand fighting men and one thousand craftsmen and metal-workers, who were all strong and ready for battle.
012	2KI	024	017	The king of Babylon made Mattaniah, Jehoiachin's uncle, king instead of him, and changed his name to Zedekiah.
012	2KI	024	018	Zedekiah was twenty-one when he became king, and he reigned in Jerusalem for eleven years. His mother's name was Hamutal, daughter of Jeremiah. She came from Libnah.
012	2KI	024	019	He did evil in the Lord's sight, just as Jehoiakim had done.
012	2KI	024	020	All this happened in Jerusalem and Judah, because of the Lord's anger, until he eventually banished them from his presence. Zedekiah rebelled against the king of Babylon.
012	2KI	025	001	In the ninth year of Zedekiah's reign, on the tenth day of the tenth month, Nebuchadnezzar, king of Babylon, attacked Jerusalem with his entire army. He set up camp around the city and built siege ramps against the walls.
012	2KI	025	002	The city remained under siege until the eleventh year of King Zedekiah.
012	2KI	025	003	By the ninth day of the fourth month, the famine in the city was so bad that the people had nothing left to eat.
012	2KI	025	004	Then the city wall was broken through, and all the soldiers escaped at night through the gate between the two walls by the king's garden, even though the Babylonians had the city surrounded. They ran away in the direction of the Arabah,
012	2KI	025	005	but the Babylonian army chased after the king and caught up with him on the plains of Jericho. His whole army had scattered and left him.
012	2KI	025	006	They captured the king and took him to the king of Babylon at Riblah, where he was sentenced.
012	2KI	025	007	They slaughtered Zedekiah's sons while he watched, and then gouged out his eyes, bound him in bronze shackles, and took him to Babylon.
012	2KI	025	008	On the seventh day of the fifth month, in the nineteenth year of Nebuchadnezzar, king of Babylon, Nebuzaradan, the commander of the guard, an officer of the king of Babylon, entered Jerusalem.
012	2KI	025	009	He burned down the Lord's Temple, the royal palace, and all the large buildings of Jerusalem.
012	2KI	025	010	The whole Babylonian army under the commander of the guard knocked down the walls around Jerusalem.
012	2KI	025	011	Nebuzaradan, the commander of the guard, deported those who were left in the city, even those who had gone over to the side of the king of Babylon, as well as the rest of the population.
012	2KI	025	012	But the commander of the guard allowed the poor people who were left in the country to stay and take care of the vineyards and the fields.
012	2KI	025	013	The Babylonians broke into pieces the bronze pillars, the movable carts, and the bronze Sea that belonged to the Lord's Temple, and they took all the bronze to Babylon.
012	2KI	025	014	They also took all the pots, shovels, lamp snuffers, dishes, and all the other bronze items used in the Temple service.
012	2KI	025	015	The commander of the guard removed the censers and bowls, anything that was made of pure gold or silver.
012	2KI	025	016	The amount of bronze that came from the two columns, the Sea and the movable carts, which Solomon had made for the Lord's Temple, all of this weighed more than could be measured.
012	2KI	025	017	Each column was eighteen cubits tall. The bronze capital on top of one column was three cubits high, with a network of bronze pomegranates around it. The second column was the same, and also had a decorative network.
012	2KI	025	018	The commander of the guard took as prisoners Seraiah, the chief priest, Zephaniah the priest, second in rank, and the three Temple doorkeepers.
012	2KI	025	019	From those left in the city he took the officer in charge of the soldiers, and five of the king's advisors. He also took the secretary to the army commander who was in charge of calling up the people for military service, and sixty other men who were present in the city.
012	2KI	025	020	Nebuzaradan, the commander of the guard, took them and brought them before the king of Babylon at Riblah.
012	2KI	025	021	The king of Babylon had them executed at Riblah in the land of Hamath. So the people of Judah had to leave their land.
012	2KI	025	022	Nebuchadnezzar, king of Babylon, appointed Gedaliah, son of Ahikam, son of Shaphan, as governor over the people he had left in the land of Judah.
012	2KI	025	023	When all the army officers of Judah and their men learned that the king of Babylon had appointed Gedaliah as governor, they and their men met with Gedaliah at Mizpah. They included: Ishmael son of Nethaniah, Johanan, son of Kareah, Seraiah, son of Tanhumeth the Netophathite, Jaazaniah, son of the Maakathite.
012	2KI	025	024	Gedaliah swore an oath to them and their men, telling them, “Don't be afraid of the Babylonian officials. Stay here in the land and serve the king of Babylon, and you'll be fine.”
012	2KI	025	025	But in the seventh month, Ishmael, son of Nethaniah, son of Elishama, of royal blood, came with ten men. They attacked and killed Gedaliah, along with the men of Judea and Babylonians who were with him at Mizpah.
012	2KI	025	026	As a result, all the people, from the least to the greatest, along with the army commanders, ran away to Egypt, terrified of what the Babylonians would do.
012	2KI	025	027	In the year Evil-merodach became king of Babylon, he released Jehoiachin, king of Judah, from prison. This happened on the twenty-seventh day of the twelfth month of the thirty-seventh year of the exile of Jehoiachin, king of Judah.
012	2KI	025	028	The king of Babylon treated him well and gave him a position of honor higher than the other kings there with him in Babylon.
012	2KI	025	029	So Jehoiachin was able to remove his prison clothes, and he ate frequently at the king's table for the rest of his life.
012	2KI	025	030	The king provided Jehoiachin with a daily allowance for the rest of his life.
#
# BOOK	013	1CH	1 Chronicles	1 Chronicles
013	1CH	001	001	Adam, Seth, Enosh,
013	1CH	001	002	Kenan, Mahalalel, Jared,
013	1CH	001	003	Enoch, Methuselah, Lamech, Noah.
013	1CH	001	004	The sons of Noah: Shem, Ham, and Japheth.
013	1CH	001	005	The sons of Japheth: Gomer, Magog, Madai, Javan, Tubal, Meshech, and Tiras.
013	1CH	001	006	The sons of Gomer: Ashkenaz, Riphath, and Togarmah.
013	1CH	001	007	The sons of Javan: Elishah, Tarshish, Kittim, Rodanim.
013	1CH	001	008	The sons of Ham: Cush, Mizraim, Put, and Canaan.
013	1CH	001	009	The sons of Cush: Seba, Havilah, Sabta, Raamah, and Sabteca. The sons of Raamah: Sheba and Dedan.
013	1CH	001	010	Cush was the father of Nimrod, who became the world's first tyrant.
013	1CH	001	011	Mizraim was the father of the Ludites, Anamites, Lehabites, Naphtuhites,
013	1CH	001	012	Pathrusites, Casluhites, and Caphtorites (ancestors of the Philistines),
013	1CH	001	013	Canaan was the father of his firstborn son, Sidon, and of the Hittites,
013	1CH	001	014	Jebusites, Amorites, Girgashites,
013	1CH	001	015	Hivites, Arkites, Sinites,
013	1CH	001	016	Arvadites, Zemarites, and Hamathites.
013	1CH	001	017	The sons of Shem: Elam, Asshur, Arphaxad, Lud, and Aram. The sons of Aram: Uz, Hul, Gether, and Meshech.
013	1CH	001	018	Arphaxad was the father of Shelah, and Shelah the father of Eber.
013	1CH	001	019	Eber had two sons. One was named Peleg, because in his time the earth was divided; the name of his brother was Joktan.
013	1CH	001	020	Joktan was the father of Almodad, Sheleph, Hazarmaveth, Jerah,
013	1CH	001	021	Hadoram, Uzal, Diklah,
013	1CH	001	022	Obal, Abimael, Sheba,
013	1CH	001	023	Ophir, Havilah, and Jobab. These were all Joktan's sons.
013	1CH	001	024	Shem, Arphaxad, Shelah,
013	1CH	001	025	Eber, Peleg, Reu,
013	1CH	001	026	Serug, Nahor, Terah,
013	1CH	001	027	and Abram (also called Abraham).
013	1CH	001	028	The sons of Abraham: Isaac and Ishmael.
013	1CH	001	029	These were their descendants: Nebaioth was the firstborn son of Ishmael, Kedar, Adbeel, Mibsam,
013	1CH	001	030	Mishma, Dumah, Massa, Hadad, Tema,
013	1CH	001	031	Jetur, Naphish, and Kedemah. These were Ishmael's sons.
013	1CH	001	032	The sons born to Keturah, Abraham's concubine. She gave birth to: Zimran, Jokshan, Medan, Midian, Ishbak, and Shuah. The sons of Jokshan: Sheba and Dedan.
013	1CH	001	033	The sons of Midian: Ephah, Epher, Hanoch, Abida, and Eldaah. These were all descendants of Keturah.
013	1CH	001	034	Abraham was the father of Isaac. The sons of Isaac: Esau and Israel.
013	1CH	001	035	The sons of Esau: Eliphaz, Reuel, Jeush, Jalam and Korah.
013	1CH	001	036	The sons of Eliphaz: Teman, Omar, Zepho, Gatam and Kenaz; and Amalek through Timna.
013	1CH	001	037	The sons of Reuel: Nahath, Zerah, Shammah and Mizzah.
013	1CH	001	038	The sons of Seir: Lotan, Shobal, Zibeon, Anah, Dishon, Ezer and Dishan.
013	1CH	001	039	The sons of Lotan: Hori and Homam. Lotan's sister was Timna.
013	1CH	001	040	The sons of Shobal: Alvan, Manahath, Ebal, Shepho and Onam. The sons of Zibeon: Aiah and Anah.
013	1CH	001	041	The son of Anah: Dishon. The sons of Dishon: Hemdan, Eshban, Ithran and Keran.
013	1CH	001	042	The sons of Ezer: Bilhan, Zaavan and Akan. The sons of Dishan: Uz and Aran.
013	1CH	001	043	These were the kings who reigned over Edom before any Israelite king reigned over them: Bela son of Beor, the name of whose city was Dinhabah.
013	1CH	001	044	When Bela died, Jobab son of Zerah from Bozrah took over as king.
013	1CH	001	045	When Jobab died, Husham from the land of the Temanites took over as king.
013	1CH	001	046	When Husham died, Hadad son of Bedad, took over as king. He was the one who defeated Midian in the country of Moab. The name of his city was Avith.
013	1CH	001	047	When Hadad died, Samlah from Masrekah took over as king.
013	1CH	001	048	When Samlah died, Shaul from Rehoboth on the River took over as king.
013	1CH	001	049	When Shaul died, Baal-Hanan, son of Acbor, took over as king.
013	1CH	001	050	When Baal-Hanan died, Hadad took over as king. The name of his city was Pau. The name of his wife was Mehetabel, daughter of Matred, the daughter of Me-Zahab.
013	1CH	001	051	Then Hadad died. The chiefs of Edom were: Timna, Alvah, Jetheth,
013	1CH	001	052	Oholibamah, Elah, Pinon,
013	1CH	001	053	Kenaz, Teman, Mibzar,
013	1CH	001	054	Magdiel, and Iram. These were Edom's chiefs.
013	1CH	002	001	These were the sons of Israel: Reuben, Simeon, Levi, Judah, Issachar, Zebulun,
013	1CH	002	002	Dan, Joseph, Benjamin, Naphtali, Gad, and Asher.
013	1CH	002	003	The sons of Judah: Er, Onan, and Shelah—these three the daughter of Shua, a Canaanite woman, bore to him. Er, Judah's firstborn, was wicked in the sight of the Lord, so he put him to death.
013	1CH	002	004	Tamar was Judah's daughter-in-law, and she bore him Perez and Zerah. Judah had a total of five sons.
013	1CH	002	005	The sons of Perez: Hezron and Hamul.
013	1CH	002	006	The sons of Zerah: Zimri, Ethan, Heman, Calcol and Darda—a total of five.
013	1CH	002	007	The son of Carmi: Achar, who caused trouble for Israel by being unfaithful in taking what was consecrated to the Lord.
013	1CH	002	008	The son of Ethan: Azariah.
013	1CH	002	009	The sons that were born to Hezron: Jerahmeel, Ram and Caleb.
013	1CH	002	010	Ram was the father of Amminadab, and Amminadab was the father of Nahshon, a leader of Judah's descendants.
013	1CH	002	011	Nahshon was the father of Salmon, Salmon was the father of Boaz,
013	1CH	002	012	Boaz was the father of Obed, and Obed was the father of Jesse.
013	1CH	002	013	Jesse was the father of his firstborn son Eliab; the second son was Abinadab, the third Shimea,
013	1CH	002	014	the fourth Nethanel, the fifth Raddai,
013	1CH	002	015	the sixth Ozem, and the seventh David.
013	1CH	002	016	Their sisters were Zeruiah and Abigail. The sons of Zeruiah's were Abishai, Joab, and Asahel, three in total.
013	1CH	002	017	Abigail gave birth to Amasa, and the father of Amasa was Jether the Ishmaelite.
013	1CH	002	018	Caleb son of Hezron had children by his wife Azubah, and also by Jerioth. These were her sons: Jesher, Shobab, and Ardon.
013	1CH	002	019	When Azubah died, Caleb took Ephrath to be his wife, and she bore him Hur.
013	1CH	002	020	Hur was the father of Uri, and Uri the father of Bezalel.
013	1CH	002	021	Later on Hezron slept with the daughter of Makir the father of Gilead, whom he married when he was sixty years old, and she bore him Segub.
013	1CH	002	022	Segub was the father of Jair, who had twenty-three towns in Gilead.
013	1CH	002	023	But Geshur and Aram took from them Havvoth Jair, along with Kenath and its towns, a total of sixty towns. These were all descendants of Makir the father of Gilead.
013	1CH	002	024	After Hezron died in Caleb Ephrathah, his wife Abijah bore him Ashhur the father of Tekoa.
013	1CH	002	025	The sons of Jerahmeel the firstborn of Hezron: Ram (firstborn), Bunah, Oren, Ozem, and Ahijah.
013	1CH	002	026	Jerahmeel had another wife called Atarah. She was the mother of Onam.
013	1CH	002	027	The sons of Ram the firstborn of Jerahmeel: Maaz, Jamin and Eker.
013	1CH	002	028	The sons of Onam: Shammai and Jada. The sons of Shammai: Nadab and Abishur.
013	1CH	002	029	Abishur's wife was called Abihail, who bore him Ahban and Molid.
013	1CH	002	030	The sons of Nadab: Seled and Appaim. Seled died without having children.
013	1CH	002	031	The son of Appaim: Ishi, the father of Sheshan. Sheshan was the father of Ahlai.
013	1CH	002	032	The sons of Jada, the brother of Shammai: Jether and Jonathan. Jether died without having children.
013	1CH	002	033	The sons of Jonathan: Peleth and Zaza. These are all the descendants of Jerahmeel.
013	1CH	002	034	Sheshan had no sons, he only had daughters, but he did have an Egyptian servant named Jarha.
013	1CH	002	035	So Sheshan gave his daughter in marriage to his servant Jarha, and she bore him Attai.
013	1CH	002	036	Attai was the father of Nathan. Nathan was the father of Zabad,
013	1CH	002	037	Zabad was the father of Ephlal, Ephlal was the father of Obed,
013	1CH	002	038	Obed was the father of Jehu, Jehu was the father of Azariah,
013	1CH	002	039	Azariah was the father of Helez, Helez was the father of Eleasah,
013	1CH	002	040	Eleasah was the father of Sismai, Sismai was the father of Shallum,
013	1CH	002	041	Shallum was the father of Jekamiah, and Jekamiah was the father of Elishama.
013	1CH	002	042	The sons of Caleb, the brother of Jerahmeel: Mesha his firstborn, who was the father of Ziph, and his son Mareshah, who was the father of Hebron.
013	1CH	002	043	The sons of Hebron: Korah, Tappuah, Rekem, and Shema.
013	1CH	002	044	Shema was the father of Raham, and Raham the father of Jorkeam. Rekem was the father of Shammai.
013	1CH	002	045	Shammai's son was Maon, and Maon was the father of Beth Zur.
013	1CH	002	046	Ephah, Caleb's concubine, was the mother of Haran, Moza, and Gazez. Haran was the father of Gazez.
013	1CH	002	047	The sons of Jahdai: Regem, Jotham, Geshan, Pelet, Ephah, and Shaaph.
013	1CH	002	048	Maacah, Caleb's concubine, was the mother of Sheber and Tirhanah.
013	1CH	002	049	She was also the mother of Shaaph, the father of Madmannah, and of Sheva, the father of Macbenah and Gibea. Caleb's daughter was Acsah.
013	1CH	002	050	These are all the descendants of Caleb. The sons of Hur the firstborn of Ephrathah: Shobal, the father of Kiriath Jearim,
013	1CH	002	051	Salma, the father of Bethlehem, and Hareph, the father of Beth Gader.
013	1CH	002	052	The descendants of Shobal the father of Kiriath Jearim were: Haroeh, half the Manahathites,
013	1CH	002	053	and the families of Kiriath Jearim: the Ithrites, Puthites, Shumathites, and Mishraites. From these descended the Zorathites and Eshtaolites.
013	1CH	002	054	The descendants of Salma: Bethlehem, the Netophathites, Atroth Beth Joab, half the Manahathites, the Zorites,
013	1CH	002	055	and the families of scribes who lived at Jabez: the Tirathites, Shimeathites, and Sucathites. These are the Kenites who descended from Hammath, the father of the house of Recab.
013	1CH	003	001	These were the sons of David born to him in Hebron: The firstborn was Amnon, whose mother was Ahinoam of Jezreel. The second was Daniel, whose mother was Abigail of Carmel.
013	1CH	003	002	The third was Absalom, whose mother was Maacah, the daughter of Talmai, king of Geshur. The fourth was Adonijah, whose mother was Haggith.
013	1CH	003	003	The fifth was Shephatiah, whose mother was Abital. The sixth was Ithream, whose mother was his wife Eglah.
013	1CH	003	004	These were the six sons born to David in Hebron, where he reigned seven years and six months. David reigned in Jerusalem thirty-three more years,
013	1CH	003	005	and these were the children born to him there: Shammua, Shobab, Nathan, and Solomon. Their mother was Bathsheba, daughter of Ammiel.
013	1CH	003	006	In addition there were also Ibhar, Elishua, Eliphelet,
013	1CH	003	007	Nogah, Nepheg, Japhia,
013	1CH	003	008	Elishama, Eliada, and Eliphelet, a total of nine.
013	1CH	003	009	These were all the sons of David, apart from his sons by his concubines. Their sister was Tamar.
013	1CH	003	010	The male lineage from Solomon was: Rehoboam, Abijah, Asa,
013	1CH	003	011	Jehoram, Ahaziah, Joash,
013	1CH	003	012	Amaziah, Azariah, Jotham,
013	1CH	003	013	Ahaz, Hezekiah, Manasseh,
013	1CH	003	014	Amon, Josiah.
013	1CH	003	015	The sons of Josiah: Johanan (firstborn), Jehoiakim (second), Zedekiah (third), Shallum (fourth).
013	1CH	003	016	The sons of Jehoiakim: Jehoiachin and Zedekiah.
013	1CH	003	017	The sons of Jehoiachin who were taken into captivity: Shealtiel,
013	1CH	003	018	Malkiram, Pedaiah, Shenazzar, Jekamiah, Hoshama, and Nedabiah.
013	1CH	003	019	The sons of Pedaiah: Zerubbabel and Shimei. The sons of Zerubbabel: Meshullam and Hananiah. Their sister was Shelomith.
013	1CH	003	020	Five additional sons were: Hashubah, Ohel, Berekiah, Hasadiah, and Jushab-Hesed.
013	1CH	003	021	The sons of Hananiah: Pelatiah and Jeshaiah, and the sons of Rephaiah, the sons of Arnan, the sons of Obadiah, and the sons of Shecaniah.
013	1CH	003	022	The sons of Shecaniah: Shemaiah and his sons: Hattush, Igal, Bariah, Neariah, and Shaphat—a total of six.
013	1CH	003	023	The sons of Neariah: Elioenai, Hizkiah and Azrikam—a total of three.
013	1CH	003	024	The sons of Elioenai: Hodaviah, Eliashib, Pelaiah, Akkub, Johanan, Delaiah, and Anani—a total of seven.
013	1CH	004	001	The sons of Judah: Perez, Hezron, Carmi, Hur, and Shobal.
013	1CH	004	002	Reaiah, son of Shobal, was the father of Jahath. Jahath was the father of Ahumai and Lahad. These were the families of the Zorathites.
013	1CH	004	003	These were the sons of Etam: Jezreel, Ishma, and Idbash. Their sister was called Hazzelelponi.
013	1CH	004	004	Penuel was the father of Gedor, and Ezer was the father of Hushah. These were the descendants of Hur, Ephrathah's firstborn and father of Bethlehem.
013	1CH	004	005	Ashhur was the father of Tekoa and had two wives, Helah and Naarah.
013	1CH	004	006	Naarah was the mother of Ahuzzam, Hepher, Temeni, and Haahashtari. These were the sons of Naarah.
013	1CH	004	007	The sons of Helah: Zereth, Zohar, Ethnan,
013	1CH	004	008	and Koz, who was the father of Anub and Hazzobebah and of the families of Aharhel, son of Harum.
013	1CH	004	009	Jabez was more faithful to God than his brothers. His mother had given him the name Jabez, saying, “I gave birth to him in pain.”
013	1CH	004	010	Jabez begged the God of Israel, “Please bless me and expand my borders! Be with me and keep me safe from harm so I won't have pain.” And God gave him what he asked for.
013	1CH	004	011	Kelub, Shuhah's brother, was the father of Mehir, who in turn was the father of Eshton.
013	1CH	004	012	Eshton was the father of Beth Rapha, Paseah, and Tehinnah, the father of Ir Nahash. These were the men of Recah.
013	1CH	004	013	The sons of Kenaz: Othniel and Seraiah. The sons of Othniel: Hathath and Meonothai.
013	1CH	004	014	Meonothai was the father of Ophrah. Seraiah was the father of Joab, the father of Ge Harashim, so called because craftsmen lived there.
013	1CH	004	015	The sons of Caleb son of Jephunneh: Iru, Elah, and Naam. The son of Elah: Kenaz.
013	1CH	004	016	The sons of Jehallelel: Ziph, Ziphah, Tiria, and Asarel.
013	1CH	004	017	The sons of Ezrah: Jether, Mered, Epher, and Jalon. One wife of Mered was the mother of Miriam, Shammai and Ishbah the father of Eshtemoa.
013	1CH	004	018	(Another wife who came from Judah was the mother of Jered the father of Gedor, Heber the father of Soco, and Jekuthiel the father of Zanoah.) These were the sons of Bithiah, Pharaoh's daughter, whom Mered had married.
013	1CH	004	019	The sons of Hodiah's wife, Nathan's sister: one son was the father of Keilah the Garmite, and another the father of Eshtemoa the Maacathite.
013	1CH	004	020	The sons of Shimon: Amnon, Rinnah, Ben-Hanan, and Tilon. The sons of Ishi: Zoheth and Ben-Zoheth.
013	1CH	004	021	The sons of Shelah son of Judah: Er, who was the father of Lecah, Laadah, who was the father of Mareshah, the families of the linen workers at Beth Ashbea,
013	1CH	004	022	Jokim, the men of Cozeba, and Joash and Saraph, who ruled over Moab and Jashubi Lehem. (These are old records.)
013	1CH	004	023	They were potters, inhabitants of Netaim and Gederah, who lived there and worked for the king.
013	1CH	004	024	The sons of Simeon: Nemuel, Jamin, Jarib, Zerah, and Shaul.
013	1CH	004	025	Shallum was the son of Shaul, Mibsam his son, and Mishma his son.
013	1CH	004	026	The sons of Mishma: Hammuel his son, Zaccur his son, and Shimei his son.
013	1CH	004	027	Shimei had sixteen sons and six daughters, but his brothers did not have many children; so their tribe was not as large as that of Judah.
013	1CH	004	028	They lived in Beersheba, Moladah, Hazar Shual,
013	1CH	004	029	Bilhah, Ezem, Tolad,
013	1CH	004	030	Bethuel, Hormah, Ziklag,
013	1CH	004	031	Beth Marcaboth, Hazar Susim, Beth Biri, and Shaaraim. These were their towns until David became king.
013	1CH	004	032	They also lived in Etam, Ain, Rimmon, Token, and Ashan—a total of five towns,
013	1CH	004	033	along with all the surrounding villages as far as Baal. These were the places where they lived and they recorded their genealogy.
013	1CH	004	034	Meshobab, Jamlech, Joshah (son of Amaziah),
013	1CH	004	035	Joel, Jehu (son of Joshibiah, son of Seraiah, son of Asiel),
013	1CH	004	036	Elioenai, Jaakobah, Jeshohaiah, Asaiah, Adiel, Jesimiel, Benaiah,
013	1CH	004	037	and Ziza (son of Shiphi, son of Allon, son of Jedaiah, son of Shimri, son of Shemaiah).
013	1CH	004	038	These were the names of the leaders of their families whose lineage increased significantly.
013	1CH	004	039	They went far as the border of Gedor on the east side of the valley to look for pasture for their flocks.
013	1CH	004	040	They found good pastureland there, and the area was open, quiet, and peaceful, for those who used to live there were Ham's descendants.
013	1CH	004	041	In the time of Hezekiah, king of Judah, the leaders listed above by name came and attacked these descendants of Ham where they lived, along with the Meunites there and totally destroyed them, as is clear to this very day. Then they settled there, because there was pastureland for their flocks.
013	1CH	004	042	Some of these Simeonites invaded Mount Seir—five hundred men led by Pelatiah, Neariah, Rephaiah, and Uzziel, the sons of Ishi.
013	1CH	004	043	They destroyed the rest of the Amalekites who had escaped. They have lived there to this very day.
013	1CH	005	001	The sons of Reuben the firstborn of Israel. (Though he was the firstborn, his birthright was given to the sons of Joseph son of Israel because he had defiled his father's bed. That is why Reuben is not listed in the genealogy according to birthright,
013	1CH	005	002	and even though Judah became the strongest of his brothers and a ruler came from him, the birthright belonged to Joseph.)
013	1CH	005	003	The sons of Reuben the firstborn of Israel: Hanoch, Pallu, Hezron, and Carmi.
013	1CH	005	004	The sons of Joel: Shemaiah his son, Gog his son, Shimei his son,
013	1CH	005	005	Micah his son, Reaiah his son, Baal his son,
013	1CH	005	006	and Beerah his son, the one whom Tiglath-Pileser the king of Assyria took into exile. He (Beerah) was a leader of the Reubenites.
013	1CH	005	007	Beerah's relatives are, listed in their genealogical records by family: Jeiel (chief), Zechariah,
013	1CH	005	008	and Bela of Azaz, son of Shema, son of Joel. They lived from Aroer to Nebo and Baal Meon.
013	1CH	005	009	On the eastern side they expanded into the land right up to the edge of the desert that continues to the Euphrates River, because their flocks had grown so big in Gilead.
013	1CH	005	010	In the time of Saul they went to war against the Hagrites, defeating them. They took over the places where the Hagrites had lived in all the regions east of Gilead.
013	1CH	005	011	Next to them the descendants of Gad lived in Basha, all the way to Salecah.
013	1CH	005	012	Joel (chief), Shapham (second), and Janai and Shaphat, in Bashan.
013	1CH	005	013	Their relatives, according to family, were: Michael, Meshullam, Sheba, Jorai, Jacan, Zia, and Eber—a total of seven.
013	1CH	005	014	These were the sons of Abihail, son of Huri, son of Jaroah, son of Gilead, son of Michael, son of Jeshishai, son of Jahdo, son of Buz.
013	1CH	005	015	Ahi son of Abdiel, son of Guni, was their family chief.
013	1CH	005	016	They lived in Gilead, in Bashan and its towns, and throughout the pasturelands of Sharon all the way to their borders.
013	1CH	005	017	They were all recorded in the genealogy during the time of Jotham, king of Judah, and Jeroboam, king of Israel.
013	1CH	005	018	The tribe of Reuben, the tribe of Gadites, and the half-tribe of Manasseh had 44,760 battle-ready strong warriors capable of using shields and swords and bows.
013	1CH	005	019	They went to war against the Hagrites, Jetur, Naphish, and Nodab.
013	1CH	005	020	They received help in fighting these enemies because they called out to God during the battles. In this way they were able to defeat the Hagrites and all who were with them. God answered their prayers because they trusted in him.
013	1CH	005	021	They captured their enemies' livestock—fifty thousand camels, two hundred fifty thousand sheep, and two thousand donkeys. They also captured one hundred thousand people,
013	1CH	005	022	and many others were killed because the battle belonged to God. They took over the land and lived there until the exile.
013	1CH	005	023	The half-tribe of Manasseh had grown very large. They lived in the land from Bashan to Baal Hermon (otherwise known as Senir and Mount Hermon).
013	1CH	005	024	These were the family heads: Epher, Ishi, Eliel, Azriel, Jeremiah, Hodaviah, and Jahdiel. They were strong warriors, famous men, heads of their families.
013	1CH	005	025	But they were unfaithful to the God of their forefathers. They prostituted themselves by following the gods of the peoples of the land, those that God had destroyed before them.
013	1CH	005	026	So the God of Israel encouraged Pul, king of Assyria (otherwise known as Tiglath-Pileser king of Assyria), to invade the land. He took into exile the Reubenites, the Gadites and the half-tribe of Manasseh. He brought them to Halah, Habor, Hara, and the river of Gozan, where they remain to this very day.
013	1CH	006	001	The sons of Levi: Gershon, Kohath, and Merari.
013	1CH	006	002	The sons of Kohath: Amram, Izhar, Hebron, and Uzziel.
013	1CH	006	003	The children of Amram: Aaron, Moses, and Miriam. The sons of Aaron: Nadab, Abihu, Eleazar and Ithamar.
013	1CH	006	004	Eleazar was the father of Phinehas, Phinehas was the father of Abishua,
013	1CH	006	005	Abishua was the father of Bukki, Bukki was the father of Uzzi,
013	1CH	006	006	Uzzi was the father of Zerahiah, Zerahiah was the father of Meraioth,
013	1CH	006	007	Meraioth was the father of Amariah, Amariah was the father of Ahitub,
013	1CH	006	008	Ahitub was the father of Zadok, Zadok was the father of Ahimaaz,
013	1CH	006	009	Ahimaaz was the father of Azariah, Azariah was the father of Johanan,
013	1CH	006	010	Johanan was the father of Azariah (he was the one who was serving as priest when Solomon built the Temple in Jerusalem),
013	1CH	006	011	Azariah was the father of Amariah, Amariah was the father of Ahitub,
013	1CH	006	012	Ahitub was the father of Zadok, Zadok was the father of Shallum,
013	1CH	006	013	Shallum was the father of Hilkiah, Hilkiah was the father of Azariah,
013	1CH	006	014	Azariah was the father of Seraiah, and Seraiah was the father of Jehozadak.
013	1CH	006	015	Jehozadak was taken into captivity when the Lord used Nebuchadnezzar to send Judah and Jerusalem into exile.
013	1CH	006	016	The sons of Levi: Gershon, Kohath, and Merari.
013	1CH	006	017	These are the names of the sons of Gershon: Libni and Shimei.
013	1CH	006	018	The sons of Kohath: Amram, Izhar, Hebron, and Uzziel.
013	1CH	006	019	The sons of Merari: Mahli, and Mushi. These are the families of the Levites listed in order according to their fathers:
013	1CH	006	020	The descendants of Gershon: Libni his son, Jehath his son, Zimmah his son,
013	1CH	006	021	Joah his son, Iddo his son, Zerah his son, and Jeatherai his son.
013	1CH	006	022	The descendants of Kohath: Amminadab his son, Korah his son, Assir his son,
013	1CH	006	023	Elkanah his son, Ebiasaph his son, Assir his son,
013	1CH	006	024	Tahath his son, Uriel his son, Uzziah his son and Shaul his son.
013	1CH	006	025	The descendants of Elkanah: Amasai, Ahimoth,
013	1CH	006	026	Elkanah his son, Zophai his son, Nahath his son,
013	1CH	006	027	Eliab his son, Jeroham his son, Elkanah his son, and Samuel his son.
013	1CH	006	028	The sons of Samuel: Joel (firstborn) and Abijah (second).
013	1CH	006	029	The descendants of Merari: Mahli, Libni his son, Shimei his son, Uzzah his son,
013	1CH	006	030	Shimea his son, Haggiah his son, and Asaiah his son.
013	1CH	006	031	These are the musicians David appointed to direct the music in the house of the Lord once the Ark had been placed there.
013	1CH	006	032	They led out in the music and singing before the Tabernacle, the Tent of Meeting, until Solomon built the Lord's Temple in Jerusalem. They served following the regulations they had been given.
013	1CH	006	033	Here are the men who served, together with their sons: From the Kohathites: Heman, the singer, the son of Joel, the son of Samuel,
013	1CH	006	034	the son of Elkanah, the son of Jeroham, the son of Eliel, the son of Toah,
013	1CH	006	035	the son of Zuph, the son of Elkanah, the son of Mahath, the son of Amasai,
013	1CH	006	036	the son of Elkanah, the son of Joel, the son of Azariah, the son of Zephaniah,
013	1CH	006	037	the son of Tahath, the son of Assir, the son of Ebiasaph, the son of Korah,
013	1CH	006	038	the son of Izhar, the son of Kohath, the son of Levi, the son of Israel.
013	1CH	006	039	Asaph, Heman's relative, who served beside him on the right: Asaph son of Berekiah, the son of Shimea,
013	1CH	006	040	the son of Michael, the son of Baaseiah, the son of Malkijah,
013	1CH	006	041	the son of Ethni, the son of Zerah, the son of Adaiah,
013	1CH	006	042	the son of Ethan, the son of Zimmah, the son of Shimei,
013	1CH	006	043	the son of Jahath, the son of Gershon, the son of Levi.
013	1CH	006	044	To Heman's left served sons of Merari: Ethan son of Kishi, the son of Abdi, the son of Malluch,
013	1CH	006	045	the son of Hashabiah, the son of Amaziah, the son of Hilkiah,
013	1CH	006	046	the son of Amzi, the son of Bani, the son of Shemer,
013	1CH	006	047	the son of Mahli, the son of Mushi, the son of Merari, the son of Levi.
013	1CH	006	048	The other Levites carried out all the other functions in the Tabernacle, the house of God.
013	1CH	006	049	However, it was Aaron and his descendants who were the ones who gave offerings on the altar of burnt offering and on the altar of incense and did all the work in the Most Holy Place, making atonement for Israel according to everything Moses the servant of God had commanded.
013	1CH	006	050	The descendants of Aaron were: Eleazar his son, Phinehas his son, Abishua his son,
013	1CH	006	051	Bukki his son, Uzzi his son, Zerahiah his son,
013	1CH	006	052	Meraioth his son, Amariah his son, Ahitub his son,
013	1CH	006	053	Zadok his son, and Ahimaaz his son.
013	1CH	006	054	These were the places they were given to live as their territory allotted to descendants of Aaron, beginning with the Kohathite clan, because theirs was the first lot:
013	1CH	006	055	They received Hebron in Judah together with its surrounding pasturelands.
013	1CH	006	056	But the fields and villages near the town were given to Caleb son of Jephunneh.
013	1CH	006	057	So the descendants of Aaron received Hebron, a town of refuge, Libnah, Jattir, Eshtemoa,
013	1CH	006	058	Hilen, Debir,
013	1CH	006	059	Ashan, Juttah and Beth Shemesh, together with their pasturelands.
013	1CH	006	060	From the tribe of Benjamin they received Gibeon, Geba, Alemeth, and Anathoth, together with their pasturelands. They had a total of thirteen towns among their families.
013	1CH	006	061	The rest of Kohath's descendants received by lot ten towns from the half tribe of Manasseh.
013	1CH	006	062	The descendants of Gershon, by family, received thirteen towns from the tribes of Issachar, Asher, and Naphtali, and from the partial tribe of Manasseh in Bashan.
013	1CH	006	063	The descendants of Merari, by family, received twelve towns from the tribes of Reuben, Gad, and Zebulun.
013	1CH	006	064	So the people of Israel gave the Levites these towns and their pasturelands.
013	1CH	006	065	They allotted the towns already mentioned by name from the tribes of Judah, Simeon, and Benjamin.
013	1CH	006	066	Some of the Kohathite families received as their territory towns from the tribe of Ephraim.
013	1CH	006	067	They were given Shechem, a town of refuge, in the hill country of Ephraim, Gezer,
013	1CH	006	068	Jokmeam, Beth Horon,
013	1CH	006	069	Aijalon, and Gath Rimmon, together with their pasturelands.
013	1CH	006	070	From half the tribe of Manasseh the people of Israel gave Aner and Bileam, together with their pasturelands, to the rest of the Kohathite families.
013	1CH	006	071	The descendants of Gershon received the following. From the family of the half-tribe of Manasseh: Golan in Bashan, and Ashtaroth, together with their pasturelands;
013	1CH	006	072	from the tribe of Issachar: Kedesh, Daberath,
013	1CH	006	073	Ramoth, and Anem, together with their pasturelands;
013	1CH	006	074	from the tribe of Asher: Mashal, Abdon,
013	1CH	006	075	Hukok, and Rehob, together with their pasturelands;
013	1CH	006	076	and from the tribe of Naphtali: Kedesh in Galilee, Hammon, and Kiriathaim, together with their pasturelands.
013	1CH	006	077	The rest of the descendants of Merari received the following. From the tribe of Zebulun: Jokneam, Kartah, Rimmono, and Tabor, together with their pasturelands;
013	1CH	006	078	from the tribe of Reuben on the east side of the Jordan opposite Jericho: Bezer (in the desert), Jahzah,
013	1CH	006	079	Kedemoth, and Mephaath, together with their pasturelands;
013	1CH	006	080	and from the tribe of Gad: Ramoth in Gilead, Mahanaim,
013	1CH	006	081	Heshbon, and Jazer, together with their pasturelands.
013	1CH	007	001	The sons of Issachar: Tola, Puah, Jashub and Shimron—a total of four.
013	1CH	007	002	The sons of Tola: Uzzi, Rephaiah, Jeriel, Jahmai, Ibsam, and Samuel—leaders of their families. In the time of David, the descendants of Tola listed in their genealogy a total of 22,600 warriors.
013	1CH	007	003	The son of Uzzi: Izrahiah. The sons of Izrahiah: Michael, Obadiah, Joel, and Isshiah. All five were family heads.
013	1CH	007	004	They had many wives and sons and so in their genealogy they list 36,000 battle-ready fighting men.
013	1CH	007	005	The relatives who were warriors belonging to all the families of Issachar, according to their genealogy, were 87,000 in total.
013	1CH	007	006	Three sons of Benjamin: Bela, Beker, and Jediael.
013	1CH	007	007	The sons of Bela: Ezbon, Uzzi, Uzziel, Jerimoth, and Iri, leaders of their families—a total of five. They had 22,034 fighting men according to their genealogy.
013	1CH	007	008	The sons of Beker: Zemirah, Joash, Eliezer, Elioenai, Omri, Jeremoth, Abijah, Anathoth, and Alemeth. These were all the sons of Beker.
013	1CH	007	009	Their genealogy included the family heads and 20,200 fighting men.
013	1CH	007	010	The son of Jediael: Bilhan. The sons of Bilhan: Jeush, Benjamin, Ehud, Kenaanah, Zethan, Tarshish, and Ahishahar.
013	1CH	007	011	All these sons of Jediael were family heads. They had 17,200 battle-ready warriors.
013	1CH	007	012	Shuppim and Huppim were sons of Ir, and Hushim was the son of Aher.
013	1CH	007	013	The sons of Naphtali: Jahziel, Guni, Jezer, and Shallum—the descendants of Bilhah.
013	1CH	007	014	The sons of Manasseh: Asriel whose mother was his Aramean concubine. She was also the mother of Makir, the father of Gilead.
013	1CH	007	015	Makir found a wife for Huppim and a wife for Shuppim. His sister's name was Maacah. The second was called Zelophehad. She only had daughters.
013	1CH	007	016	Maacah, Makir's wife, had a son and called him Peresh. His brother was called Sheresh, and his sons were Ulam and Rakem.
013	1CH	007	017	The son of Ulam: Bedan. These were all the sons of Gilead, son of Makir, son of Manasseh.
013	1CH	007	018	His sister Hammoleketh was the mother of Ishhod, Abiezer, and Mahlah.
013	1CH	007	019	The sons of Shemida were: Ahian, Shechem, Likhi, and Aniam.
013	1CH	007	020	The descendants of Ephraim: Shuthelah, Bered his son, Tahath his son, Eleadah his son, Tahath his son,
013	1CH	007	021	Zabad his son, and Shuthelah his son. Ezer and Elead were killed by the men who were living in Gath when they went there to try and steal their livestock.
013	1CH	007	022	Their father Ephraim mourned for them a long time, and his relatives came to comfort him.
013	1CH	007	023	Then he slept with his wife again. She became pregnant and gave birth to a son, whom he named Beriah because of this family tragedy.
013	1CH	007	024	Sheerah, his daughter, founded Lower and Upper Beth Horon together with Uzzen Sheerah.
013	1CH	007	025	Rephah was his son, Resheph his son, Telah his son, Tahan his son,
013	1CH	007	026	Ladan his son, Ammihud his son, Elishama his son,
013	1CH	007	027	Nun his son and Joshua his son.
013	1CH	007	028	The land they owned and the places they lived included Bethel and nearby towns, from Naaran to the east to Gezer and its towns to the west, and Shechem and its towns up to Ayyah and its towns.
013	1CH	007	029	On the border with Manasseh were Beth Shan, Taanach, Megiddo, and Dor, along with their towns. These were the towns where the descendants of Joseph son of Israel lived.
013	1CH	007	030	The sons of Asher: Imnah, Ishvah, Ishvi, and Beriah. Their sister was Serah.
013	1CH	007	031	The sons of Beriah: Heber and Malkiel, the father of Birzaith.
013	1CH	007	032	Heber was the father of Japhlet, Shomer, and Hotham, and of their sister Shua.
013	1CH	007	033	The sons of Japhlet: Pasach, Bimhal, and Ashvath. These were all the sons of Japhlet.
013	1CH	007	034	The sons of Shomer: Ahi, Rohgah, Hubbah, and Aram.
013	1CH	007	035	The sons of his brother Helem: Zophah, Imna, Shelesh, and Amal.
013	1CH	007	036	The sons of Zophah: Suah, Harnepher, Shual, Beri, Imrah,
013	1CH	007	037	Bezer, Hod, Shamma, Shilshah, Ithran, and Beera.
013	1CH	007	038	The sons of Jether: Jephunneh, Pispah, and Ara.
013	1CH	007	039	The sons of Ulla: Arah, Hanniel, and Rizia.
013	1CH	007	040	These were all descendants of Asher—family heads, proven men, strong warriors, and great leaders. According to their genealogy, they had 26,000 battle-ready warriors.
013	1CH	008	001	Benjamin was the father of Bela (firstborn son), Ashbel (second), Aharah (third),
013	1CH	008	002	Nohah (fourth), and Rapha (fifth).
013	1CH	008	003	The sons of Bela were: Addar, Gera, Abihud,
013	1CH	008	004	Abishua, Naaman, Ahoah,
013	1CH	008	005	Gera, Shephuphan, and Huram.
013	1CH	008	006	These were the sons of Ehud, family heads living in Geba, and were exiled to Manahath:
013	1CH	008	007	Naaman, Ahijah, and Gera. Gera was the one who exiled them. He was the father of Uzza and Ahihud.
013	1CH	008	008	Shaharaim had sons in Moab after he divorced his wives Hushim and Baara.
013	1CH	008	009	He married Hodesh and had Jobab, Zibia, Mesha, Malcam,
013	1CH	008	010	Jeuz, Sakia, and Mirmah. These were all his sons, family heads.
013	1CH	008	011	He also had sons with Hushim: Abitub and Elpaal.
013	1CH	008	012	The sons of Elpaal: Eber, Misham, Shemed (he built Ono and Lod with its nearby towns),
013	1CH	008	013	and Beriah and Shema, who were family heads living in Aijalon and who drove away the people who lived in Gath.
013	1CH	008	014	Ahio, Shashak, Jeremoth,
013	1CH	008	015	Zebadiah, Arad, Eder,
013	1CH	008	016	Michael, Ishpah, and Joha were the sons of Beriah.
013	1CH	008	017	Zebadiah, Meshullam, Hizki, Heber,
013	1CH	008	018	Ishmerai, Izliah, and Jobab were the sons of Elpaal.
013	1CH	008	019	Jakim, Zicri, Zabdi,
013	1CH	008	020	Elienai, Zillethai, Eliel,
013	1CH	008	021	Adaiah, Beraiah, and Shimrath were the sons of Shimei.
013	1CH	008	022	Ishpan, Eber, Eliel,
013	1CH	008	023	Abdon, Zicri, Hanan,
013	1CH	008	024	Hananiah, Elam, Anthothijah,
013	1CH	008	025	Iphdeiah, and Penuel were the sons of Shashak.
013	1CH	008	026	Shamsherai, Shehariah, Athaliah,
013	1CH	008	027	Jaareshiah, Elijah, and Zicri were the sons of Jeroham.
013	1CH	008	028	All these were family heads, according to their genealogy. They lived in Jerusalem.
013	1CH	008	029	Jeiel founded Gibeon and lived there. His wife was called Maacah.
013	1CH	008	030	His firstborn son was Abdon, then Zur, Kish, Baal, Ner, Nadab,
013	1CH	008	031	Gedor, Ahio, Zeker,
013	1CH	008	032	and Mikloth. Mikloth was the father of Shimeah. They also lived near their relatives in Jerusalem.
013	1CH	008	033	Ner was the father of Kish, Kish was the father of Saul, and Saul was the father of Jonathan, Malki-Shua, Abinadab and Esh-Baal.
013	1CH	008	034	The son of Jonathan: Merib-Baal, who was the father of Micah.
013	1CH	008	035	The sons of Micah: Pithon, Melech, Tarea, and Ahaz.
013	1CH	008	036	Ahaz was the father of Jehoaddah, Jehoaddah was the father of Alemeth, Azmaveth, and Zimri, and Zimri was the father of Moza.
013	1CH	008	037	Moza was the father of Binea. Raphah was his son, Eleasah his son, and Azel his son.
013	1CH	008	038	Azel had six sons. These were their names: Azrikam, Bokeru, Ishmael, Sheariah, Obadiah and Hanan. These were all the sons of Azel.
013	1CH	008	039	The sons of his brother Eshek: Ulam (firstborn), Jeush (second), and Eliphelet (third).
013	1CH	008	040	The sons of Ulam were strong warriors and skilled archers. They had many sons and grandsons—a total of 150. All of them were the sons of Benjamin.
013	1CH	009	001	All of the people of Israel were recorded in the genealogies written down in the book of the kings of Israel. The people of Judah were taken away to captivity in Babylon because they had been unfaithful.
013	1CH	009	002	The first to return and reclaim their property and live in their towns were some Israelites, priests, Levites, and Temple servants.
013	1CH	009	003	Some from the tribes of Judah, Benjamin, Ephraim, and Manasseh returned to live in Jerusalem. They included:
013	1CH	009	004	Uthai son of Ammihud, son of Omri, son of Imri, son of Bani, a descendant of Perez, son of Judah.
013	1CH	009	005	From the Shilonites: Asaiah (firstborn) and his sons.
013	1CH	009	006	From the Zerahites: Jeuel and relatives totaled 690.
013	1CH	009	007	From the Benjamites: Sallu son of Meshullam, son of Hodaviah, son of Hassenuah;
013	1CH	009	008	Ibneiah son of Jeroham; Elah son of Uzzi, son of Micri; and Meshullam son of Shephatiah, son of Reuel, son of Ibnijah.
013	1CH	009	009	All of them were family heads as recorded in their genealogies, and they totaled 956.
013	1CH	009	010	From the priests: Jedaiah, Jehoiarib, Jakin,
013	1CH	009	011	Azariah son of Hilkiah, son of Meshullam, son of Zadok, son of Meraioth, son of Ahitub. (Azariah was the head official in charge of the house of God.)
013	1CH	009	012	Also Adaiah son of Jeroham, son of Pashhur, son of Malkijah, and Maasai son of Adiel, son of Jahzerah, son of Meshullam, son of Meshillemith, son of Immer.
013	1CH	009	013	The priests, who were family heads, totaled 1,760. They were strong and capable men who served in the house of God.
013	1CH	009	014	From the Levites: Shemaiah, son of Hasshub, son of Azrikam, son of Hashabiah, a descendant of Merari;
013	1CH	009	015	Bakbakkar, Heresh, Galal, and Mattaniah, son of Mica, son of Zicri, son of Asaph;
013	1CH	009	016	Obadiah son of Shemaiah, son of Galal, son of Jeduthun; and Berekiah son of Asa, son of Elkanah, who used to live in the villages of the Netophathites.
013	1CH	009	017	The gatekeepers of the Temple: Shallum, Akkub, Talmon, Ahiman and their relatives. Shallum was the chief gatekeeper.
013	1CH	009	018	They had the responsibility up until now of looking after the King's Gate on the east side. They were the gatekeepers of the camps of the Levites.
013	1CH	009	019	Shallum was the son of Kore, son of Ebiasaph, son of Korah. He and his relatives, the Korahites, were responsible for guarding the entrances to the sanctuary in the same way their fathers had been responsible for guarding the entrance to the tented house of the Lord.
013	1CH	009	020	Previously Phinehas son of Eleazar had been the leader of the gatekeepers. The Lord was with him.
013	1CH	009	021	Zechariah son of Meshelemiah was the gatekeeper at the entrance to the tent of meeting.
013	1CH	009	022	In total there were 212 chosen to be gatekeepers at the entrances. They recorded their genealogies in their home towns. David and Samuel the prophet had selected their forefathers for their faithfulness.
013	1CH	009	023	They and their descendants were responsible for guarding the entrance to the house of the Lord, also when it was a tent.
013	1CH	009	024	The gatekeepers were placed on four sides: east, west, north, and south.
013	1CH	009	025	Their relatives in their towns came every seven days at certain times to help them.
013	1CH	009	026	The four chief gatekeepers, who were Levites, were given the responsibility of looking after the rooms and treasuries in the house of God.
013	1CH	009	027	They spent the night around the house of God because they had to guard it and they had the key to unlock it in the morning.
013	1CH	009	028	Some of the gatekeepers were responsible for the articles that were used in worship services. They counted what was brought in and what was taken out.
013	1CH	009	029	Others were given the job of looking after the furniture and equipment used in the sanctuary, as well as the special flour, wine, olive oil, incense, and spices.
013	1CH	009	030	(However, some of the priests were in charge of mixing the spices.)
013	1CH	009	031	Mattithiah, a Levite, who was the firstborn son of Shallum the Korahite, was given the responsibility of baking the bread used in offerings.
013	1CH	009	032	Other Kohathites were in charge of preparing the bread set out on the table every Sabbath.
013	1CH	009	033	The musicians, heads of Levite families, lived in the rooms in the Temple and were not required to carry out other duties because they were on duty day and night.
013	1CH	009	034	These were all heads of Levite families, leaders as recorded in their genealogies, and they lived in Jerusalem.
013	1CH	009	035	Jeiel was the father of Gibeon and he lived in Gibeon. His wife's name was Maacah.
013	1CH	009	036	His firstborn son was Abdon, then Zur, Kish, Baal, Ner, Nadab,
013	1CH	009	037	Gedor, Ahio, Zechariah, and Mikloth.
013	1CH	009	038	Mikloth was the father of Shimeam. They also lived near their relatives in Jerusalem.
013	1CH	009	039	Ner was the father of Kish, Kish was the father of Saul, and Saul was the father of Jonathan, Malki-Shua, Abinadab, and Esh-Baal.
013	1CH	009	040	The son of Jonathan: Merib-Baal, who was the father of Micah.
013	1CH	009	041	The sons of Micah: Pithon, Melech, Tahrea, and Ahaz.
013	1CH	009	042	Ahaz was the father of Jadah, Jadah was the father of Alemeth, Azmaveth, and Zimri, and Zimri was the father of Moza.
013	1CH	009	043	Moza was the father of Binea; Rephaiah was his son, Eleasah his son, and Azel his son.
013	1CH	009	044	Azel had six sons. Their names were: Azrikam, Bokeru, Ishmael, Sheariah, Obadiah, and Hanan. These were the sons of Azel.
013	1CH	010	001	The Philistines attacked Israel and all the Israelites soldiers ran away from them. Many Israelites were cut down on Mount Gilboa.
013	1CH	010	002	The Philistines chased down Saul and his sons. They killed Saul's sons Jonathan, Abinadab, and Malchishua.
013	1CH	010	003	The battle raged intensely around Saul. The enemy archers saw where he was, and they wounded him.
013	1CH	010	004	Saul told his armor bearer, “Take out your sword and kill me before these heathen come and torment me.” But his armor bearer refused—he was too afraid to do it. So Saul took his own sword and fell on it.
013	1CH	010	005	When his armor bearer saw that Saul was dead, he also fell on his sword and died.
013	1CH	010	006	So Saul and three of his sons died there, along with his royal line.
013	1CH	010	007	When all the Israelites in the valley saw that their army had run away, and that Saul and his sons were dead, they abandoned their towns and they also ran away. The Philistines came and occupied them.
013	1CH	010	008	The following day, when the Philistines came to strip the dead, they discovered the bodies of Saul and his sons on Mount Gilboa.
013	1CH	010	009	They stripped him, cut off his head, and took his armor. Then they sent the news throughout the land of Philistia, to their idols and their people.
013	1CH	010	010	They put Saul's armor in the temple of their idols and fixed his head to the temple of Dagon.
013	1CH	010	011	However, when everyone in Jabesh-gilead heard everything the Philistines had done to Saul,
013	1CH	010	012	all their fighting men went and recovered the bodies of Saul and his sons. They brought them back and buried them under the large tree in Jabesh. Then they fasted for seven days.
013	1CH	010	013	Saul died because he was unfaithful to the Lord. He did not keep the Lord's commands, and he also went to consult a medium.
013	1CH	010	014	He did not consult the Lord, so the Lord put him to death and he handed over the kingship to David, son of Jesse.
013	1CH	011	001	All the Israelites gathered to meet with David in Hebron. They told him, “We are your flesh and blood.
013	1CH	011	002	In recent times, even though Saul was king, you were the real leader of Israel. The Lord your God told you, ‘You will be the shepherd of my people, and you will be the leader of my people Israel.’”
013	1CH	011	003	All the elders of Israel came to the king at Hebron, and David made a solemn agreement with them before the Lord. There they anointed David as king of Israel, as the Lord had promised through Samuel.
013	1CH	011	004	Then David and all the Israelites went to Jerusalem (formerly known as Jebus) where the Jebusites lived.
013	1CH	011	005	The Jebusites told David, “You will not enter here!” But David did capture the fortress of Zion, now known as the City of David.
013	1CH	011	006	David had said, “Whoever is first to attack the Jebusites will be my commander-in-chief.” Since Joab, son of Zeruiah, was the first, he became commander-in-chief.
013	1CH	011	007	David decided to live in the fortress, which is why they named it after him the City of David.
013	1CH	011	008	He built up the city all around it, from the Millo in a circuit all around, while Joab repaired the rest of the city.
013	1CH	011	009	David became more and more powerful, for the Lord Almighty was with him.
013	1CH	011	010	These were the leaders of David's powerful warriors who, along with all the Israelites, gave him strong support in becoming king, just as the Lord had promised would happen to Israel.
013	1CH	011	011	This is the list of the leading warriors who supported David: Jashobeam, son of Hachmoni, leader of the Three. Using his spear, he once killed 300 men in a single battle.
013	1CH	011	012	After him came Eleazar, son of Dodo the Ahohite, one of the Three leading warriors.
013	1CH	011	013	He was with David at Pas-dammin when the Philistines gathered for battle that took place in a field full of barley. The Israelite army ran away when the Philistines attacked,
013	1CH	011	014	but David and Eleazar took a stand in the middle of the field, defending their ground and killing the Philistines. The Lord saved them by giving them a great victory.
013	1CH	011	015	Another time, the Three, who were part of the Thirty leading warriors, went down to meet David when he was at the cave of Adullam. The Philistine army was camped in the valley of Rephaim.
013	1CH	011	016	At the time David was in the stronghold, and the Philistine garrison was in Bethlehem.
013	1CH	011	017	David was feeling really thirsty, and he said, “If only someone could bring me a drink of water from the well beside the entrance gate to Bethlehem!”
013	1CH	011	018	The Three broke through the Philistine defenses, took some water from the well at Bethelehem's gate, and brought it back to David. But David refused to drink it, and poured it out as an offering to the Lord.
013	1CH	011	019	“God forbid that I should do this!” he said. “It would be like drinking the blood of these men who risked their lives! They risked their lives to bring me a drink.” So he did not drink it. This is just some of the things the Three leading warriors did.
013	1CH	011	020	Abishai, Joab's brother, was leader of the second Three. Using his spear, he once killed 300 men, and became famous among the Three.
013	1CH	011	021	He was the most highly regarded of the Three and was their commander, though he was not one of the first Three.
013	1CH	011	022	Benaiah, son of Jehoiada, a strong warrior from Kabzeel, did many amazing things. He killed two sons of Ariel of Moab. He also went after a lion into a pit in the snow and killed it.
013	1CH	011	023	Another time he killed an Egyptian—a huge man who stood seven feet six inches tall. The Egyptian had a spear whose shaft was as thick as a weaver's rod. Benaiah attacked him with just a club, but he was able to grab the spear from the Egyptian's hand, and killed him with his own spear.
013	1CH	011	024	These were the kind of things Benaiah did that made him as famous as the Three leading warriors.
013	1CH	011	025	He was the most highly regarded of the Thirty, though he was not one of the Three. David put him in charge of his personal bodyguard.
013	1CH	011	026	Other leading warriors were: Asahel, Joab's brother; Elhanan, son of Dodo, from Bethlehem;
013	1CH	011	027	Shammoth the Harorite; Helez the Pelonite;
013	1CH	011	028	Ira, son of Ikkesh from Tekoa; Abiezer from Anathoth;
013	1CH	011	029	Sibbecai the Hushathite; Ilai the Ahohite;
013	1CH	011	030	Maharai of Netophah; Heled, son of Baanah of Netophah;
013	1CH	011	031	Ithai, son of Ribai from Gibeah of the Benjamites; Benaiah the Pirathonite;
013	1CH	011	032	Hurai from the valleys of Gaash; Abiel the Arbathite;
013	1CH	011	033	Azmaveth the Baharumite; Eliahba the Shaalbonite;
013	1CH	011	034	the sons of Hashem the Gizonite; Jonathan, son of Shagee the Hararite;
013	1CH	011	035	Ahiam, son of Sachar the Hararite; Eliphal, son of Ur;
013	1CH	011	036	Hepher the Mecherathite; Ahijah the Pelonite;
013	1CH	011	037	Hezro the Carmelite; Naarai, son of Ezbai;
013	1CH	011	038	Joel, Nathan's brother; Mibhar, son of Hagri;
013	1CH	011	039	Zelek the Ammonite; Naharai the Beerothite; Joab's armor-bearer, son of Zeruiah;
013	1CH	011	040	Ira the Ithrite; Gareb the Ithrite;
013	1CH	011	041	Uriah the Hittite; Zabad son of Ahlai;
013	1CH	011	042	Adina, son of Shiza the Reubenite, leader of the Reubenites, and the thirty who were with him;
013	1CH	011	043	Hanan, son of Maacah; Joshaphat the Mithnite;
013	1CH	011	044	Uzzia the Ashterathite; Shama and Jeiel, the sons of Hotham the Aroerite;
013	1CH	011	045	Jediael, son of Shimri, and his brother, Joha the Tizite;
013	1CH	011	046	Eliel the Mahavite; Jeribai and Joshaviah, the sons of Elnaam; Ithmah the Moabite;
013	1CH	011	047	Eliel; Obed; and Jaasiel the Mezobaite.
013	1CH	012	001	The following is a list of the men who joined David when he was at Ziklag, still hiding from Saul, son of Kish. They were some of the leading warriors who fought on David's side.
013	1CH	012	002	They were all skilled archers, and could shoot arrows or slingshots with their right or their left hands. They were relatives of Saul from the tribe of Benjamin.
013	1CH	012	003	Ahiezer was their leader, then Joash; the sons of Shemaah the Gibeathite; Jeziel and Pelet the sons of Azmaveth; Beracah; Jehu the Anathothite;
013	1CH	012	004	Ishmaiah the Gibeonite (a strong warrior among the Thirty, and leader over the Thirty); Jeremiah; Jahaziel; Johanan; Jozabad the Gederathite;
013	1CH	012	005	Eluzai; Jerimoth; Bealiah; Shemariah; Shephatiah the Haruphite;
013	1CH	012	006	Elkanah, Isshiah, Azarel, Joezer, and Jashobeam (they were Korahites);
013	1CH	012	007	and Joelah and Zebadiah, the sons of Jeroham from Gedor.
013	1CH	012	008	Some warriors from the tribe of Gad went over to David's side when he was at the stronghold in the desert. They were strong and experienced warriors, battle-hardened, experts in the use of shields and spears. Their faces looked as fierce as lions, and they ran as fast as gazelles in the mountains.
013	1CH	012	009	Ezer was the leader, Obadiah (second), Eliab (third),
013	1CH	012	010	Mishmannah (fourth), Jeremiah (fifth),
013	1CH	012	011	Attai (sixth), Eliel (seventh),
013	1CH	012	012	Johanan (eighth), Elzabad (ninth),
013	1CH	012	013	Jeremiah (tenth), Machbannai (eleventh).
013	1CH	012	014	These warriors from Gad were army officers. The least able of them was in charge of 100 men; the best was in charge of 1,000.
013	1CH	012	015	These were ones who crossed the Jordan River in the first month of the year when it overflows its banks. They chased out all the people living in the valley, both to the east and to the west.
013	1CH	012	016	Some others from the tribes of Benjamin and Judah also came to join David at the stronghold.
013	1CH	012	017	David went out to meet them and told them, “If you've come in peace to help me, we can be friends. But if you've come to betray me to my enemies, even though I've done no wrong, then may the God of our fathers see what you're doing and condemn you.”
013	1CH	012	018	Then the Spirit came upon Amasai, the leader of the Thirty. “We are yours, David, and we are with you, son of Jesse! May peace, prosperity, and success be yours and those who help you, for God is the one who is helping you.” So David allowed them to join him, and put them in charge of his bands of raiders.
013	1CH	012	019	Others came over to David's side from the tribe of Manasseh and joined him when he went with Philistines to attack Saul. However, the Philistine rulers eventually decided to send them away, saying to themselves, “It will cost us our heads if he deserts us and goes over to his master Saul.”
013	1CH	012	020	The following is a list of the men from Manasseh who went over to David's side as he returned to Ziklag: Adnah, Jozabad, Jediael, Michael, Jozabad, Elihu, and Zillethai, leaders of thousands in Manasseh.
013	1CH	012	021	They helped David against raiders for they were all strong and experienced warriors and commanders in the army.
013	1CH	012	022	Men arrived daily to help David until he had a large army, like the army of God.
013	1CH	012	023	This is a list of the numbers of armed warriors who came and joined David in Hebron to turn over Saul's kingdom to him, as the Lord had said.
013	1CH	012	024	From the tribe of Judah, 6,800 warriors carrying shields and spears.
013	1CH	012	025	From the tribe of Simeon, 7,100 strong warriors.
013	1CH	012	026	From the tribe of Levi, 4,600,
013	1CH	012	027	including Jehoiada, leader of the family of Aaron, and with him 3,700,
013	1CH	012	028	and Zadok, a strong young warrior, with 22 members of his family, all officers.
013	1CH	012	029	From the tribe of Benjamin, from among Saul's relatives, 3,000, most of whom had remained loyal to Saul up until this time.
013	1CH	012	030	From the tribe of Ephraim, 20,800 strong warriors, each highly regarded in his own clan.
013	1CH	012	031	From the half-tribe of Manasseh, 18,000 men were designated by name to come and make David king.
013	1CH	012	032	From the tribe of Issachar came leaders who knew and could understand the signs of the times and what Israel should do—a total of 200 leaders of the tribe together with their relatives.
013	1CH	012	033	From the tribe of Zebulun, 50,000 warriors. They were fully armed and battle-ready, and totally dedicated.
013	1CH	012	034	From the tribe of Naphtali, 1,000 officers and 37,000 warriors carrying shields and spears.
013	1CH	012	035	From the tribe of Dan, 28,600 warriors, all battle-ready.
013	1CH	012	036	From the tribe of Asher, 40,000 experienced warriors, all battle-ready.
013	1CH	012	037	From the east side of the Jordan River, from the tribes of Reuben, Gad, and the half-tribe of Manasseh, 120,000 warriors carrying all kinds of weapons.
013	1CH	012	038	All these men came to Hebron dressed for battle, completely committed to making David king. All of Israel agreed that David should become king.
013	1CH	012	039	They stayed three days there, eating and drinking together, for their relatives had provided them with supplies.
013	1CH	012	040	Their neighbors, even as far away as Issachar, Zebulun, and Naphtali, arrived bringing food on donkeys, camels, mules, and oxen. They had plenty of flour, fig cakes, bunches of raisins, wine, olive oil, cattle, and sheep, for Israel was so happy.
013	1CH	013	001	David had discussions with all his leaders, including the army commanders of thousands and hundreds.
013	1CH	013	002	Then he addressed the whole assembly of Israel, saying, “If you agree, and if God approves, let us send an invitation to every Israelite in the land, including the priests and Levites in their towns and pastures, to come and join us.
013	1CH	013	003	Let us bring the Ark of our God back to us, because we forgot about it during the time of Saul.”
013	1CH	013	004	The whole assembly was pleased with the proposal, agreeing that this would be a good thing to do.
013	1CH	013	005	So David summoned all of Israel, from the Shihor River of Egypt up to Lebo-hamath, to help bring the Ark from Kiriath-jearim.
013	1CH	013	006	So David and all of Israel went to Baalah (otherwise called Kiriath-jearim) in Judah to bring back the Ark of God the Lord, whose throne is between the cherubim and who is called by the Name.
013	1CH	013	007	They loaded the Ark of God onto a brand new cart and brought it from Adinadab's house, with Uzzah and Ahio directing it.
013	1CH	013	008	David and all of Israel were celebrating before the Lord as loudly as possible, singing songs and playing music on lyres, harps, tambourines, cymbals, and trumpets.
013	1CH	013	009	But when they came to the threshing floor of Chidon, the oxen stumbled and Uzzah stretched out his hand to stop the Ark falling.
013	1CH	013	010	The Lord was angry with Uzzah for daring to touch the Ark like this so he struck him down, and Uzzah died there before the Lord.
013	1CH	013	011	David became angry with the Lord for his violent outburst against Uzzah. He named the place Perez-uzzah, and it is still called that today.
013	1CH	013	012	David became afraid of God that day. “How can I ever bring back the Ark of God home to me?” he asked.
013	1CH	013	013	So David did not move the Ark of God to be with him in the City of David. Instead, he had it taken to the home of Obed-edom the Gittite.
013	1CH	013	014	The Ark of God remained in Obed-edom's home for three months, and the Lord blessed Obed-edom's household and all that he had.
013	1CH	014	001	Then Hiram, king of Tyre, sent messengers to David along with cedar timber, stonemasons, and carpenters to build him a palace.
013	1CH	014	002	In this way David realized that the Lord had placed him on the throne as king of Israel and had blessed him by supporting his kingdom for the sake of the Lord's people Israel.
013	1CH	014	003	David married more wives in Jerusalem, and had more sons and daughters.
013	1CH	014	004	This is a list of the names of the children he had in Jerusalem: Shammua, Shobab, Nathan, Solomon,
013	1CH	014	005	Ibhar, Elishua, Elpelet,
013	1CH	014	006	Nogah, Nepheg, Japhia,
013	1CH	014	007	Elishama, Beeliada, and Eliphelet.
013	1CH	014	008	When the Philistines heard that David had been anointed king over all of Israel, they gathered their whole army to go after him. But David heard they were coming and went out to confront them.
013	1CH	014	009	The Philistines arrived and raided the valley of Rephaim.
013	1CH	014	010	David consulted God, asking “Shall I go and attack the Philistines? Will you make me victorious over them?” “Go ahead,” the Lord told him, “I will make you victorious over them.”
013	1CH	014	011	So David attacked and defeated them there at Baal-perazim. “God used me to defeat my enemies like a torrent of water that bursts out,” he declared. That's why the place was named Baal-perazim.
013	1CH	014	012	The Philistines had left their gods behind, so David gave orders that they should be burned.
013	1CH	014	013	However, the Philistines returned and made another raid on the valley.
013	1CH	014	014	David consulted God again. “Don't make a frontal attack,” God told him. “Instead go around behind them and attack them in front of the balsam trees.
013	1CH	014	015	As soon as you hear the sound of marching in the tops of the balsam trees, go and attack for the Lord has gone before you to strike down the Philistine army.”
013	1CH	014	016	So David did what God told him, striking down the Philistine army all the way from Gibeon to Gezer.
013	1CH	014	017	As a result David's reputation was spread everywhere, and the Lord made all the nations afraid of David.
013	1CH	015	001	Once David had finished building houses for himself in the City of David, he made a place for the Ark of God and set up a tent there.
013	1CH	015	002	Then he gave orders: “Nobody is to carry the Ark of God except the Levites, for the Lord himself chose them to carry the Ark of the Lord and to serve him forever.”
013	1CH	015	003	David summoned all of Israel to Jerusalem to bring the Ark of the Lord to the place he'd prepared for it.
013	1CH	015	004	This is the list of the Levites, the sons of Aaron, that David called to attend:
013	1CH	015	005	From the sons of Kohath, Uriel (chief), and 120 of his relatives;
013	1CH	015	006	from the sons of Merari, Asaiah (chief), with 220 of his relatives;
013	1CH	015	007	from the sons of Gershom, Joel (chief), with 130 of his relatives;
