014	2CH	008	012	Then Solomon presented burnt offerings to the Lord on the altar of the Lord he had built in front of the Temple's porch.
014	2CH	008	013	He followed the requirement for daily offerings as Moses had ordered for Sabbaths, new moons, and the three annual festivals—the Festival of Unleavened Bread, the Festival of Weeks, and the Festival of Shelters.
014	2CH	008	014	Following the instructions of his father David, he assigned the divisions of the priests for their service, and the Levites in their responsibilities to offer praise, and to help the priests in their daily duties. He also assigned gatekeepers by their divisions at each gate as David, the man of God, had instructed.
014	2CH	008	015	They followed David's instructions exactly regarding the priests, the Levites, and anything to do with the treasuries.
014	2CH	008	016	This is how all Solomon's work was carried out, from the day the foundation was laid for the Lord's Temple until it was finished. So the Lord's Temple was completed.
014	2CH	008	017	After this Solomon went to Ezion-geber and to Eloth on the coast of the land of Edom.
014	2CH	008	018	Hiram sent him ships under the command of his own officers, along with experienced sailors. They went with Solomon's men to Ophir where they loaded 450 talents of gold, which they then brought back to King Solomon.
014	2CH	009	001	The queen of Sheba heard how famous Solomon was, so she came to Jerusalem to test him with tough questions. She brought with her a very large entourage, with camels loaded with spices, large amounts of gold, and precious gemstones. She came to Solomon and asked him about everything she had on her mind.
014	2CH	009	002	Solomon answered all her questions. There was nothing he couldn't explain to her.
014	2CH	009	003	When the queen of Sheba saw Solomon's wisdom, and the palace he had built,
014	2CH	009	004	the food on the table, how his officials lived, how his servants operated and how they were dressed, the clothes of the waiters, and the burnt offerings he presented at the Lord's Temple, she was so astonished she could hardly breathe.
014	2CH	009	005	She told the king, “It's true what I heard in my own country about your proverbs and your wisdom!
014	2CH	009	006	But I didn't believe what they told me until I came and saw with my own eyes. In fact, I wasn't told the half of it—the extent of your wisdom far exceeds what I heard!
014	2CH	009	007	How happy your people must be! How happy those who work for you, who stand here every day listening to your wisdom!
014	2CH	009	008	Praise the Lord your God who is so pleased with you, who placed you on his throne as king to rule on his behalf. Because of the love of your God for Israel he has made them secure forever, and he has made you king over them to do what is fair and right.”
014	2CH	009	009	She presented the king with one hundred and twenty talents of gold, huge amounts of spices and precious stones. Never before had there been spices like those the queen of Sheba gave to King Solomon.
014	2CH	009	010	(Hiram and Solomon's men, who brought gold from Ophir, also brought algum wood and precious stones.
014	2CH	009	011	The king used the algum wood to make steps for the Temple and for the royal palace, and into lyres and harps for the musicians. Nothing like them had ever been seen before in the land of Judah.)
014	2CH	009	012	King Solomon gave the queen of Sheba all she wanted, whatever she asked for. This was far more than she had brought the king. Then she and her attendants returned home to her own country.
014	2CH	009	013	The weight of gold that Solomon received each year was 666 talents,
014	2CH	009	014	not including that received from traders and merchants. All the kings of Arabia and governors of the land also brought gold and silver to Solomon.
014	2CH	009	015	King Solomon made two hundred shields of hammered gold. Each shield required six hundred shekels of hammered gold.
014	2CH	009	016	He also made three hundred small shields of hammered gold. Each of these shields required three hundred gold coins. The king placed them in the Palace of the Forest of Lebanon.
014	2CH	009	017	The king also made a great throne of ivory, and covered it with pure gold.
014	2CH	009	018	The throne had six steps, with a golden footstool attached. There were armrests on both sides of the seat, with lions standing beside the armrests.
014	2CH	009	019	Twelve lions stood on the six steps, one on opposite ends of each step. Nothing like this had ever been made for any kingdom.
014	2CH	009	020	All of King Solomon's drinking cups were gold, and all the utensils of the Palace of the Forest of Lebanon were pure gold. No silver was used, because it was not valued in the days of Solomon.
014	2CH	009	021	The king had a fleet of ships from Tarshish crewed by Hiram's sailors. Once every three years the ships of Tarshish would arrive with a cargo of gold, silver, ivory, apes, and peacocks.
014	2CH	009	022	King Solomon was greater than any other king on earth in wealth and wisdom.
014	2CH	009	023	All the kings of the earth wanted to meet Solomon to hear the wisdom that God had placed in his mind.
014	2CH	009	024	Year after year, every visitor would bring gifts—articles of silver and gold, clothes, weapons, spices, horses, and mules.
014	2CH	009	025	Solomon had four thousand stalls for horses and chariots, and twelve thousand horsemen. He kept them in the chariot towns, and also with him in Jerusalem.
014	2CH	009	026	He ruled over all the kings from the Euphrates River to the land of the Philistines, and to the border with Egypt.
014	2CH	009	027	The king made silver as plentiful in Jerusalem as stones, and cedar wood as plentiful as sycamore-figs in the foothills.
014	2CH	009	028	Solomon's horses were imported from Egypt and from many other lands.
014	2CH	009	029	The rest of the acts of Solomon, from start to finish, are written in the Records of Nathan the Prophet, in the Prophecy of Ahijah the Shilonite, and in the Visions of Iddo the Seer about Jeroboam, son of Nebat.
014	2CH	009	030	Solomon ruled in Jerusalem over all of Israel for forty years.
014	2CH	009	031	Then Solomon died and was buried in the city of his father David. His son Rehoboam took over as king.
014	2CH	010	001	Rehoboam went to Shechem, because all the Israelites had gone to Shechem to make him king.
014	2CH	010	002	Jeroboam, son of Nebat, was still in Egypt when he heard about this. (He had run away to Egypt to escape from King Solomon and was living there.)
014	2CH	010	003	The Israelite leaders sent for him. Jereboam and all the Israelites went to talk with Rehoboam.
014	2CH	010	004	“Your father placed a heavy burden on us,” they told him. “But now if you lighten the load your father imposed and the heavy demands he laid on us, we will serve you.”
014	2CH	010	005	Rehoboam answered, “Come back in three days time.” So the people left.
014	2CH	010	006	King Rehoboam asked for advice from the elders who had served his father Solomon when he was alive. “How do you advise me to reply to these people about this?” he asked.
014	2CH	010	007	They replied, “If you treat these people well, and please them by speaking kindly to them, they will always serve you.”
014	2CH	010	008	But Rehoboam dismissed the advice of the elders. He instead asked advice from the young men he had grown up with, those close to him.
014	2CH	010	009	He asked them, “What response do you advise that we send back to these people who have told me, ‘Lighten the burden your father put on us’?”
014	2CH	010	010	The young men who he had grown up with told him, “This is what you have to tell these people who said to you, ‘Your father made our burden heavy, but you should make it lighter.’ This is what you should answer them: ‘My little finger is thicker than my father's waist!
014	2CH	010	011	My father placed a heavy burden on you, and I will make it even heavier. My father punished you with whips; I will punish you with scorpions.’”
014	2CH	010	012	Three days later, Jeroboam and all the people returned to Rehoboam, because the king had told them, “Come back in three days time.”
014	2CH	010	013	The king answered them sharply. Dismissing the advice of the elders,
014	2CH	010	014	he replied using the advice of the young men. He said, “My father placed a heavy burden on you, and I will make it even heavier. My father punished you with whips; I will punish you with scorpions.”
014	2CH	010	015	The king did not listen to what the people said, for this change in circumstances was from God, to fulfill what the Lord had told Jeroboam son of Nebat through Ahijah the Shilonite.
014	2CH	010	016	When all the Israelites saw that the king wasn't listening to them, they told the king: “What share do we have in David, and what part do we have in the son of Jesse? Go home, Israel! You're on your own, house of David!” So all the Israelites went home.
014	2CH	010	017	However, Rehoboam still ruled over the Israelites who lived in Judah.
014	2CH	010	018	Then King Rehoboam sent out Hadoram, who was in charge of forced labor, but the Israelites stoned him to death. King Rehoboam quickly jumped into his chariot and raced back to Jerusalem.
014	2CH	010	019	As a result, Israel has been in rebellion against the house of David to this day.
014	2CH	011	001	When Rehoboam arrived in Jerusalem, he gathered the men from the households of Judah and Benjamin— 180,000 chosen warriors—to go and fight against Israel to bring the kingdom back to Rehoboam.
014	2CH	011	002	But a message from the Lord came to Shemaiah the man of God that said,
014	2CH	011	003	“Tell Rehoboam, son of Solomon, king of Judah, and all the Israelites living in Judah and Benjamin:
014	2CH	011	004	‘This is what the Lord says: Don't fight against your relatives. Every one of you, go home! For what has happened is down to me.’” So they obeyed what the Lord told them and did not fight against Jeroboam.
014	2CH	011	005	Rehoboam stayed in Jerusalem, and he strengthened the defenses of the towns in Judah.
014	2CH	011	006	He built up Bethlehem, Etam, Tekoa,
014	2CH	011	007	Beth-zur, Soco, Adullam,
014	2CH	011	008	Gath, Mareshah, Ziph,
014	2CH	011	009	Adoraim, Lachish, Azekah,
014	2CH	011	010	Zorah, Aijalon and Hebron. These are the fortified towns in Judah and in Benjamin.
014	2CH	011	011	He strengthened their fortresses and put commanders in charge of them, together with supplies of food, olive oil, and wine.
014	2CH	011	012	He stored shields and spears in all the towns and made them very strong. So he held Judah and Benjamin under his rule.
014	2CH	011	013	The priests and Levites throughout Israel chose to side with Rehoboam.
014	2CH	011	014	The Levites even left their pasturelands and properties behind, and came to Judah and Jerusalem, because Jeroboam and his sons refused to allow them to serve as priests of the Lord.
014	2CH	011	015	Jeroboam chose his own priests for the high places and for the goat and calf idols he had made.
014	2CH	011	016	Those from every tribe of Israel who were committed to worshiping their God followed the Levites to Jerusalem to sacrifice to the Lord, the God of their forefathers.
014	2CH	011	017	So they supported the kingdom of Judah and for three years they were loyal to Rehoboam, son of Solomon, because they followed the way of David and Solomon.
014	2CH	011	018	Rehoboam married Mahalath, who was the daughter of David's son Jerimoth and of Abihail, the daughter of Eliab, son of Jesse.
014	2CH	011	019	She was the mother of his sons Jeush, and Shamariah, and Zaham.
014	2CH	011	020	After her he married Maacah Absalom's daughter, and she was the mother of his sons Abijah, Attai, Ziza, and Shelomith.
014	2CH	011	021	Rehoboam loved Maacah Absalom's daughter more than all his other wives and concubines. He had a total of eighteen wives and sixty concubines, twenty-eight sons and sixty daughters.
014	2CH	011	022	Rehoboam made Abijah son of Maacah crown prince among his brothers, planning to make him king.
014	2CH	011	023	Rehoboam was also wise to place some of his sons throughout the land of Judah and Benjamin, and to all the fortified towns. He gave them plenty of supplies and sought many wives for them. He worked to arrange many wives for them.
014	2CH	012	001	Once Rehoboam was secure on the throne and was sure of his power, he together with all the Israelites abandoned the law of the Lord.
014	2CH	012	002	In the fifth year of Rehoboam's reign, Shishak, king of Egypt, came and attacked Jerusalem because they had been unfaithful to God.
014	2CH	012	003	He came with 1,200 chariots, 60,000 horsemen, and an army from Egypt that couldn't be counted—Libyans, Sukkites, and Cushites.
014	2CH	012	004	He conquered the fortified towns of Judah and then approached Jerusalem.
014	2CH	012	005	Shemaiah the prophet came to Rehoboam and the leaders of Judah who had run for safety to Jerusalem because of Shishak. He told them, “This is what the Lord says: ‘You have abandoned me, so I have abandoned you to Shishak.’”
014	2CH	012	006	The leaders of Israel and the king admitted they were wrong and said, “The Lord is right.”
014	2CH	012	007	When the Lord saw that they had repented, he sent a message to Shemaiah, saying, “They have repented. I won't destroy them, and I will soon save them. My anger won't be poured out on Jerusalem through Shishak.
014	2CH	012	008	Even so they will become his subjects, so that they can learn the difference between serving me and serving the kings of earth.”
014	2CH	012	009	King Shishak of Egypt attacked Jerusalem and took the treasures of the Lord's Temple and the treasures of the royal palace. He took away everything, including the gold shields that Solomon had made.
014	2CH	012	010	Later Rehoboam replaced them with bronze shields and gave them to be looked after by the commanders of the guard stationed at the entrance to the royal palace.
014	2CH	012	011	Whenever the king would enter the Temple of the Lord the guards would go with him, carrying the shields, and then take them back to the guardroom.
014	2CH	012	012	Because Rehoboam repented, the anger of the Lord did not fall on him, and the Lord did not destroy him completely. Things went well in Judah.
014	2CH	012	013	King Rehoboam became powerful in Jerusalem. He was forty-one when he became king, and he reigned seventeen years in Jerusalem, the city the Lord had chosen from all the tribes of Israel where he would be honored. The name of his mother was Naamah the Ammonite.
014	2CH	012	014	But Rehoboam did what was evil because he did not commit himself to following the Lord.
014	2CH	012	015	What Rehoboam did, from beginning to end, is written down in the records of Shemaiah the Prophet and of Iddo the Seer dealing with genealogies. However, Rehoboam and Jeroboam were always at war with each other.
014	2CH	012	016	Rehoboam died and was buried in the City of David. His son Abijah took over as king.
014	2CH	013	001	Abijah became king of Judah in the eighteenth year of the reign of Jeroboam.
014	2CH	013	002	He reigned in Jerusalem for three years. His mother's name was Micaiah, daughter of Uriel—she was from Gibeah. Abijah and Jeroboam were at war.
014	2CH	013	003	Abijah went out to fight with an army of 400,000 brave warriors, while Jeroboam opposed him with his army of 800,000 chosen warriors of great strength.
014	2CH	013	004	Abijah stood on Mount Zemaraim, in the hill country of Ephraim, and said, “Listen to me, Jeroboam and all of Israel!
014	2CH	013	005	Don't you understand that the Lord, the God of Israel, gave the kingdom of Israel to David and his descendants forever by a binding agreement?
014	2CH	013	006	Yet Jeroboam, son of Nebat, just a servant of Solomon, son of David, had the audacity to rebel against his master.
014	2CH	013	007	Then some good-for-nothing evil men gathered round him and defied Rehoboam, son of Solomon, when he was young and inexperienced, and couldn't confront them.
014	2CH	013	008	Now do you really think you can oppose the kingdom of the Lord, held by David's descendants? You may be a large horde, and have with you the golden calves that Jeroboam made for you as gods.
014	2CH	013	009	But didn't you drive out the priests of the Lord, Aaron's descendants, and the Levites, and make priests for yourselves just like people in other nations do? Now anyone who wants to can come and dedicate himself, sacrificing a young bull and seven rams, and he can become a priest of things that really are not gods.
014	2CH	013	010	But for us, the Lord is our God! We have not abandoned him. We have priests serving the Lord who are descendants of Aaron, and we have Levites who help them in their ministry.
014	2CH	013	011	Morning and evening they present burnt offerings and burn fragrant incense to the Lord. They place the rows of showbread on the purified table, and light the lamps of the gold lampstand every evening. We are doing what the Lord our God told us to do, while you have abandoned him.
014	2CH	013	012	God is leading us! His priests blow their trumpets to go into battle against you. People of Israel, don't fight against the Lord, the God of your fathers, for you won't win!”
014	2CH	013	013	But Jeroboam had sent troops around to attack from the rear, so that while he and the main force was in front of Judah, the ambush was behind them.
014	2CH	013	014	Judah turned around and realized that they were having to fight front and rear. They cried out to the Lord for help. Then the priests blew their trumpets,
014	2CH	013	015	and the men of Judah gave a loud shout. When they shouted, God struck Jeroboam and all Israel in front of Abijah and Judah.
014	2CH	013	016	The Israelites ran away from Judah, and God handed them over to Judah, defeated.
014	2CH	013	017	Abijah and his men hit them hard, and 500,000 of Israel's best warriors were killed.
014	2CH	013	018	So the Israelites were subdued at that time, and the people of Judah were victorious because they relied on the Lord, the God of their forefathers.
014	2CH	013	019	Abijah chased Jeroboam and captured some towns from him: Bethel, Jeshanah, and Ephron, along with their villages.
014	2CH	013	020	Jereboam never regained his power during Abijah's reign. Eventually the Lord struck him down and he died.
014	2CH	013	021	But Abijah grew stronger and stronger. He married fourteen wives and had twenty-two sons and sixteen daughters.
014	2CH	013	022	The rest of what Abijah did—what he said and what he accomplished—is recorded in the history written by Iddo the Prophet.
014	2CH	014	001	Abijah died and was buried in the City of David. His son Asa took over as king. For ten years of his reign the country was at peace.
014	2CH	014	002	Asa did what was good and right in the Lord's sight.
014	2CH	014	003	He took down the foreign altars and high places, smashed their sacred pillars, and cut down the Asherah poles.
014	2CH	014	004	He ordered Judah to worship the Lord, the God of their forefathers, and to observe the law and the commandments.
014	2CH	014	005	He also took down the high places and the incense altars from all the towns of Judah. Under his rule the kingdom was at peace.
014	2CH	014	006	Because the country was at peace he was able to rebuild the fortified towns of Judah. There were no wars during these years because the Lord had granted him peace.
014	2CH	014	007	Asa told the people of Judah, “Let us build up these towns and surround them with walls and towers and barred gates. The land is still ours, because we continue to worship the Lord our God. We worship him, and he has given us peace from all our enemies.” So they began the building projects, and completed them successfully.
014	2CH	014	008	Asa had an army made up of three hundred thousand men from Judah who carried large shields and spears, and two hundred eighty thousand men from Benjamin who carried regular shields and bows. All of them were brave warriors.
014	2CH	014	009	Zerah the Ethiopian attacked them with an army of a thousand times a thousand men and three hundred chariots, advancing as far as Mareshah.
014	2CH	014	010	Asa went out to confront him, lining up for battle in the Valley of Zephathah at Mareshah.
014	2CH	014	011	Asa called out for help to the Lord his God: “Lord, there is no one apart from you who can help the powerless against the powerful. Please help us, Lord our God, for we trust in you. We have come against this horde because we stand for you, Lord. You are our God. Do not let a mere human being beat you.”
014	2CH	014	012	The Lord struck the Ethiopians in front of Asa and Judah, and the Ethiopians ran away.
014	2CH	014	013	Asa and his army chased them as far as Gerar. The Ethiopians were killed—there were none who survived, for they were caught between the Lord and his army. The men of Judah carried off a large amount of plunder.
014	2CH	014	014	They also attacked all the towns around Gerar, because the inhabitants were terrified of the Lord. The men of Judah took a large amount of plunder from all the towns.
014	2CH	014	015	Then they attacked the camps of the herdsmen and took many sheep and camels. Then they went back to Jerusalem.
014	2CH	015	001	The Spirit of God came upon Azariah the son of Oded.
014	2CH	015	002	He went out to meet Asa and said to him, “Listen to me, Asa and all of Judah and Benjamin. The Lord is with you while you are with him. If you look for him, you will find him; but if you abandon him, he will abandon you.
014	2CH	015	003	For many years Israel was without the true God, without a priest to teach them, and without the law.
014	2CH	015	004	But when they were in trouble they returned to the Lord, the God of Israel—they looked for him, and they found him.
014	2CH	015	005	During those times travel was dangerous, for all the inhabitants of the lands were in great turmoil. People everywhere had terrible problems.
014	2CH	015	006	Nation fought nation, and town fought town, for God threw them into a panic with all kinds of trouble.
014	2CH	015	007	But you need to be strong, not weak, for you will be rewarded for the work you do.”
014	2CH	015	008	When Asa heard these words of prophecy from Azariah the prophet, son of Oded, he was encouraged. He removed the vile idols from the whole territory of Judah and Benjamin and from the towns he had captured in the hill country of Ephraim. Then he repaired the altar of the Lord that stood in front of the porch of the Lord's Temple.
014	2CH	015	009	Then Asa summoned all of Judah and Benjamin, along with those Israelites from the tribes of Ephraim, Manasseh, and Simeon who were living among them, for many people had deserted Israel and come over to Asa when they saw that the Lord his God was with him.
014	2CH	015	010	They gathered in Jerusalem in the third month of the fifteenth year of Asa's reign.
014	2CH	015	011	That day they sacrificed to the Lord seven hundred oxen and seven thousand sheep from the plunder they had brought back.
014	2CH	015	012	Then they made an agreement to conscientiously and completely follow the Lord, the God of their forefathers.
014	2CH	015	013	They also agreed that anyone who refused to follow the Lord, the God of Israel, would be put to death, whether young or old, man or woman.
014	2CH	015	014	They declared their oath with a loud shout, accompanied by trumpets and blasts from rams' horns.
014	2CH	015	015	The whole of Judah was happy at the oath they had conscientiously sworn. They looked for him sincerely, and they found him. The Lord gave them peace from all their enemies.
014	2CH	015	016	King Asa also removed Maacah from her position as queen mother for making an offensive Asherah pole. Asa cut down her vile idol, crushed it up, and burned it in the Kidron Valley.
014	2CH	015	017	While the high places were not removed from Israel, Asa was completely devoted to the Lord all his life.
014	2CH	015	018	He brought into God's Temple the silver and gold articles he and his father had dedicated.
014	2CH	015	019	There was no more war until the thirty-fifth year of Asa's reign.
014	2CH	016	001	In the thirty-sixth year of Asa's reign, Baasha, king of Israel, invaded Judah. He fortified Ramah to stop anyone coming from or going to Asa, king of Judah.
014	2CH	016	002	Asa took the silver and gold from the treasuries of the Lord's Temple and the king's palace and sent them to Ben-hadad, king of Syria, who lived in Damascus, with a message that said:
014	2CH	016	003	“Make an alliance between me and you like the one between my father and your father. Look at the silver and gold I've sent you. Go ahead and break your agreement with Baasha, king of Israel, so that he will leave me and go home.”
014	2CH	016	004	King Ben-hadad did as Asa had asked, and he sent his armies and their commanders to attack the towns of Israel. They conquered Ijon, Dan, Abel-maim, and all the storehouse towns of Naphtali.
014	2CH	016	005	When Baasha heard about it, he stopped fortifying Ramah and gave up his project.
014	2CH	016	006	So King Asa went with all the men of Judah, and they carried away from Ramah the stones and the timbers Baasha had used for building, and with them he built up Geba and Mizpah.
014	2CH	016	007	But right then Hanani the seer came to Asa, king of Judah, and told him, “Because you have put your trust in the king of Aram and have not put your trust in the Lord your God, your opportunity to destroy the army of the king of Aram has gone.
014	2CH	016	008	Didn't the Ethiopians and Libyans have a huge army with many chariots and horsemen? Yet because you trusted in the Lord, he made you victorious over them.
014	2CH	016	009	For the Lord looks all over the earth for the opportunity to show his power on behalf of those who are completely and sincerely devoted to him. You have acted stupidly in doing this. So from now on you will always be at war.”
014	2CH	016	010	Asa was angry with the seer. He was so angry with him over this that he put him in prison. At this same time Asa started to mistreat some of the people.
014	2CH	016	011	The rest of what Asa did, from beginning to end, is written down in the Book of the Kings of Judah and Israel.
014	2CH	016	012	In the thirty-ninth year of his reign, Asa had trouble with disease in his feet, which only became worse and worse. Yet even in his sickness he did not turn to the Lord, but only the physicians.
014	2CH	016	013	Asa died in the forty-first year of his reign.
014	2CH	016	014	He was buried in the tomb that he had prepared for himself in the City of David. They placed him on a bed full of spices, perfumed oils, and fragrances. Then they made a great fire to honor him.
014	2CH	017	001	Asa's son Jehoshaphat took over as king. He strengthened his country's defenses against Israel.
014	2CH	017	002	He assigned troops to every fortified city of Judah and placed garrisons throughout Judah and in the towns of Ephraim that his father Asa had captured.
014	2CH	017	003	The Lord supported Jehoshaphat because he followed the ways of his father David. He did not believe in the Baals,
014	2CH	017	004	but worshiped the God of his father and obeyed his commandments, unlike what the kingdom of Israel was doing.
014	2CH	017	005	So the Lord made Jehoshaphat's hold on the kingdom secure, and all the people of Judah paid their dues to him. As a result he became very wealthy and highly honored.
014	2CH	017	006	He was sincerely committed to what the Lord wanted. He also removed the high places and Asherah poles from Judah.
014	2CH	017	007	In the third year of his reign, Jehoshaphat sent his officials Ben-hail, Obadiah, Zechariah, Nethanel, and Micaiah to teach in the towns of Judah.
014	2CH	017	008	He sent along with the them Levites named Shemaiah, Nethaniah, Zebadiah, Asahel, Shemiramoth, Jehonathan, Adonijah, Tobijah, and Tobadonijah, and with them the priests Elishama and Jehoram.
014	2CH	017	009	Taking with them the Book of the Law of the Lord they taught as they went around Judah. They visited all the towns of Judah, teaching the people.
014	2CH	017	010	All the surrounding kingdoms were in awe of the Lord, so that they did not attack Jehoshaphat.
014	2CH	017	011	Some of the Philistines even brought him gifts and silver, while the Arabians brought him 7,700 rams and 7,700 goats.
014	2CH	017	012	Jehoshaphat grew increasingly powerful, and he built fortresses and storehouse towns in Judah.
014	2CH	017	013	He maintained a great deal of supplies in the towns of Judah. He also had troops, experienced warriors, in Jerusalem.
014	2CH	017	014	This is a count of them, according to their family lines: from Judah, the commanders of thousands: Adnah the commander, and 300,000 mighty warriors with him;
