018	JOB	024	006	They are forced to find what they can in other people's fields, to glean among the vineyards of the wicked.
018	JOB	024	007	They spend the night naked because they have no clothes; they have nothing to cover themselves against the cold.
018	JOB	024	008	They are soaked by the cold mountain storms, and huddle beside the rocks for shelter.
018	JOB	024	009	Fatherless children are snatched from their mother's breasts, taking the babies of the poor as security for a debt.
018	JOB	024	010	Because they have no clothes to wear they have to go naked, harvesting sheaves of grain while they themselves are hungry.
018	JOB	024	011	In the olive groves they work to produce oil, but do not taste it; they tread the winepress, but are thirsty.
018	JOB	024	012	In the city the dying groan, and the wounded cry for help, but God ignores their prayers.
018	JOB	024	013	These are people who rebel against the light. They do not want to know its ways, or to stay on its paths.
018	JOB	024	014	The murderer gets up at dawn to kill the poor and needy, and when night falls he becomes a thief.
018	JOB	024	015	The adulterer waits for dusk, saying to himself, ‘No one will see me now,’ and he covers his face.
018	JOB	024	016	Thieves break into houses during the night and they sleep during the day. They don't even know what the light is like!
018	JOB	024	017	Total darkness is like light to them, for they are familiar with the night.
018	JOB	024	018	Like bubbles on the surface of a river they are quickly carried away. The land they own is cursed by God. They don't enter their own vineyards.
