018	JOB	036	022	Look how much power God has! What teacher is like him?
018	JOB	036	023	Who has instructed him what to do? Who can say to him ‘You have done wrong’?
018	JOB	036	024	Instead you should praise him for what he has done, as people have done in song.
018	JOB	036	025	Everyone has seen God's creation, though only from a distance.
018	JOB	036	026	See how great God is—more than we can understand! No one can count his years.
018	JOB	036	027	He draws up the water and distils it into the dew and the rain.
018	JOB	036	028	The clouds pour down rain, falling plentifully on humankind.
018	JOB	036	029	Can anyone understand how the clouds spread out, or how thunder roars from where he lives?
018	JOB	036	030	See how he scatters lightning around him, and covers the depths of the sea in darkness.
018	JOB	036	031	By these actions he rules the people, he provides abundant food.
018	JOB	036	032	He holds lightning in his hands and commands where it should strike.
018	JOB	036	033	Thunder declares his presence—even cattle know when a storm is coming.”
018	JOB	037	001	“At this my heart trembles, beating rapidly within me!
018	JOB	037	002	Listen carefully to God's thunderous voice that rumbles as he speaks.
018	JOB	037	003	He sends it across the heaven; his lightning flashes to the ends of the earth.
018	JOB	037	004	Then comes the roar of thunder, his majestic voice holding nothing back when he speaks.
018	JOB	037	005	God's thunderous voice is wonderful! We can't comprehend the great things he does!
018	JOB	037	006	He tells the snow to fall and the rain to pour down on the earth.
018	JOB	037	007	By this he stops people working so that everyone can understand what he does.
018	JOB	037	008	Even the animals take shelter and remain in their dens.
018	JOB	037	009	The south wind blows in storms, while the north wind blows in cold weather.
018	JOB	037	010	God's breath produces ice, freezing the surface of water solid.
018	JOB	037	011	He fills the clouds with moisture; he scatters his lightning from them.
018	JOB	037	012	They swirl around under his control; they move over all the earth as he commands.
018	JOB	037	013	He does this to accomplish his will, either to discipline or to show his goodness.
018	JOB	037	014	Listen to this, Job! Stop for a moment and consider the wonderful things God does.
018	JOB	037	015	Do you know how God controls the clouds, or makes his lightning flash from them?
018	JOB	037	016	Do you know how clouds float in the sky—the wonderful work of him who knows everything.
018	JOB	037	017	You know how your clothes drip with sweat when the south wind brings air that is hot and heavy.
018	JOB	037	018	Can you hammer out the sky so it becomes like a molten mirror, as he does?
018	JOB	037	019	So why don't you teach us what to tell God? We cannot make our case because we are in the dark!
018	JOB	037	020	Should God be told that I want to speak? Anyone who wanted to would be destroyed!
018	JOB	037	021	After all, we can't look at the sun when it blazes brightly in the sky, after the wind has cleared away the clouds.
018	JOB	037	022	Out of the north God comes shining like gold, surrounded in awesome majesty.
018	JOB	037	023	We cannot approach the Almighty for he is far beyond us in power and justice, and doing right.
018	JOB	037	024	He does not act like a tyrant—no wonder people are in awe of him, though he does not value those who think they're wise.”
018	JOB	038	001	Then the Lord answered Job out of the whirlwind,
018	JOB	038	002	“Who is this who questions my wisdom by talking so ignorantly?
018	JOB	038	003	Prepare yourself, be strong, for I am going to question you, and you must answer me.
018	JOB	038	004	Where were you when I laid the foundation of the earth? Tell me, if you have such knowledge!
018	JOB	038	005	Who decided its dimensions? Don't you know? Who stretched out a measuring line?
018	JOB	038	006	What do its foundations rest upon? Who laid its cornerstone,
018	JOB	038	007	when the stars of the morning sang together and all the angels shouted for joy.
018	JOB	038	008	Who laid down the boundaries of the sea when it was born?
018	JOB	038	009	Who clothed it with clouds, and wrapped it in a blanket of deep darkness?
018	JOB	038	010	I set its limits, marking its borders.
018	JOB	038	011	I said, ‘You may come here, but no farther. Here is where your proud waves stop.’
018	JOB	038	012	During your lifetime, have you ever ordered the morning to begin?
018	JOB	038	013	Have you ever told the dawn where to appear that it might take hold of the corners of the earth and shake the wicked out?
018	JOB	038	014	The earth is changed like clay under a seal; its features stand out like a crumpled garment.
018	JOB	038	015	The ‘light’ of the wicked is taken away from them; their acts of violence are stopped.
018	JOB	038	016	Have you entered the sources of the sea? Have you explored their hidden depths?
018	JOB	038	017	Have you been shown where the gates of death are? Have you seen the gates of utter darkness?
018	JOB	038	018	Do you know how far the earth extends? Tell me if you know all this!
018	JOB	038	019	In which direction does light live? Where does darkness dwell?
018	JOB	038	020	Can you take them home? Do you know the way to where they live?
018	JOB	038	021	Of course you know, because you were already born then! You've lived so long!
018	JOB	038	022	Have you been to where the snow is kept? Have you seen where the hail is held?
018	JOB	038	023	I have stored them up for the time of trouble, for the day of war and battle.
018	JOB	038	024	Do you know the way to where light comes from, or to where the east wind blows over the earth?
018	JOB	038	025	Who cuts a channel for the rain to flow? Who creates a path for the thunderbolt?
018	JOB	038	026	Who brings rain to an uninhabited land, to a desert where nobody lives,
018	JOB	038	027	to water a parched wasteland to make the green grass grow?
018	JOB	038	028	Does the rain have a father? Who was the father of the dewdrops?
018	JOB	038	029	Who was the mother of ice? Does the frost of the air have a mother?
018	JOB	038	030	Water turns into rock-hard ice; its surface freezes solid.
018	JOB	038	031	Can you tie together the stars of the Pleiades? Can you loosen the belt of the Orion constellation?
018	JOB	038	032	Can you guide the stars of Mazzaroth at the right time? Can you direct the Great Bear constellation and its other stars?
018	JOB	038	033	Do you know the laws of the heavens? Can you apply them to the earth?
018	JOB	038	034	Can you shout to the clouds and command them to pour rain down on you?
018	JOB	038	035	Can you send out bolts of lightning and direct them, so that they can answer you saying, ‘Here we are’?
018	JOB	038	036	Who has placed wisdom inside people? Who has given understanding to the mind?
018	JOB	038	037	Who is clever enough to count the clouds? Who can turn heaven's water jars on their sides
018	JOB	038	038	when the dust has baked into a solid mass?
018	JOB	038	039	Can you hunt prey for the lion? Can you feed the lion cubs
018	JOB	038	040	as they crouch down in their dens and lie in wait in the bushes?
018	JOB	038	041	Who provides food for the raven when its young cry out to God, weak from starvation?”
018	JOB	039	001	“Do you know when the wild goats give birth? Have you watched the birth-pains of the deer?
018	JOB	039	002	Do you know how many months they carry their young? Do you know the time when they give birth?
018	JOB	039	003	They crouch down in labor to deliver their offspring.
018	JOB	039	004	Their young grow strong in the open countryside; they leave and never return.
018	JOB	039	005	Who gave the wild donkey its freedom? Who set it free from its bonds?
018	JOB	039	006	I have given it the wilderness as its home, the salt plains as a place to live.
018	JOB	039	007	It despises the noise of the city; it doesn't need to listen to the shouts of a driver.
018	JOB	039	008	It hunts in the mountains for pastureland, searching for all kinds of green plants to eat.
018	JOB	039	009	Is the wild ox willing to serve you? Will it spend the night at your manger?
018	JOB	039	010	Can you tie a wild ox to a plow? Can you make it till your fields for you?
018	JOB	039	011	Because it's so powerful can you trust it? Can you depend on it to do your heavy work for you?
018	JOB	039	012	Are you sure it will gather your grain and bring it to your threshing floor?
018	JOB	039	013	The ostrich proudly flaps her wings, but they are nothing like the flight feathers of the stork.
018	JOB	039	014	The ostrich abandons her eggs on the ground, leaving them to be warmed in the dust.
018	JOB	039	015	She doesn't think that they can be crushed underfoot, trampled by a wild animal.
018	JOB	039	016	She is tough towards her young, acting as if they didn't belong to her. She doesn't care that all her work was for nothing.
018	JOB	039	017	For I, God, made her forget wisdom—she didn't get her share of intelligence.
018	JOB	039	018	But when she needs to, she can jump up and run, mocking a horse and its rider with her speed.
018	JOB	039	019	Did you give the horse its strength? Did you place a mane upon its neck?
018	JOB	039	020	Did you make it able to jump like a locust? Its loud snorting is terrifying!
018	JOB	039	021	It paws at the ground, rearing up with power as it charges into battle.
018	JOB	039	022	It laughs at fear; it is not frightened at all.
018	JOB	039	023	The quiver full of arrows rattles against it; the spear and the javelin flash in the sunlight.
018	JOB	039	024	Shaking with rage it gallops across the ground; it cannot remain still when the trumpet sounds.
018	JOB	039	025	Whenever the trumpet calls, it is ready; he senses the sound of battle from far away, he hears the commanders shouting.
018	JOB	039	026	Is it through your wisdom that the hawk soars, spreading its wings towards the south?
018	JOB	039	027	Do you command the eagle to fly high and make its nest in the summits of the mountains?
018	JOB	039	028	It lives among the cliffs, and roosts on a remote rocky crag.
018	JOB	039	029	From there it spies its prey from far away, fixing its gaze on its victim. Its chicks eagerly swallow blood.
018	JOB	039	030	Where the carcasses are, that's where birds of prey are found.”
018	JOB	040	001	God continued speaking to Job.
018	JOB	040	002	“Are you still going to fight with the Almighty and try to set him straight? Anyone who argues with God must give some answers.”
018	JOB	040	003	Job answered the Lord,
018	JOB	040	004	“Me—I am nothing at all. I have no answers. I put my hand in front of my mouth.
018	JOB	040	005	I have already said far too much and I won't say anything more.”
018	JOB	040	006	Then the Lord answered Job out of the whirlwind,
018	JOB	040	007	“Prepare yourself, be strong, for I am going to question you, and you must answer me.
018	JOB	040	008	Are you really going to say my decisions are wrong? Are you going to condemn me so you can be right?
018	JOB	040	009	Are you as powerful as I am? Does your voice thunder like mine?
018	JOB	040	010	Why don't you dress yourself with majesty and dignity, and clothe yourself with glory and splendor!
018	JOB	040	011	Let loose your fierce anger. Humble the proud with a glance.
018	JOB	040	012	Bring down the proud with your gaze; tread the wicked underfoot right where they are.
018	JOB	040	013	Bury them in the dust; lock them away in the grave.
018	JOB	040	014	Then I will also agree that your own strength can save you.
018	JOB	040	015	Consider Behemoth, a creature I made just like I made you. It eats grass like cattle.
018	JOB	040	016	Look at its powerful loins, the muscles of its belly.
018	JOB	040	017	It bends its tail like a cedar; its thigh sinews are strong.
018	JOB	040	018	Its bones are like bronze tubes; its limbs like iron rods.
018	JOB	040	019	It is the most important example of what God can do; only the one who made it can approach it with a sword.
018	JOB	040	020	The hills produce food for it, and all the wild animals play there.
018	JOB	040	021	It lies under the lotus; it hides in the reeds of the marsh.
018	JOB	040	022	The lotus covers it with shade; the willow trees of the valley surround it.
018	JOB	040	023	Even if the river is in flood, it is not concerned; it remains calm when the Jordan river surges against it.
018	JOB	040	024	No one can catch it while it is watching, or pierce its nose with a noose.”
018	JOB	041	001	“Can you pull out Leviathan with a hook? Can you tie its mouth shut?
018	JOB	041	002	Can you thread a rope through its nose? Can you pass a hook through its jaw?
018	JOB	041	003	Will it beg you to let it go? Or will it talk softly to you?
018	JOB	041	004	Will it make a contract with you? Will it agree to be your slave forever?
018	JOB	041	005	Will you play with it like a pet bird? Will you put it on a leash for your girls?
018	JOB	041	006	Will your trading partners decide on a price for him, and divide him up among the merchants?
018	JOB	041	007	Can you pierce his skin with many harpoons, its head with fishing spears?
018	JOB	041	008	If you were to grab hold of it, imagine the battle you would have! You wouldn't do that again!
018	JOB	041	009	Any hope to capture it is foolish. Anyone who tries is thrown to the ground.
018	JOB	041	010	Since no one has the courage to provoke Leviathan, who would dare to stand up against me?
018	JOB	041	011	Who has confronted me with any claim that I should repay? Everything under heaven belongs to me.
018	JOB	041	012	Let me tell you about Leviathan: its powerful legs and graceful proportions.
018	JOB	041	013	Who can remove its hide? Who can penetrate its double coat of armor?
018	JOB	041	014	Who can open its jaws? Its teeth are terrifying!
018	JOB	041	015	Its pride is its rows of scales, closed tightly together.
018	JOB	041	016	Its scales are so close together that no air can pass between them.
018	JOB	041	017	Each scale attaches to the next; they lock together and nothing can penetrate them.
018	JOB	041	018	When it sneezes light shines out. Its eyes are like the rising sun.
018	JOB	041	019	Flames pour from its mouth, sparks of fire shoot out.
018	JOB	041	020	Smoke comes from its nostrils, like steam from a kettle on a fire made of reeds.
018	JOB	041	021	Its breath sets fire to charcoal as flames shoot from its mouth.
018	JOB	041	022	Its neck is powerful, and all who face him shake with terror.
018	JOB	041	023	Its body is dense and solid, as if it is made from cast metal.
018	JOB	041	024	Its heart is rock-hard, like a millstone.
018	JOB	041	025	When it rises, even the powerful are terrified; they retreat as it thrashes about.
018	JOB	041	026	Swords just bounce off it, as do spears, darts, and javelins.
018	JOB	041	027	It brushes aside iron like straw, and bronze like rotten wood.
018	JOB	041	028	Arrows cannot make it run away; stones from slingshots are like pieces of stubble.
018	JOB	041	029	Clubs are also treated like stubble; it laughs at the sound made by flying spears.
018	JOB	041	030	Its underparts are covered with points as sharp as broken pots; when it drags itself through the mud it leaves marks like a threshing sledge.
018	JOB	041	031	It churns up the sea like water in a boiling pot, like a steaming bowl when ointment is mixed.
018	JOB	041	032	It leaves a glistening wake behind it as if the sea had white hair.
018	JOB	041	033	There is nothing on earth like it: a creature that has no fear.
018	JOB	041	034	It looks down on all other creatures. It is the proudest of all.”
018	JOB	042	001	Then Job answered the Lord.
018	JOB	042	002	“I know you can do anything. No one can prevent you doing what you want.
018	JOB	042	003	You asked, ‘Who is this who questions my wisdom by talking so ignorantly?’ I was speaking about things I didn't understand—things too wonderful for me to know.
018	JOB	042	004	You told me, ‘Listen to me, I am going to speak. I am going to question you, and you must answer me.’
018	JOB	042	005	Before, I had only heard about you, but now I've seen you for myself.
018	JOB	042	006	That is why I'm sorry for what I said, and I repent in dust and ashes.”
018	JOB	042	007	After the Lord had finished speaking to Job he said to Eliphaz the Temanite, “I am angry with you and your two friends because you have not told the truth about me, as my servant Job did.
018	JOB	042	008	So take seven bulls and seven rams and go to my servant Job, and you shall offer a burnt offering for yourselves. My servant Job will pray on your behalf and I will accept his prayer and not deal with you according to your foolishness, because you have not told the truth about me, as my servant Job did.”
018	JOB	042	009	So Eliphaz the Temanite, Bildad the Shuhite, and Zophar the Naamathite did what the Lord commanded them, and the Lord accepted Job's prayer.
018	JOB	042	010	The Lord restored Job's prosperity when he prayed for his friends, and gave him twice as much as he had before.
018	JOB	042	011	Then all his brothers and sisters and friends he'd previously known came and ate with him at his home. They showed him sympathy and comforted him because of all the trouble the Lord had caused him. Each one of them gave him money and a gold ring.
018	JOB	042	012	The Lord blessed the last part of Job's life more than the first part. Now he owned 14,000 sheep, 6,000 camels, 1,000 teams of oxen, and 1,000 female donkeys.
018	JOB	042	013	Job also had seven sons and three daughters.
018	JOB	042	014	Job called the first daughter Jemimah, the second Keziah, and the third Keren-Happuch.
018	JOB	042	015	Nowhere in all the land were there women as beautiful as Job's daughters, and Job gave them the same inheritance as their brothers.
018	JOB	042	016	Job lived 140 years after this, seeing his children, and grandchildren, in fact four generations.
018	JOB	042	017	Then Job died at an old age, having lived a very full life.
