044	ACT	027	001	When the time came for us to sail to Italy, Paul and some other prisoners were handed over to a centurion called Julius, of the Imperial Regiment.
044	ACT	027	002	We boarded a ship based in Adramyttium that was headed to the coastal ports of the province of Asia, and we set sail. Aristarchus, a Macedonian from Thessalonica, went with us.
044	ACT	027	003	The next day we had a brief stop at Sidon, and Julius was kind enough to let Paul go ashore and visit his friends so they could provide what he needed.
044	ACT	027	004	We set out from there and sailed to the leeward of Cyprus because the winds were against us.
044	ACT	027	005	Then we sailed directly across the open sea off the coast of Cilicia and Pamphylia, arriving at the port of Myra in Lycia.
044	ACT	027	006	There the centurion found a ship from Alexandria that would be sailing to Italy, and arranged for us to join it.
044	ACT	027	007	We sailed slowly for several days and eventually arrived off Cnidus. But since the winds wouldn't allow us to continue we sailed across to the lee of Crete, near to Salmone.
044	ACT	027	008	With some difficulty we made our way along the coast until we arrived at a place called Fair Havens, near to the town of Lasea.
044	ACT	027	009	We'd lost a lot of time, and the voyage was becoming dangerous because it was now after the Fast. Paul warned them,
044	ACT	027	010	“Men, I predict that this voyage will result in much hardship and loss—not just of the cargo and the ship, but also our very lives.”
044	ACT	027	011	But the centurion paid more attention to the advice of the ship's captain and its owner than to what Paul said.
044	ACT	027	012	Since the harbor was not large enough to over winter in, the majority were in favor of leaving and trying if possible to reach Phoenix and spend the winter there—a harbor in Crete that faces northwest and southwest.
044	ACT	027	013	When a moderate south wind began to blow, they thought they could do what they planned. They pulled up the anchor and sailed close inshore along the coast of Crete.
044	ACT	027	014	But it wasn't long before a hurricane-force wind called a “northeaster” blew from the land.
044	ACT	027	015	The ship was forced out to sea and could not face into the wind. So we had to give in and allow ourselves to be driven before the wind.
044	ACT	027	016	We were eventually able to run into the lee of a small island called Cauda, and managed with some difficulty to secure the ship's boat on board.
044	ACT	027	017	After hoisting it aboard, the sailors bound ropes around the hull to strengthen it. Then, worried that they would be wrecked on the Syrtis banks, they lowered the sea anchor and allowed the ship to be driven along.
044	ACT	027	018	The following day as we were violently thrown about by the storm, the crew started throwing the cargo overboard.
044	ACT	027	019	On the third day they grabbed the ship's gear and tossed it into the sea.
044	ACT	027	020	We hadn't seen the sun or the stars for many days as the storm beat down on us; so any hope of our being saved was lost.
044	ACT	027	021	Nobody had eaten anything for a long time. Then Paul stood before them and told them, “Men, you should have listened to me and not sailed from Crete. Then you could have avoided all this hardship and loss.
044	ACT	027	022	But now I advise you keep up your courage, because nobody is going to be lost, just the ship.
044	ACT	027	023	Last night an angel of my God and whom I serve, stood beside me.
044	ACT	027	024	‘Don't be afraid, Paul,’ he told me. ‘You have to stand trial before Caesar. See, God has graciously given to you everyone who is sailing with you.’
044	ACT	027	025	So men, have courage! I trust God, and I'm convinced things will happen just as I was told.
044	ACT	027	026	However, we will be wrecked on some island.”
044	ACT	027	027	At around midnight on the fourteenth night of the storm, still being blown over the Sea of Adria, the crew suspected they were getting close to land.
044	ACT	027	028	They checked the depth and found it was forty meters, and a little while later they checked again and it was thirty meters.
044	ACT	027	029	They were concerned that we might be wrecked on rocks, so they dropped four anchors from the stern, and prayed for daylight to come.
044	ACT	027	030	The crew tried to leave the ship, and had lowered the ship's boat into the water with the pretext that they were going to drop anchors from the ship's bow.
044	ACT	027	031	But Paul told the centurion and the soldiers, “Unless the crew stays with the ship, you will be lost.”
044	ACT	027	032	So the soldiers cut the ropes holding the ship's boat, and let it loose.
044	ACT	027	033	At daybreak Paul urged all of them to eat something. “It's been fourteen days now that you haven't eaten anything because you've been so worried and preoccupied,” he told them.
044	ACT	027	034	“Please do what I say and eat some food. This will help give you strength. For not even a hair from anyone's head is going to be lost!”
044	ACT	027	035	When he'd finished speaking he picked up a loaf of bread, and gave thanks to God for it in front of everyone. Then he broke the bread, and began to eat.
044	ACT	027	036	Everyone was encouraged and they ate too.
044	ACT	027	037	The total number of people on board was two hundred and seventy-six.
044	ACT	027	038	Once they'd had enough to eat, the crew made the ship lighter by throwing the supplies of wheat overboard.
044	ACT	027	039	When dawn came they didn't recognize the coastline, but they saw a bay that had a beach. They planned to try running the ship aground there.
044	ACT	027	040	So they cut the anchor ropes, leaving the anchors in the sea. At the same time they untied the ropes holding the rudders, raised the foresail to the wind, and made for the beach.
044	ACT	027	041	But they struck a sandbar and the ship grounded. The bow hit, and stuck so firmly it couldn't be moved, while the stern began to be broken apart by the pounding surf.
044	ACT	027	042	The soldiers planned to kill the prisoners so none of them could swim away and escape.
044	ACT	027	043	But the centurion, because he wanted to save Paul's life, prevented them from doing this, and ordered those who could swim to jump overboard first and make for land.
044	ACT	027	044	The rest grabbed hold of planks and other wreckage, so that everyone was able to reach land safely.
044	ACT	028	001	When we were safely ashore, we discovered that we were on the island of Malta.
044	ACT	028	002	The people there were very kind—they started a fire and called all of us over so we could warm up from the rain and the cold.
044	ACT	028	003	Paul collected a bundle of firewood and threw it on the fire. But a poisonous snake was driven out of the bundle because of the heat and bit him, fastening itself on his hand.
044	ACT	028	004	When the people there saw the snake hanging from his hand, they said to each other, “This man must be a murderer. Even though he escaped death from the sea, Justice won't let him live.”
044	ACT	028	005	However, Paul shook the snake off into the fire, and suffered no ill effects.
044	ACT	028	006	They were expecting him to swell up, or suddenly fall down dead. But after waiting a long time, they saw that nothing bad had happened to him so they changed their minds and decided he must be a god.
044	ACT	028	007	Nearby were lands that belonged to Publius, the chief official of the island. He welcomed us and looked after us for three days very hospitably.
044	ACT	028	008	Now Publius' father was sick, lying in bed suffering from fever and dysentery. Paul went in to see him, and prayed for him, placed his hands on him, and healed him.
044	ACT	028	009	After this happened, everyone else who was sick on the island came and was healed.
044	ACT	028	010	They presented us with many gifts, and when we had to sail they provided everything we needed for the voyage.
044	ACT	028	011	After a three month stay we set sail aboard a ship from Alexandria that had spent the winter at the island. It had a figurehead of the Heavenly Twins.
044	ACT	028	012	We stopped at Syracuse, and spent three days there.
044	ACT	028	013	From there we sailed on to Rhegium. The following day a south wind blew, and on the second day we arrived at the port of Puteoli,
044	ACT	028	014	where we discovered some believers. They asked us to stay with them for a week. So we came to Rome.
044	ACT	028	015	When some of the believers from Rome heard we had arrived they came to meet us at the Forum of Appius and the Three Taverns. When Paul saw them, he was thankful to God and much encouraged.
044	ACT	028	016	When we entered Rome, Paul was permitted to stay under house arrest with a soldier to guard him.
044	ACT	028	017	Three days later Paul invited the Jewish leaders there to come to see him. When they were all together he told them, “Brothers, even though I had not done anything wrong against the people or the customs of our forefathers, I was arrested in Jerusalem and handed over to the Roman authorities.
044	ACT	028	018	After they had interrogated me they wanted to release me because I had done nothing that warranted execution.
044	ACT	028	019	But the Jewish leaders opposed this, so I was forced to appeal to Caesar—not that I had any accusations against my own people.
044	ACT	028	020	That's why I asked to see you, and talk to you, because it is on account of the hope of Israel that I am chained up like this.”
044	ACT	028	021	“We have not received any letters from Judea about you, and none of our people have come here with reports against you or to speak badly of you,” they told him.
044	ACT	028	022	“But we do want to hear from you what you believe—especially regarding this sect, which we know is condemned everywhere.”
044	ACT	028	023	They made an appointment to meet with him. On that day many people came to where he was staying. From morning till night Paul was explaining to them, telling them about the kingdom of God. He tried to convince them about Jesus using the writings of the law of Moses and the prophets.
044	ACT	028	024	Some accepted what Paul said, but some refused to believe.
044	ACT	028	025	They couldn't agree among themselves, and they left after Paul told them this: “The Holy Spirit said it well through Isaiah the prophet to your forefathers,
044	ACT	028	026	‘Go to these people and tell them: Even though you hear, you won't ever understand, and even though you see, you won't ever comprehend.
044	ACT	028	027	For the minds of these people have become calloused and hard, their ears cannot hear, their eyes are shut, in case they might see with their eyes and hear with their ears, and understand with their minds, and might come back to me and I would heal them.’
044	ACT	028	028	Consequently you should know that this salvation that comes from God has been sent to the foreigners and they will listen.”
044	ACT	028	030	For two full years Paul stayed there in the house he rented, welcoming everyone who came to see him.
044	ACT	028	031	He spoke of the kingdom of God, and taught about the Lord Jesus Christ very boldly. No one prevented him.
