# BOOK	045	ROM	Romans	Romans
045	ROM	001	001	This letter comes from Paul, a servant of Jesus Christ. I was called to be an apostle by God. God appointed me to announce the good news
045	ROM	001	002	that he had previously promised through his prophets in the Holy Scriptures.
045	ROM	001	003	The good news is about his Son, whose human forefather was David,
045	ROM	001	004	but who was revealed as God's Son by his resurrection from the dead through the power of the Holy Spirit. He is Jesus Christ our Lord.
045	ROM	001	005	It was through him that I received the privilege of becoming an apostle to call all nations to obedient trust in him.
045	ROM	001	006	You are also included among those who were called to belong to Jesus Christ.
045	ROM	001	007	I'm writing to all of you in Rome who are loved by God, and called to be his special people. Grace and peace to you from God our Father and the Lord Jesus Christ!
045	ROM	001	008	Let me begin by saying that I thank my God through Jesus Christ for all of you, because the way in which you trust in God is spoken about all over the world.
045	ROM	001	009	I'm always praying for you, as God can confirm—the God I serve with the whole of my being as I share the good news about his Son.
045	ROM	001	010	In my prayers I'm always asking that I might eventually come and see you, if that's what God wants.
045	ROM	001	011	I really want to visit you and share with you a spiritual blessing to strengthen you.
045	ROM	001	012	In this way we can be encouraged together by each others' trust in God, both your trust and mine.
045	ROM	001	013	I want you to know, my brothers and sisters, I often planned to visit you, but I was kept from coming up till now. I want to see some good spiritual results among you just as I've seen among other people.
045	ROM	001	014	For I have an obligation to work for both the civilized and the uncivilized, both the educated and the uneducated.
045	ROM	001	015	That's why I'm really keen to come to Rome and share the good news with you.
045	ROM	001	016	I'm certainly not ashamed about the good news, for it's God's power to save everyone who trusts in him—to the Jewish people first, and then to everyone else as well.
045	ROM	001	017	For in the good news God is revealed as good and right, trustworthy from start to finish. As Scripture says, “Those who are right with God live by trusting him.”
045	ROM	001	018	God's hostility is revealed from heaven against those who are godless and who are not morally right, those who suppress the truth through the evil that they do.
045	ROM	001	019	What can be known about God is obvious, because he has made it very clear to them.
045	ROM	001	020	Ever since the creation of the world, the invisible aspects of God—his eternal power and divinity—are clearly visible in what he has made. Such people have no excuse,
045	ROM	001	021	because even though they knew God, they did not praise him or thank him, but instead their thinking about God turned into complete foolishness, and darkness filled their empty minds.
045	ROM	001	022	Even though they claimed to be wise, they became foolish.
045	ROM	001	023	They exchanged the glory of the immortal God for idols, images of mortal human beings, birds, animals, and reptiles.
045	ROM	001	024	So God abandoned them to the evil desires of their depraved minds, and they did shameful, degrading things to each other.
045	ROM	001	025	They exchanged God's truth for a lie, worshiping and serving creatures instead of the Creator, who deserves praise forever. Amen.
045	ROM	001	026	That's why God abandoned them to their evil desires. Their women exchanged natural sex for that which is unnatural,
045	ROM	001	027	and in the same way the men gave up sex with women and burned with lust for each other. Men did indecent things to each other, and as a result they suffered the inevitable consequences of their perversions.
045	ROM	001	028	Since they didn't consider it worthwhile to get to know God, he abandoned them to their worthless, distrustful way of thinking, doing things that should never be done.
045	ROM	001	029	They filled themselves with all that's wrong: evil, greed, hate, envy, murder, quarreling, deception, malice, and gossip.
045	ROM	001	030	They're back-stabbers and God-haters. They're arrogant, proud, and boastful. They devise new ways of sinning. They rebel against their parents.
045	ROM	001	031	They don't want to understand, they don't keep their promises, they don't show any kindness or compassion.
045	ROM	001	032	Even though they realize exactly what God requires, they do things that deserve death. Not only do they do such things themselves, they also support others in doing them.
045	ROM	002	001	So if you judge others you don't have any excuse, whoever you are! For in whatever way you condemn others, you're judging yourself, because you're doing the same things.
045	ROM	002	002	We know that God's judgment on those who do such things is based on truth.
045	ROM	002	003	When you judge them do you really think that you however can avoid God's judgment?
045	ROM	002	004	Or is it that you're treating his wonderful kindness, tolerance, and patience with contempt, not realizing that God in his kindness is trying to lead you to repent?
045	ROM	002	005	Due to your hard-hearted attitude and your refusal to repent, you're making things far worse for yourself on the day of retribution when God's judgment is demonstrated to be absolutely right.
045	ROM	002	006	God will make sure everyone receives what they deserve according to what they've done.
045	ROM	002	007	Those who have kept on trying to do what is good and right will receive glory and honor, immortality and eternal life.
045	ROM	002	008	But those who think only of themselves, rejecting the truth and deliberately choosing to do evil, will receive punishment and furious hostility.
045	ROM	002	009	Everyone who does evil will have trouble and suffering—the Jewish people first, and the foreigners too.
045	ROM	002	010	But everyone who does good will have glory, honor, and peace—the Jewish people first, and the foreigners too.
045	ROM	002	011	God has no favorites.
045	ROM	002	012	Those who sin even though they don't have the written law are still lost, while those who sin that do have the written law will be condemned by that law.
045	ROM	002	013	Just listening to what the law says doesn't make you right in God's sight. It's those who do what the law says who are made right.
045	ROM	002	014	The foreigners don't have the written law, but when they instinctively do what it says, they are following the law even without having the written law.
045	ROM	002	015	In this way they show how the law works that's written in their minds. As they think about what they're doing, their conscience either accuses them for doing wrong or defends them for doing what is good and right.
045	ROM	002	016	The good news I share is that a day is coming when God will judge, through Jesus Christ, everyone's secret thoughts.
045	ROM	002	017	What about you who call yourself a Jew? You rely on the written law and boast about having a special relationship to God.
045	ROM	002	018	You know what he wants; you do what's right because you've been taught from the law.
045	ROM	002	019	You're absolutely sure that you can guide the blind, and that you are a light to those in darkness.
045	ROM	002	020	You think you can set ignorant people straight, a teacher of “children,” because you know from the law all the truth there is to know.
045	ROM	002	021	So if you're busy teaching others, why don't you teach yourself? You tell people not to steal, but are you stealing?
045	ROM	002	022	You tell people not to commit adultery, but are you committing adultery? You tell people not to worship idols, but do you profane temples?
045	ROM	002	023	You boast about having the law, but don't you misrepresent God by breaking it?
045	ROM	002	024	As Scripture says, “God's character is defamed among the foreigners because of you.”
045	ROM	002	025	Being circumcised has value only if you do what the law says. But if you break the law, your circumcision is as worthless as those who are not circumcised at all.
045	ROM	002	026	If a man who is not circumcised keeps the law, he should be considered as being circumcised even though he's not.
045	ROM	002	027	The uncircumcised foreigners who keep the law will condemn you if you break the law, even though you have the written law and circumcision.
045	ROM	002	028	It's not what's on the outside that makes you a Jew; it's not the physical sign of circumcision.
045	ROM	002	029	What makes you a Jew is on the inside, a “circumcision of the heart” that doesn't follow the letter of the law but the Spirit. Someone like that is looking for praise from God, not from people.
045	ROM	003	001	So does a Jew have any advantage? Does circumcision have any benefits?
045	ROM	003	002	Yes, there are many benefits! First of all, God's message was entrusted to them.
045	ROM	003	003	What if some of them didn't trust in God? Does their lack of trust in God obliterate the trustworthiness of God?
045	ROM	003	004	Of course not! Even if everyone else is proved to be lying, God always tells the truth. As Scripture says, “What you say will be proved right, and you will win your case when you are judged.”
045	ROM	003	005	But if the fact that we're wrong helps to show that God is right, what should we conclude? That God is wrong to pronounce judgment on us? (I'm talking from a human perspective here.)
045	ROM	003	006	Of course not! How else could God judge the world?
045	ROM	003	007	Someone could say, “Why am I still condemned as a sinner if my lies make the truth of God and his glory more obvious in contrast?”
045	ROM	003	008	Is it a case of, “Let's sin to bring about good”? That's what some people have slanderously accused us of saying. They should be rightly condemned!
045	ROM	003	009	So then, are we Jews better than others? Definitely not! Remember that we've already argued that both Jews and foreigners are under the control of sin.
045	ROM	003	010	As Scripture says, “No one does what is right, not even one.
045	ROM	003	011	No one understands; no one seeks God.
045	ROM	003	012	Everyone has turned their backs on him; everyone does what is totally wrong. No one does what is good, not a single one.
045	ROM	003	013	Their throats are like an open grave; their tongues spread deceit; their lips ooze with the venom of snakes.
045	ROM	003	014	Their mouths are full of bitterness and curses,
045	ROM	003	015	and they are quick to cause pain and death.
045	ROM	003	016	Their way leads to disaster and misery;
045	ROM	003	017	they don't know how to live in peace.
045	ROM	003	018	They don't care about respecting God at all.”
045	ROM	003	019	It's clear that everything in the law applies to those who live under the law so that no one could have any excuses, and to make sure everyone in the whole world is answerable to God.
045	ROM	003	020	For no one is made right before God by doing what the law requires. The law only helps us recognize what sin really is.
045	ROM	003	021	But now God's character of goodness and right has been demonstrated. It has nothing to do with law-keeping, even though it was spoken of by the law and the prophets.
045	ROM	003	022	This character of God that is good and right comes to everyone who trusts in Jesus Christ, those who place their confidence in him. It doesn't matter who we are:
045	ROM	003	023	We have all sinned, and we fall far short of God's glorious ideal.
045	ROM	003	024	Yet through the free gift of his grace, God makes us right through Christ Jesus who sets us free.
045	ROM	003	025	God openly presented Jesus as the gift that brings peace to those trusting in him, the one who shed his blood. God did this to demonstrate he is truly good and right, for previously he would hold back and pass over sins,
045	ROM	003	026	but now at this present time God proves he is fair and does what is right, and that he makes right those who trust in Jesus.
045	ROM	003	027	So do we have anything to boast about then? Absolutely not, there's no place for that! And why's that? Is it because we follow the law of observing requirements? No, we follow the law of trusting in God.
045	ROM	003	028	We conclude that people are made right with God through their trust in him, and not from legal observance.
045	ROM	003	029	Is God only the God of the Jews? Isn't he the God of other people as well? Of course he is!
045	ROM	003	030	There is only one God, and he makes us morally right through our trust in him, whoever we are, Jew or foreigner.
045	ROM	003	031	Does that mean that by trusting in God we do away with the law? Of course not! In fact we affirm the importance of the law.
045	ROM	004	001	Let's take Abraham as an example. From a human viewpoint he is the father of our nation. Let's ask, “What was his experience?”
045	ROM	004	002	For if Abraham was set right by what he did, he would have had something to boast about—but not in God's eyes.
045	ROM	004	003	However, what does Scripture say? “Abraham trusted God, and so he was considered as being a good person who did right.”
045	ROM	004	004	Whoever works gets paid—it's not considered as a gift, but because they've earned their wages.
045	ROM	004	005	But God, who makes sinners right, considers them as right not because they've worked for it but because they trust in him. This is why
045	ROM	004	006	David speaks of the happiness of those whom God considers as right, and not because they worked for it:
045	ROM	004	007	“How happy are those whose wrongs are forgiven, and whose sins are covered.
045	ROM	004	008	How happy are those the Lord does not consider sinful.”
045	ROM	004	009	Now is this blessing just for the Jews, or is it for others too? We've just stated that Abraham was accepted as good and right because he trusted God.
045	ROM	004	010	But when did this happen? When Abraham was a Jew or before?
045	ROM	004	011	It was actually before he became a Jew by being circumcised, which was a confirmation of his trust in God to make him right. This happened before he was circumcised, so he is the father of everyone who trusts in God and are considered as right by God, even though they may not be circumcised Jews.
045	ROM	004	012	He is also the father of circumcised Jews not merely because they're circumcised, but because they follow the example of the trust in God our father Abraham had before he was circumcised.
045	ROM	004	013	God's promise to Abraham and his descendants that the world would belong to him was not based on his keeping of the law, but because he was made right through his trust in God.
045	ROM	004	014	For if the promised inheritance is based on keeping the law, then the issue of trusting God is not necessary, and the promise is pointless.
045	ROM	004	015	For the law results in punishment—but if there's no law then it cannot be broken.
045	ROM	004	016	So the promise is based on trusting God. It is provided as a free gift, guaranteed to all the children of Abraham—not merely to those who follow the law, but also to those who trust like Abraham, the father of us all.
045	ROM	004	017	As Scripture says, “I've made you the father of many nations.” For in the presence of God, Abraham trusted in the God who makes the dead alive and speaks into existence what didn't previously exist.
045	ROM	004	018	Against all hope Abraham in hope trusted God, so he could become the father of many peoples, just as God had promised him: “This is how many descendants you'll have!”
045	ROM	004	019	His trust in God didn't weaken even though he thought his body was practically dead (he was around a hundred years old), and knew that Sarah was too old to have children.
045	ROM	004	020	He held on to God's promise—he didn't doubt it. Instead his trust in God grew stronger, and he gave glory to God.
045	ROM	004	021	He was totally convinced that what God had promised he had the power to deliver.
045	ROM	004	022	That's why Abraham was considered right by God.
045	ROM	004	023	The words “Abraham was considered right” weren't just written down for his benefit.
045	ROM	004	024	They were for us too, those of us who will be considered as right, since we trust in God who raised our Lord Jesus from the dead.
045	ROM	004	025	Jesus was handed over to die because of our sins, and was raised to life to make us right.
045	ROM	005	001	Now that we have been made right by God by trusting in him, we have peace with him through our Lord Jesus Christ.
045	ROM	005	002	It's through Jesus, trusting in him, that we've gained access to this position of grace where we now stand, looking forward with happiness and confidence to sharing in God's glory.
045	ROM	005	003	Not only this—we also remain confident when problems come, because we know that experiencing problems develops spiritual stamina.
045	ROM	005	004	Spiritual stamina in turn develops a mature character, and this mature character results in confident hope.
045	ROM	005	005	Since we have this hope we're never disappointed, for God's love has been poured into us through the Holy Spirit he's given us.
045	ROM	005	006	When we were completely helpless, at just the right time Christ died for us ungodly people.
045	ROM	005	007	Who would die for anybody else, even someone who does what's right? (Though perhaps somebody would be brave enough to die for someone really good.)
045	ROM	005	008	But God demonstrates his love for us in that Christ died for us while we were still sinners.
045	ROM	005	009	Now that we are made right by his death, we can be absolutely sure we'll be saved by him from the coming judgment.
045	ROM	005	010	While we were his enemies, God made us his friends through the death of his Son, and so we can be absolutely sure that he will save us through the life of his Son.
045	ROM	005	011	In addition to this we now celebrate what God has done through our Lord Jesus Christ to reconcile us and make us his friends.
045	ROM	005	012	For through one man sin entered the world, and sin led to death. In this way death came to everyone, for everyone sinned.
045	ROM	005	013	Even before the law was given, sin was in the world, however it wasn't counted as sin because there was no law.
045	ROM	005	014	But death still ruled from Adam until Moses, even over those who didn't sin in the same way as Adam did. Now Adam prefigured the one to come.
045	ROM	005	015	But the gift of Jesus is not like the sin of Adam. Though many people died because of one man's sin, God's grace is so much greater and has been shared with so many through his gracious gift in the person of Jesus Christ.
045	ROM	005	016	The result of the gift is not like that of the sin. The result of Adam's sin was judgment and condemnation, but the gift sets us right with God, despite our many sins.
045	ROM	005	017	As a result of one man's sin, death ruled because of him. But God's grace is so much greater and his gift sets us right, for everyone who receives it will rule in life through the person of Jesus Christ!
045	ROM	005	018	Just as one sin brought condemnation on everyone, in the same way one right act gave everyone the opportunity to live right.
045	ROM	005	019	Just as through one man's disobedience many became sinners, in the same way through one man's obedience many are made right with God.
045	ROM	005	020	When the law was introduced, sin became even more obvious. But while sin became much more obvious, grace became even more obvious!
045	ROM	005	021	Just as sin ruled us and brought us death, now grace rules by making us right with God, bringing us eternal life through Jesus Christ our Lord.
045	ROM	006	001	So what's our response? Should we continue to sin so we can have even more grace?
045	ROM	006	002	Of course not! Since we're already dead to sin, how can we continue to live in sin?
045	ROM	006	003	Don't you know that all of us who were baptized into Christ Jesus were baptized into his death?
045	ROM	006	004	Through baptism we were buried with him in death so that just as Christ was raised from the dead through the glory of the Father we too can live a new life.
045	ROM	006	005	If we've become one with him in dying like he did, then we'll be raised like him too.
045	ROM	006	006	We know that our old self was crucified with him to dispose of the dead body of sin so that we wouldn't be enslaved by sin any longer.
045	ROM	006	007	Anyone who has died has been set free from sin.
045	ROM	006	008	Since we died with Christ, we have confidence that we will also live with him,
045	ROM	006	009	for we know that because Christ has been raised from the dead he won't ever die, because death has no longer any power over him.
045	ROM	006	010	In dying, he died to sin once and for all, but now he lives, and he lives for God!
045	ROM	006	011	In just the same way you should consider yourselves dead to sin, but alive to God through Christ Jesus.
045	ROM	006	012	Don't let sin have control over your mortal body, don't give in to its temptations,
045	ROM	006	013	and don't use any parts of your body as evil tools of sin. Instead dedicate yourselves to God as those who have been brought back from death to life, and use all parts of your body as tools to do something good for God.
045	ROM	006	014	Sin won't rule over you, because you're not under law but under grace.
045	ROM	006	015	So then, should we sin because we're not under law, but under grace? Of course not!
045	ROM	006	016	Don't you realize that if you make yourselves someone's slaves, obeying their orders, then you are slaves to the one you obey? If you are slaves of sin, the result is death; if you obey God, the result is you are made right with him.
045	ROM	006	017	Thank God that though you once were slaves to sin, you whole-heartedly chose to follow the truth about God that you learned.
045	ROM	006	018	Now that you've been freed from sin, you've become slaves of doing what is morally right.
045	ROM	006	019	I'm using this everyday example because your human thinking is limited. Just as you once enslaved yourselves to immorality, piling up sin upon sin, now you must enslave yourselves to what is pure and right.
045	ROM	006	020	When you were slaves to sin you were not required to do what's right.
045	ROM	006	021	But what were the results back then? Aren't you ashamed of the things you did? Such things that lead to death!
045	ROM	006	022	But now that you're set free from sin, and have become God's slaves, the results will be a pure life—and in the end, eternal life.
045	ROM	006	023	The wage sin pays is death, but God's free gift is eternal life through Christ Jesus our Lord.
045	ROM	007	001	Brothers and sisters (I'm speaking here to people who know the law), don't you see that the law has authority over someone only while they're alive?
045	ROM	007	002	For example, a married woman is bound by the law to her husband while he's alive, but if he dies, she's released from this legal obligation to him.
045	ROM	007	003	So if she lives with another man while her husband is alive, she would be committing adultery. However, if her husband dies and then she marries another man, she wouldn't be guilty of adultery.
045	ROM	007	004	In the same way, my friends, you've become dead to the law through the body of Christ, and so now you belong to someone else—Christ, who was raised from the dead so that we could live productive lives for God.
045	ROM	007	005	While we were controlled by old nature, our sinful desires (as revealed by the law) were at work within us and resulted in death.
045	ROM	007	006	But now we've been set free from the law, and have died to what kept us in chains, so that we can serve in the newness of the spirit and not the old letter of the law.
045	ROM	007	007	So what do we conclude? That the law is sin? Of course not! I wouldn't have known what sin was unless the law defined it. I wouldn't have realized that wanting to have other people's things for myself was wrong without the law that says, “Don't desire for yourself what belongs to someone else.”
045	ROM	007	008	But through this commandment sin found a way to stir up in me all kinds of selfish desires—for without law, sin is dead.
045	ROM	007	009	I used to live without realizing what the law really meant, but when I understood the implications of that commandment, then sin came back to life, and I died.
045	ROM	007	010	I discovered that the very commandment that was meant to bring life brought death instead,
045	ROM	007	011	because sin found a way through the commandment to deceive me, and used the commandment to kill me!
045	ROM	007	012	However, the law is holy, and the commandment is holy, right, and good.
045	ROM	007	013	Now would something that is good kill me? Of course not! But sin shows itself to be sin by using good to cause my death. So by means of the commandment, it's revealed how evil sin really is.
045	ROM	007	014	We realize that the law is spiritual; but I'm all-too-human, a slave to sin.
045	ROM	007	015	I really don't understand what I'm doing. I do the things I don't want to do, and what I hate doing, that's what I do!
045	ROM	007	016	But if I'm saying that I do what I don't want to, this shows that I admit the law is good and right.
045	ROM	007	017	So it's no longer me who does this, but sin living in me—
045	ROM	007	018	for I know that there's nothing good in me as far as my sinful human nature is concerned. Even though I want to do good, I'm just not able to do it.
045	ROM	007	019	The good I want to do, I don't do; while the evil I don't want to do, that's what I end up doing!
045	ROM	007	020	However, if I'm doing what I don't want to, then it's no longer me doing it, but sin living in me.
045	ROM	007	021	This is the principle I've discovered: if I want to do what's good, evil is always there too.
045	ROM	007	022	My inner self is delighted with God's law,
045	ROM	007	023	but I see a different law at work within me that is at war with the law my mind has decided to follow, making me a prisoner of the law of sin that is within me.
045	ROM	007	024	I'm totally miserable! Who will rescue me from this body that's causing my death? Thank God—for he does this through Jesus Christ our Lord!
045	ROM	007	025	Here's the situation: while I myself choose with my mind to obey God's law, my human nature obeys the law of sin.
045	ROM	008	001	So there is now no condemnation for those who are in Christ Jesus.
045	ROM	008	002	The law of the Spirit of life in Christ Jesus has set me free from the law of sin and death.
045	ROM	008	003	What the law couldn't do because it was powerless due to our sinful nature, God was able to do! By sending his own Son in human form, God dealt with the whole problem of sin and destroyed sin's power in our sinful human nature.
